{
  "raw": {
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41399": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral in Structural studies of mitochondrial DNA replication",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral in Structural studies of mitochondrial DNA replication For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral in Structural studies of mitochondrial DNA replication Reference number PAR 2026/677 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 360 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets This postdoctoral position is part of a research project funded by a Wallenberg Scholar grant. The postdoctoral researcher will work in an interdisciplinary team with expertise in molecular biology, biochemistry and structural biology. The project aims to understand the molecular mechanisms that govern mitochondrial DNA replication and gene expression, and how defects in these processes contribute to human disease. Subject area Structural studies of mitochondrial DNA replication Subject area description Mitochondrial DNA replication is essential for maintaining mtDNA integrity and stability and thereby for normal mitochondrial function and cellular energy production. Defects in the replication process can lead to mtDNA depletion or deletions and cause mitochondrial disease. Our research aims to understand the molecular mechanisms that govern replication of human mtDNA. We combine structural biology, in vitro biochemistry and cell biology to investigate how the mitochondrial DNA replication machinery is organized and regulated. A particular focus is on structural and functional studies of the protein complexes involved in mtDNA replication and on understanding how disease-associated mutations affect these processes. Job assignments In this project, In this project, the postdoctoral researcher will carry out structural and functional studies of protein and protein-DNA complexes involved in mitochondrial DNA replication. A central aim will be to determine structures of replication complexes using single-particle cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM) and use these structures to understand how the proteins interact and regulate mtDNA replication at the molecular level. The work will include preparation and biochemical characterization of protein complexes, cryo-EM sample preparation and grid optimization, as well as processing and structural analysis of cryo-EM data. Structural findings will be combined with in vitro biochemical analyses to test mechanistic models and investigate how disease-associated mutations affect the function of the replication machinery. The postdoctoral researcher is expected to drive the project independently while working closely with other members of the research group and our collaborators. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria We are seeking a highly motivated researcher with a doctoral degree in structural biology, biochemistry, molecular biology or a related field. Documented experience in structural studies of proteins or macromolecular complexes using cryo-electron microscopy is required. Experience in protein purification and protein biochemistry is also required. Experience in single-particle cryo-EM, including sample preparation, grid optimization, data processing and structural analysis, is particularly meritorious. Experience in structural studies of protein-DNA complexes is a strong advantage. Knowledge of mitochondrial systems and experience in cloning, mutagenesis, protein expression, cell culture and in vitro biochemical analyses are considered merits. Great emphasis will be placed on scientific skills and the ability to independently plan, carry out and analyse research. The work involves close collaboration both within and outside the research group, making good teamwork and communication skills important. The candidate is expected to have strong scientific curiosity and the ability to work both independently and as part of an interdisciplinary team. Excellent spoken and written English is required, as the research group operates in an international environment. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Contact information Please contact: Maria Falkenberg, Professor, e-mail: maria.falkenberg@medkem.gu.se. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: - A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying - A CV including a list of publications - Contact details of two references - Proof of completed PhD Applications must be received by: 2026-11-15. Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41529": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in in physical oceanography",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in in physical oceanography For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in in physical oceanography Reference number PAR 2026/691 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Marine Sciences ( http://marine.gu.se ) is Sweden's most complete marine research and education environment and is one of the few such organisations in Europe. The Department of Marine Sciences, established in 2015, brings together expertise in physical oceanography, marine chemistry, marine biology, marine geology, and marine cultural conservation. The department thus has excellent conditions for meeting future challenges in marine research and education. The Department of Marine Sciences has permanent staff in three locations: in Gothenburg and at the research stations Kristineberg and Tjärnö in Bohuslän. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Subject area description Investigating fine-scale ocean and atmosphere dynamics in the Agulhas Current System. The PhD focus is on quantifying and understanding vertical transport/ventilation of properties (heat, nutrients, carbon, particles, etc) in the ocean. Physics-climate interactions in the oceans is one of the primary sources of uncertainty in global climate understanding and its future prediction. These uncertainties are particularly prevalent in the subtropical gateways that link the global overturning circulation. One such region is the Cape Basin and Agulhas Current System, where the scarcity of observations means we know remarkably little about the interaction of the ocean circulation and mixing processes occurring between the millimeter to 100s of kilometer scale and their links to the larger climate system. We seek a PhD position in Physical Oceanography to address knowledge gaps related to fine-scale ocean features/variability as a means to understand vertical mixing & transport, ventilation pathways and water mass transformation in the eddy-rich ocean south of Africa. These processes may also be related to heat and carbon fluxes across the air-sea interface, and between the upper ocean and its deep interior. The PhD will use a combination of existing field data (primarily from autonomous platforms and research ships), new glider missions, and existing high-resolution model simulations. Auxiliary reanalysis data and satellite-based products, including SWOT and PACE satellite data, are also likely to be used. The PhD will contribute to the ERC Synergy project ‘WHIRLS’ (The impact of ocean fine scale whirls on climate and ecosystems). This project has a focus area spanning the Agulhas Current System and Cape Basin south of Africa. See www.whirls.eu and https://www.gu.se/en/research/whirls-the-impacts-of-ocean-fine-scale-whirls-on-climate-and-ecosystems . There is the opportunity to participate in field experiments, which includes research voyages and field experiment execution. There will also be an expectation to learn how ocean autonomous platforms function and are piloted (e.g., underwater gliders and uncrewed surface vehicles). Doctoral studies comprise four years of full-time study and lead to a doctoral degree. The education should include courses of 45 higher education credits, distributed over the study period and planned together with the supervisor and the examiner. Duties As a PhD candidate at the Department of Marine Sciences, University of Gothenburg, the candidate will be expected to fulfill responsibilities that include: Produce a doctoral thesis, including peer-reviewed publications on the research topic Present findings at appropriate scientific conferences or forums Participate in research cruises that may be of weeks to months duration to collect observations and/or deploy equipment. Participate and contribute to the activities of the oceanography subject and colleagues at the Department of Marine Sciences. Assist with remote piloting of autonomous platforms in the field. Expected skills sharing between candidate and research group, and vice versa. Assisting in teaching activities at times in Oceanography or Marine Sciences. Expected skills sharing between candidate and research group, and vice versa. Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral education are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. To meet the specific entry requirements for third-cycle studies, applicants must: have a master’s degree in a relevant* subject area in natural sciences have completed studies for at least 60 higher education credits at a second-cycle level in relevant subject areas in the natural sciences, or have completed a corresponding programme of relevance to the planned third-cycle programme, in Sweden or in another country, or have equivalent qualifications. *Relevant subject for the planned third-cycle education is oceanography, meteorology, physics, maths, or a closely related field. Assessment criteria We are looking for a highly motivated and independent person. The candidate will work with a strongly supportive team based at the university, namely the Polar Gliders Research Group, that includes other students, Postdocs and researchers in the field ( polargliders.com ). There will also be mobility (research secondments) and collaboration with other WHIRLS PIs in GEOMAR - Germany, ENS Paris - France and the University of Cape Town - South Africa. The applicant is expected to have a willingness to work in a team, to communicate with peers,  good abilities to handle data and an ambition to learn new skills. The following are considered important: Experience with scientific programming skills in Python or similar. Willingness to work in a team, to communicate with peers, and an ambition to learn new skills. Demonstrated verbal and written communications skills in English and ability to articulate your work. Show independence, initiative and critical thinking. The following is considered advantageous: Demonstrate strong analytical skills Show a willingness in going to sea to participate in research cruises. Background/education in one or a combination of oceanography, meteorology, physics and/or maths. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at The Department of Marine Sciences require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, The Department of Marine Sciences has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information Prof Sebastiaan Swart, sebastiaan.swart@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 26-10-22 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41722": {
      "title": "Researcher in Geochemistry",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher in Geochemistry For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher in Geochemistry Reference number PAR 2026/775 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Earth Sciences conducts research and education in earth sciences and geography. Research is organized around three principal themes: climate and climate change, earth surface processes, and the geosphere. Within the Geosphere research area, we study the Earth’s geological processes and natural resources, ranging from bedrock and minerals to groundwater and geothermal energy. Research includes geology, geochemistry, mineralogy, hydrogeology, and the application of geochemical and geochronological methods to understand crustal evolution and mineral resource systems. For more information about the Department see here. The Department of Earth Sciences is seeking a researcher for a fixed-term position focusing on the thermal evolution of critical raw material mineral systems in the Bergslagen ore province, Sweden, using integrated geochronological and thermochronological approaches. Subject area Geochemistry, with a focus on geochronology, thermochronology and the thermal evolution of critical raw material mineral systems. Subject description Critical raw material deposits may record complex post-mineralization thermal histories that can modify mineral assemblages, redistribute elements and influence the preservation of mineral systems. Geochronological and thermochronological methods provide complementary constraints on the timing and temperature of these processes and can therefore improve understanding of the long-term evolution of mineral deposits. The project investigates the thermal evolution of critical raw material mineral systems in the Bergslagen ore province, Sweden. The research integrates in-situ Rb–Sr geochronology with multi-mineral (U–Th)/He thermochronology and complementary mineral characterization to constrain post-mineralization thermal events from deposit to regional scale. The purpose of the position is to complete and integrate geochronological and thermochronological datasets, interpret the thermal evolution of the investigated mineral systems, and contribute to the completion and submission of scientific manuscripts arising from the project. Duties The researcher will work with existing and newly generated geochronological and thermochronological data from critical raw material mineral systems in the Bergslagen ore province. The main duties include: processing, evaluating and interpreting in-situ Rb–Sr geochronological and multi-mineral (U–Th)/He thermochronological data; integrating geochronological, thermochronological and mineral-characterization data to constrain post-mineralization thermal events and the thermal evolution of the investigated mineral systems; conducting complementary LA-ICP-MS/MS and SEM-based analytical work where required to complete the existing datasets; contributing substantially to the interpretation of results and to the writing, submission and revision of scientific manuscripts for peer review; and collaborating with researchers at the University of Gothenburg and with national and international academic and mineral-exploration partners. The duties may be adjusted within the overall scientific scope of the project depending on data availability and manuscript needs. Qualifications Requirements The applicant must have: completed a doctoral degree, or have a corresponding foreign degree deemed comparable to a Swedish doctoral degree, in geology, geochemistry, Earth sciences, or another subject relevant to the position; documented research experience in geochronology and thermochronology; documented experience in Rb–Sr geochronology and (U–Th)/He thermochronology; experience in processing, evaluating and interpreting geochronological and thermochronological data; experience with LA-ICP-MS or LA-ICP-MS/MS and SEM-based mineral characterization; documented experience in scientific writing and publication in international peer-reviewed journals; and very good ability to communicate orally and in writing in English. Meritorious qualifications The following qualifications are considered advantageous: experience concerning the thermal evolution of mineral deposits or mineral systems; experience integrating high- and low-temperature geochronological and thermochronological methods; research experience related to critical raw materials and ore deposits; experience developing or implementing analytical workflows for geochronological applications; a strong publication record in geochemistry, geochronology, thermochronology, mineral systems, or related fields; and experience working in international and interdisciplinary research collaborations, including collaboration with geological surveys and the mineral-exploration or mining Assessment criteria Selection will be based on an overall assessment of the applicant’s scientific competence, methodological experience, publication record, technical skills, and suitability for the specific duties of the position. Particular weight will be given to documented experience of: applying and interpreting Rb–Sr geochronology; applying and interpreting multi-mineral (U–Th)/He thermochronology; integrating geochronological and thermochronological datasets to reconstruct the thermal evolution of mineral systems; using LA-ICP-MS/MS and SEM-based analytical approaches in geoscientific research; and independently completing scientific analyses and manuscripts within a limited project Personal qualities The position requires a researcher who can work independently, take responsibility for a clearly defined research task, and deliver results within a short employment period. The applicant is expected to: have strong analytical and problem-solving skills; work carefully, systematically and with a high degree of scientific rigor; take initiative and independently drive research tasks forward; communicate analytical approaches, interpretations and results clearly; collaborate effectively with researchers and external partners from different disciplinary and professional backgrounds; respond constructively to scientific feedback; and meet agreed deadlines for analyses, figures, documentation and manuscript preparation. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability and on the applicant’s documented ability to carry out the stated duties efficiently and to a high scientific standard. Employment The employment is a fixed-term position as researcher for four months. Extent: 100%, full-time Location: Department of Earth Sciences, University of Gothenburg Employment period: 1 November 2026–28 February 2027 Start date: 1 November 2026, or as agreed The University applies individual salary setting. Selection process The selection process may include an interview, reference checks, and a short presentation or discussion of the applicant’s previous research and proposed approach to the project analyses. Contact information For further information about the scientific content of the position, please contact: Prof. Robert Björk Department of Earth Sciences, University of Gothenburg Email: robert.bjork@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available on the University’s website for applicants. Application Applications must be submitted via the University of Gothenburg’s digital recruitment system. The application must include: a cover letter describing the applicant’s relevant scientific background and explaining how the applicant’s qualifications match the duties of the position; a curriculum vitae, including the date of the doctoral degree and a complete list of publications; a copy of the doctoral degree Documents that are not written in English, Swedish, Norwegian, or Danish must be accompanied by a translation into English or Swedish. The applicant is responsible for ensuring that the application is complete and has been received by the University no later than the application deadline. Only applications submitted via the recruitment system will be considered. The application must be received no later than 29 October 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41659": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/772 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. We are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic researcher with a background in single-cell genomics and spatial transcriptomics to join the lab of Dr. Joan Camuñas. The lab of Dr. Joan Camuñas stands at the interface of genomics, biophysics and precision medicine. Our mission is to develop experimental and computational methods to identify molecular mechanisms leading to dysfunctional cellular states in human disease ( www.camunaslab.org ). The project will require significant interdisciplinary work in the areas of spatial biology, image analysis, single-cell biology and genomics. The research group is embedded in the Wallenberg Center for Molecular and Translational Medicine (WCMTM) and SciLifeLab at the University of Gothenburg and is tightly connected to a broad network of international collaborators. Duties The candidate will contribute to improving the computational aspects of Slice-seq, a new method to study human pancreatic tissue slices using high-resolution calcium imaging in combination with spatial transcriptomics. The researcher will develop methods to improve the analysis of spatial transcriptomics data and the integration of imaging modalities with transcriptomics. The researcher will also contribute to the computational analysis of cell-free RNA datasets in diabetes, including gene discovery and cell-type deconvolution. Although the focus is on the computational aspects of the project, the candidate will also be expected to contribute to experimental genomics workflows, including sample processing, nucleic acid extraction, library preparation, and the operation of liquid-handling instrumentation in the lab. Develop and perform computational analyses of spatial transcriptomics data. Computationally integrate spatial transcriptomics with imaging data, including high-resolution calcium imaging. Analyze single-cell and single-nuclei RNA-seq datasets. Integrate data across genomic modalities, including spatial transcriptomics and single-nuclei RNA-seq. Analyze cell-free RNA data, including cell-type deconvolution and gene discovery. Develop and maintain reproducible computational workflows for genomic data analysis. Perform single-cell protocols using high-throughput liquid-handling instrumentation. Perform RNA extraction and NGS library preparation. Lead the project, present results in group meetings and conferences, and write research papers based on scientific discoveries. Help supervise and mentor junior team members. Qualifications The ideal candidates should have the following qualifications: PhD in a relevant research area (e.g. Bioinformatics, Molecular Biology, Mathematics). Highly motivated, proactive and diligent. Ability to work independently and in a team. Experience analyzing spatial transcriptomics and single-cell genomics datasets. Experience in computational integration of spatial transcriptomics with imaging data or quantitative image analysis. Experience programming in Python and Bash and working in HPC environments using workflow managers such as Snakemake or Nextflow. Experience in the analysis of pancreatic islet or pancreas single-cell genomic datasets is an advantage. Experience in cell-free RNA analysis, gene discovery or cell-type deconvolution is an advantage. Experience in experimental genomics methods, including RNA extraction and library preparation, is advantageous. Previous experience with liquid-handling instrumentation is advantageous. Good skills in both written and spoken English are required. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, 8 months, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Dr. Joan Camuñas Soler, joan.camunas@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying A CV including a list of publications Proof of completed PhD Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026-10-28 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41713": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in Physical Oceanography",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral position in Physical Oceanography For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral position in Physical Oceanography Reference number PAR 2026/776 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Marine Sciences, University of Gothenburg ( https://www.gu.se/en/marinavetenskaper ) combines extensive scientific competence with unique infrastructure to provide the most complete environment for marine science and education in Sweden. It is one of only a few such organisations in Europe. By integrating advanced research infrastructure with a strong link to societal needs and challenges, the Department contributes with new solutions to global ocean challenges. The Department of Marine Sciences conducts research, education, and innovation related to the science of the ocean and its resources. Its activities are based in Gothenburg, at two research stations (Kristineberg Marine Research Station, and Tjärnö Marine Laboratory), and on the research vessel Skagerak. The Department has an interdisciplinary scope that encompasses marine biology, marine chemistry, marine geology, preservation of marine cultural heritage, and oceanography, working in all marine habitats from shallow bays and coastal seas to the open oceans. Our scientists and students often play leading roles in international collaborative research, including expeditions to Polar regions and the great seas of the world, as well as in our newly built laboratories with advanced equipment and complex analytical techniques. The Department also offers a wide range of undergraduate courses, a Bachelor’s degree, three Master's degrees, and a Ph.D. programme, all within marine sciences. Subject area Oceanography Subject area description Combining satellite and in-situ observations to observe fine-scale ocean dynamics and related heat transport across the Southern Ocean. The ocean has absorbed more than 90% of the excess heat generated by human activities, and the Southern Ocean accounts for most of this uptake. Yet climate models disagree on ocean heat uptake by up to 40%, partly because they cannot resolve the vertical heat transport induced by fine-scale (1–100 km) ocean fronts, eddies, and filaments. These motions are strongest in the energetic Antarctic Circumpolar Current, where meso- to submesoscale vertical velocities can reach order 100 m per day and carry heat between the mixed layer and the ocean interior. Until recently, conventional altimetry (effective resolution of roughly 150 km) could not observe these scales, so our understanding of fine-scale vertical transport has been limited to a handful of regional and ‘snapshot’ in situ process studies. New wide-swath satellite altimetry now maps sea surface height in two dimensions at roughly an order of magnitude finer resolution than previous altimeters, resolving ocean structures down to scales of about 15 km. Recent work applying surface quasi-geostrophic theory to these observations and to km-scale coupled simulations shows that much of the variance in vertical velocity and vertical heat flux below the mixed layer can be reconstructed from surface height, and that vertical velocities and heat fluxes derived from the new observations are about twice as energetic as those from conventional altimetry. Yet these results come mostly from regional case studies, and several key questions remain open related to the magnitude, spatial structure, and seasonal and inter-annual variability of fine-scale vertical heat transport across the Southern Ocean. Furthermore, satellite-based reconstructions across different dynamical regimes and seasons need to be validated against direct observations. The time is right to move from regional case studies to a circumpolar view, combining satellite observations with high-resolution in-situ data from gliders, uncrewed surface vehicles, and ship-based platforms. We are offering a three-year postdoctoral position in physical oceanography to address key research aspect of the ERC Starting Grant SOFIA (Southern Ocean fine-scale ocean dynamics and storm impacts on air-sea heat exchange). Our goal is to understand and quantify the processes that move heat between the atmosphere and the ocean interior. More information can be found here: https://marcelduplessis.github.io/sofia/ The goal of the postdoc will be combining novel satellite data together with high resolution ocean data (e.g., gliders, USVs & ship-based observations) to investigate the fine-scale vertical velocity and vertical heat transport across the Southern Ocean. The work will include: Producing circumpolar maps of satellite-observed fine-scale vertical heat transport, alongside fine-scale diagnostics such as strain and vorticity, and comparing these with conventional gridded altimetry products. Validating these maps against in-situ observations, including existing Southern Ocean glider missions and new to-be-collected datasets, and quantifying uncertainties arising from theoretical assumptions and from satellite space-time sampling. Explore regional, seasonal and inter-annual variability of fine-scale vertical heat transport and its links to storm activity, ocean warming trends and climate modes. The work will also support the upcoming field campaign “Pulse of the Weddell Sea”, set to take place in late 2027. There will be an opportunity to participate and contribute to this planned Southern Ocean expedition and to learn how autonomous platforms (e.g. Seagliders, Wave Gliders, and X-Spar buoys) are piloted. The postdoc will be supervised by Dr Marcel du Plessis and Prof Sebastiaan Swart. They will be embedded within the Polar Gliders Research Group (polargliders.com), working alongside other members of the team, such as students, postdocs and researchers working on fine-scale dynamics and air-sea interaction. There will also be mobility and collaboration with international partners in the satellite, air-sea flux, and/or Southern Ocean communities, for example in the EU SYNCHRONY project ( https://synchrony-project.eu/ ). Duties As a Postdoctoral Fellow within the Polar Gliders Research Group at the Department of Marine Sciences, University of Gothenburg, the candidate will be expected to fulfil responsibilities that include: Scientific Lead the satellite-related work package of SOFIA and produce peer-reviewed publications of sufficient quantity and quality. Reconstruct, validate and analyze fine-scale vertical velocities and vertical heat transport from remote sensing products and in-situ observations. Work closely with the other Polar Gliders team members to integrate the satellite, model and in-situ components of our work. Show independence, initiative and critical thinking, and contribute to the preparation of research proposals for funding on related topics. Technical Process, quality-control and manage large datasets, making derived products openly available following FAIR principles. Provide satellite-derived fine-scale ocean diagnostics to support the planning and real-time piloting of autonomous platforms during Southern Ocean field campaign. Participate in research expeditions that may be of weeks to months in duration to deploy and pilot platforms, if circumstances allow. Community and outreach Present findings at appropriate international scientific conferences and workshops. We expect active participation to the activities of the Polar Glider team and broader oceanography cohort at the Department of Marine Sciences. Skills sharing between candidate and research group, and vice versa; there may be possibilities to co-supervise MSc and PhD students. Assist in teaching activities at times in Oceanography or Marine Sciences. Communication of science and results to both the scientific and public spheres. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. To be eligible you also need a PhD in physical oceanography, meteorology, geophysical fluid dynamics, applied mathematics/physics, remote sensing, or a closely related subject. Assessment criteria We are looking for a highly motivated and independent person with a strong background in physical oceanography and an interest in using new satellite observations to understand fine-scale ocean dynamics and their role in climate. The candidate will work within a strongly supportive team at the university. The following are considered essential: A PhD in physical oceanography or a closely related field, with a solid understanding of ocean dynamics and geophysical fluid dynamics. Experience analyzing large datasets (e.g., satellite, observational or numerical model output). Strong scientific programming skills in Python or similar. A track record of peer-reviewed publications commensurate with career stage and demonstrated verbal and written communication skills in English. Any of the following experience is considered advantageous: Experience with satellite remote sensing data, or with wide-swath or along-track altimetry processing and mapping. Experience with in-situ observations such as gliders, Argo floats, moorings or ship-based measurements, and with comparing observations to models or satellite products. Experience with high-performance computing environments. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, 3 years. Extent: Full time, 100%. Location: Department of Marine Sciences, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden. First day of employment: Upon agreement (approximately winter/spring 2027). Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact: Dr Marcel du Plessis – Associate Senior Lecturer, marcel.du.plessis@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact: Helen Kollind – Human resources officer, helen.kollind@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. All documents in the application should be submitted in English. The application should contain: A cover letter (maximum 1 page) outlining your motivation for the position and your relevance to the position description. A CV (maximum 2 pages), including a list of publications. A copy of your doctoral degree certificate (or a statement from your institution confirming the expected date of the PhD defense). Contact details of two referees. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-29 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41313": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Linguistics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Linguistics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Linguistics Reference number PAR 2026/547 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. At the Department of Philosophy, Linguistics, and Theory of Science (FLoV) within the Faculty of Humanities, we have around 120 employees and a teaching volume that includes around 350 full-year students. We are engaged in numerous large international and interdisciplinary research projects in areas such as theoretical and practical philosophy, logic, linguistics, computational linguistics, language technology, and theory of science. Our research has many applications and collaborations with other scientific fields, including biology, computer science, economics, history of ideas, law, medicine, psychology, religion, political science, and engineering. We educate philosophers, logicians, linguists, language technologists, theorists of science, historians of philosophy, and teachers of philosophy. The department also conducts doctoral education and development work within its various academic domains. If you are interested in working in an international, dynamic and collaborative environment, FLoV offers an inspiring setting for research and education. Read more about us here. As an Assistant Professor at the University of Gothenburg, research of at least 50% and professional development are part of the position. Subject area Linguistics. Link to subject page here. Subject area description Linguistic interaction is the overarching research profile for linguistics at FLoV. The profile covers the study of language as a structured system, a social-cognitive capacity, and a situated form of action. It therefore includes both formal and/or computational modelling of the systems of conventions that constitute individual languages and studies of the social practices and psychosocial mechanisms that enable language use. Research in the subject includes psycholinguistic, sociolinguistic, and typological perspectives. In psycholinguistics, language is studied in relation to perception, language processing, language acquisition, and production. In sociolinguistics and linguistic typology, linguistic variation and diversity are investigated within and across languages, language families, and social groups. The subject also engages with broader philosophical and societal questions concerning meaning, interpretation, normativity, categorisation, and the conditions for linguistic understanding. Such questions are particularly significant in contexts where language is used and circulates through institutional, technological, and public settings, thereby shaping how knowledge, action, and shared understanding are organised. In this way, the social and societal conditions of language also become central, including how communicative practices are shaped by technological infrastructures, social conventions, and changing public contexts. Taken together, linguistics at FLoV combines theoretical, empirical, and computational approaches to language. The subject provides a foundation for investigating linguistic structure, interaction, cognition, diversity, and meaning, while also contributing to the analysis of the relationship between language, technology, communicative practices, and the societal contexts in which language is used and has an impact. Duties The position is a career-development position with an emphasis on research and scholarly development in linguistics. Job assignments consist of research, teaching, and administration. Research: As an Assistant Professor in linguistics, you are expected to conduct and publish high-quality research with international relevance within your area of expertise. The position is intended to support your development of independence as a researcher and the establishment of your own research profile within the subject area. You are also expected to develop applications for external research funding, nationally and internationally, build research networks, collaborate with researchers both at and outside the department, and contribute actively to the subject’s research environment, including participation in the subject’s research seminar. Teaching: You are expected to contribute to teaching at first- and second-cycle level in linguistics. Teaching may include central areas such as phonetics/phonology, syntax, semantics, pragmatics, psycholinguistics, and sociolinguistics, as well as teaching in programmes where linguistics is included, such as the Master in Language Technology programme and the Master’s Programme for Communication Officers in the Public Sector. Courses are taught in either Swedish or English. Applicants who are not proficient in Swedish at the start of their employment are expected to gradually develop the language skills necessary to contribute to teaching in Swedish, in accordance with the language proficiency level specified under the eligibility requirements. Teaching duties include lectures, seminars, assessment, supervision, and teaching administration. You are also expected, to an extent appropriate for a career-development position, to contribute to course and programme development within the subject area. Administration: The position may also include other tasks, such as administrative duties and development work, where you, as an assistant professor, are expected to actively participate in the department's development and work environment efforts, including workplace meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. Most of the work will be carried out on site at the University of Gothenburg and you are therefore expected to be physically present at the department. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance (link) and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Found here (only in Swedish), under Personal-Handläggningsordning . Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in linguistics or has the corresponding research competence with clear relevance to the subject area of the position. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. The ability to teach in Swedish is meritorious. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. The person appointed as assistant professor is expected to acquire proficiency in written and spoken Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to CEFR within two years of the start of employment. Qualifications for teaching in higher education in accordance with requirements at the University of Gothenburg (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103) must be validated or completed within one year of the start of the employment, more information found here. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area linguistics. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. It is particularly meritorious to be able to teach in central areas of linguistics, such as phonetics/phonology, syntax, semantics, pragmatics, psycholinguistics, or sociolinguistics, and to contribute to teaching in programmes and courses where linguistics intersects with language technology, communication, or other related areas. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. In the overall assessment, particular emphasis will be placed on the applicant’s research expertise, development potential, and ability to establish an independent research profile in linguistics with relevance to the subject’s profile in linguistic interaction. The assessment will be based on documented qualifications, ongoing work, and the description of scholarly work and pedagogical work submitted by the applicant. In this description, the applicant should explain how their research relates to the subject’s overarching profile in linguistic interaction. Weight will also be given to documented interpersonal skills, the ability to contribute to a good academic environment, the ability to plan and organise research, and the ability to communicate research findings within and beyond academia. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Philosophy, Linguistics and Theory of Science, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. For promotion to senior lecturer, the assessment is based on demonstrated progression in terms of scientific and pedagogical skills. Eligibility requirements and rules regarding promotion to Senior Lecturer are described in Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg, linked above and in the Faculty of Humanities “Instructions for Applicants in connection with Promotions to and Appointments of Academically Qualified Teaching Staff other than Professors”, see link. As an assistant professor at the University of Gothenburg, you may apply for promotion to Associate Professor (docent). For more information, please contact the department's HR officer, Jennifer Stråle (contact details below). Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg Selection process When appointing an Assistant Professor, an assessment of all applications must be obtained from at least one external expert that is appointed after the application period has expired. The amount of time given to the external expert for their assessment depends on the number of applications received. The expert assessment is reviewed by the faculty's Teacher Proposal Committee, and then the department will convene interviews and trial lectures. Once the recruitment group has drafted a proposal for employment, it is also reviewed by the committee. The Teacher Proposal Committee meets once a month, which can result in a relatively long recruitment process for this type of position. See the process step by step below: Publication period Appoint external expert Expert review Teacher proposal committee I Interviews and trial lectures Teacher proposal committee II Employment decision Interviews and trial lectures are planned to take place via Zoom between 7-11 December. More information regarding the University’s recruitment process you will find here. Contact information For questions regarding the position, subject, responsibilities, or the workplace, please contact Alexander Almér, Head of Department, alexander.almer@gu.se. For questions regarding the application, recruitment process, or employment conditions, please contact HR Officer Jennifer Stråle, jennifer.strale@gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s vacancies website, here , select the advert for this position, and follow the link marked ‘Apply’ at the bottom of the page. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should be compiled in accordance with the instructions for qualification portfolios at the Faculty of Humanities, see link. Please note that the following documents should be attached to the application: Personal letter, maximum 500 words (1 page) List of degrees and employment A description of scholarly work and pedagogical work Doctoral degree certificate Doctoral thesis The full text of a maximum of 10 scientific articles that the applicant particularly wishes to cite, and a separate list of these documents Academic Portfolio I: Scientific qualifications Academic Portfolio II: Teaching qualifications Academic Portfolio III: Third-stream activities Academic Portfolio IV: Managerial and administrative competencies Any study materials that the applicant wishes to include Appendices (optional) Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. References for candidates in the final selection may be requested. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41716": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science Reference number PAR 2026/746 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental politics, corruption and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the best in Sweden and one of the top 10-15 in Europe in its subject area, according to the global Shanghai ranking of academic subjects for 2024. We organize a large number of research seminars and workshops each year in the various research areas of the department. We offer education at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have around 170 employees. For more information about the Department, visit our website www.pol.gu.se . The research project The postdoctoral fellow will join Mistra CLEAN , a research programme examining the connections between institutional failures and environmental challenges in Sweden and beyond. The programme focuses on how institutional failures—including corruption, policy and regulatory capture, and environmental crime—emerge, how they affect environmental governance and outcomes, and how institutional resilience can be strengthened. It brings together researchers from the University of Gothenburg and the Institute for Futures Studies in a multidisciplinary research environment. Mistra CLEAN has four main objectives: (1) to provide a comprehensive mapping of capture, corruption, and crime in Swedish environmental governance; (2) to investigate the causes and consequences of institutional failure, including through selected international comparisons; (3) to develop diagnostic tools for identifying institutional vulnerabilities and strengthening resilience in public and private organizations; and (4) to train a new generation of scholars working across disciplinary boundaries. The postdoctoral fellow will primarily contribute to research connected to two work packages based at the University of Gothenburg. WP2 (Vulnerability analysis and corruption risks) examines where and why corruption risks arise across environmental sectors, focusing on formal and informal rules, stakeholder constellations, accountability mechanisms, and emerging vulnerabilities associated with the green transition. WP4 (Environmental impacts of institutional failure) investigates how institutional failures contribute to environmental degradation and identifies mechanisms through which these effects may be addressed. This includes not only overt corruption and environmental crime, but also institutional “grey zones”, such as lobbying, close regulator–industry relationships, and vulnerabilities transmitted through global value chains. The position provides substantial scope for the postdoctoral fellow to develop an independent research agenda and initiate original studies within these broader themes. Research may draw on a range of methodological approaches and data sources, including quantitative and experimental methods, surveys, text analysis, interviews, administrative and organizational data, and AI-assisted approaches. Potential topics include the causes and consequences of corruption and capture, institutional vulnerabilities in the green transition, regulatory and organizational responses to environmental crime, accountability and institutional resilience, and the environmental consequences of institutional failure. The postdoctoral fellow will join a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment with expertise spanning political science, criminology, law, computer science, environmental economics, and philosophy. Mistra CLEAN offers strong opportunities for international publication, methodological development, and collaboration across institutions and disciplines, while providing considerable scope to build an independent research profile at the intersection of environmental governance, institutional failure, corruption, and environmental crime. A more detailed project description is available upon request. Subject area Political science. Subject area description Political Science is the study of politics. Duties The employment as a postdoctoral fellow aims to provide an early opportunity to develop independence as a researcher after completing a doctoral degree and to create favourable conditions for further academic qualifications. The postdoctoral fellow is expected to focus primarily on research. Teaching may be included in the duties but may comprise no more than one-fifth of the working time. The successful candidate will join a collaborative research team and be integrated into the research environment at the Department of Political Science, including the Centre for Environmental Political Studies (CEPS) and the Quality of Government (QoG) Institute. The position offers excellent opportunities for intellectual development, collaboration and mentoring, while providing substantial scope to build an independent research agenda and academic profile. The project also provides opportunities for international collaboration, conference participation, methodological development, and ambitious new data collection. We thus expect the successful candidate to take an independent role in developing high-quality studies within the broader framework of the project and to contribute to publications in leading international journals. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. For more information regarding eligibility please visit The Appointment Procedure GU 2024/1687 Assessment criteria Applicants must hold a PhD in political science or another field relevant to the project, no later than the date on which the appointment decision is made.  We are looking for candidates with strong methodological skills and an ability to develop rigorous and creative research designs. These may include, but are not limited to, the use of surveys and experiments, registry-based data, quantitative and qualitative text analysis, and interviews. In addition, a specialization in corruption, climate and environmental politics, or related fields is considered an asset. Applicants should have a strong interest in collaborative research and demonstrate the ambition and ability to produce high-quality research, both independently and as part of a team. We are also interested in candidates who enjoy developing creative research designs and theoretically motivated empirical studies. An interest in acquiring new methodological skills and in working across disciplinary boundaries is highly valued. Applicants should also have the ability to work constructively and effectively within a collaborative research environment while developing their own ideas and research profile. Applicants must have excellent written and oral proficiency in English. To be successful in this position, you must have an eye for detail, good organizational skills and be able to solve problems. You need to have excellent communication and collaboration skills. Great emphasis is placed on personal suitability for the position. We will make an overall assessment and the applicant who is considered to be best qualified to carry out and develop the tasks described above will be appointed to the position. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 2 years, with the possibility of extension up to 3 years 100% of full-time at the Department of Political Science. Starting date; August 2027 or as agreed. Contact information If you have questions regarding the employment, or details about the projects in which this position is organized, please contact Lisa Gastaldi, Lisa.Gastaldi@gu.se If you have any questions regarding the employment procedure, please contact Helena Jensen, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. We kindly ask you to write your application in English as the application may be reviewed by English-speaking staff. In the selection process, it will likely be relevant with interviews, reference taking and work tests in English. The application should include the following: A letter of application (max 3 pages), outlining how your research agenda can contribute to this project. It should clearly show the ways in which the applicant meets the requirements specified above. A CV (including list of publications) One writing sample A list of at least three references (with contact information) from whom letters of recommendation can be solicited Applications must be received by: 2026-11-19 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41709": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in clinical and epidemiological studies of airway diseases",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in clinical and epidemiological studies of airway diseases For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in clinical and epidemiological studies of airway diseases Reference number PAR 2026/794 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Internal Medicine and Clinical Nutrition, at the Institute of Medicine, conducts research in several areas, including osteoporosis, endocrinology, hematology, gastroenterology, hypertension, respiratory medicine and allergology, and clinical nutrition focusing on body composition and energy metabolism. The research is conducted in close collaboration with health care at both the Sahlgrenska University Hospital and Mölndal Hospital. The Krefting Research Centre ( http://krefting.gu.se ), Institute of Medicine, University of Gothenburg, is now recruiting a postdoctoral researcher. The Centre brings together more than 20 researchers and research nurses, organized into three complementary areas: the epidemiology of asthma, mechanistic studies of inflammation, and extracellular vesicles. This collaborative environment offers excellent opportunities for interdisciplinary exchange and professional development. An important resource at the Centre is the Swedish National Airway Register (SNAR), a national quality register that enables large-scale clinical and epidemiological studies of patients with asthma and COPD in routine Swedish healthcare. The register contains longitudinal data on disease characteristics, treatment, and clinical outcomes and can be linked to other national health data registers, providing opportunities to investigate disease course, treatment patterns, prognosis, and other clinically relevant questions. Another important research resource is the West Sweden Asthma Study (WSAS), a large population-based epidemiological study of asthma and lung diseases. Research is conducted within a broad network of national and international collaborations, including Breathe Sweden. The successful candidate will work on epidemiological research questions in asthma and COPD, with a particular focus on analyses of SNAR data and linked register data, while also having opportunities to contribute to projects based on WSAS and other research collaborations. The position therefore offers excellent opportunities to develop independent research with clear clinical relevance in the field of airway diseases. Subject area Clinical and epidemiological studies of airway diseases Specific subject description Airway diseases such as asthma and COPD are complex conditions in which disease courses and clinical outcomes can be difficult to predict. Large epidemiological studies with longitudinal data are needed to improve our understanding of their long-term consequences and treatment in clinical practice. The Swedish National Airway Register (SNAR) is a key resource enabling clinical and epidemiological studies of patients with asthma and COPD in routine clinical care in Sweden, including studies of disease course, treatment, treatment patterns, clinical outcomes, and prognosis. The West Sweden Asthma Study (WSAS) provides a complementary resource for population-based epidemiological research on asthma and lung diseases. Together, these data sources provide excellent opportunities to address a broad range of clinically relevant and epidemiological research questions related to asthma and COPD. Job assignments The role involves responsibility for analysing and publishing results from epidemiological studies of airway diseases, with a particular focus on data from the Swedish National Airway Register (SNAR) and the West Sweden Asthma Study (WSAS). An important part of the work will address clinical and epidemiological research questions in COPD, including treatment, treatment patterns, disease course, and clinical outcomes. The position will also involve other epidemiological research questions related to both COPD and asthma. The work includes independent management and analysis of large longitudinal datasets and responsibility for ensuring high scientific standards in data quality and statistical analyses. Results will be published in peer-reviewed scientific journals and presented at national and international scientific meetings. The role requires the ability to work independently and drive research projects forward, while collaborating closely with researchers at the Krefting Research Centre, the University of Gothenburg, Sahlgrenska University Hospital, and national and international research partners. The position also includes contributing to the supervision of PhD students and other junior researchers associated with the projects. Eligibility To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in a relevant subject, or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment The applicant must have a PhD degree in biomedicine, clinical research, health sciences, epidemiology, statistics, or another relevant field, and have documented experience in statistical modeling within clinical research or epidemiology. Excellent oral and written English skills are required, as well as the ability to produce coherent scientific texts, demonstrated by at least one published first-author article. Research experience in COPD and/or asthma is considered an advantage. Research experience in pharmacology and/or public health, as well as experience in statistical analyses using SAS, SPSS and/or R, is also considered an advantage. The position requires good collaborative skills for working with colleagues and external research groups, as well as the ability to work independently, in a structured manner, and to drive research projects forward. Great importance will be placed on personal suitability, particularly collaborative skills, independence, and a structured approach to work. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, 36 months Extent: 100 % Location: Institute of Medicine, Gothenburg First day of employment: According to agreement Contact information Lowie Vanfleteren Senior lecturer lowie.vanfleteren@gu.se Madeleine Rådinger Assistant Head of Department madeleine.radinger@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Information for International Applicants Visit the webpage Join us - jobs and career for more information on the Institute of Medicine and what opportunities we have for you. Discover our world-class research and research infrastructure, as well as what the benefits are for you and your family: https://www.gu.se/en/medicine/about-us/join-us-jobs-and-career. Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ Application Please attach PhD certificate with the application, if the PhD certificate has not yet been issued, it needs to be attached latest at the time when the employment decision is to be made. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-29 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41711": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral Fellow in Political Science Reference number PAR 2026/744 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental politics, corruption and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the best in Sweden and one of the top departments in Europe. We organize many seminars and workshops each year in the various research areas of the department. We offer education at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have around 170 employees. For more information about the Department, please visit https://www.gu.se/en/political-science . The Department of Political Science now invites applications for a postdoctoral fellowship within Dying Occupations and Political Change (OCCPOL), a five-year research project funded by an ERC Advanced Grant and led by Professor Johannes Lindvall. OCCPOL investigates how the decline and disappearance of occupations shape political participation, political opinions, and party choices in democracies. The project examines the political consequences of occupational decline and disappearance in four countries, Germany, Sweden, the United Kingdom, and the United States, from the late nineteenth century to the present. A central goal is to create a new comparative data resource tracing long-term occupational changes in these countries and use it to conduct analyses of political trends and events. This part of the project will combine historical censuses, labor-force surveys, official registers, and other sources, using transparent coding protocols, computational methods, and careful validation. The resulting data will support research within OCCPOL and will be documented for wider scholarly use. The postdoctoral fellow will be an intellectual partner in developing this key part of the project. The position offers substantial opportunities to shape the project conceptually and methodologically, pursue original research using the new data, and develop a distinctive scholarly profile within the broad themes of OCCPOL. Subject area Political science Subject area description Political science is the study of politics. Duties Employment as a postdoctoral fellow aims to give the employee an early opportunity, after completing their doctoral degree, to develop their independence as a researcher and to create the conditions for further qualifications. A postdoctoral fellow must mainly conduct research. Teaching may be included in the duties but may account for no more than one-fifth of working time. The postdoctoral fellow will pursue original research on occupational decline and political change within the broader framework of OCCPOL. They will have substantial intellectual autonomy to develop and pursue their own research agenda within this framework, conducting research independently and in collaboration with other members of the project. They will have opportunities to lead their own studies, publish independently and collaboratively, and present their research at seminars, workshops, and international conferences. The position is designed to combine intellectual autonomy with close collaboration and regular intellectual exchange within the project, with the aim of supporting the postdoctoral fellow in developing their own scholarly profile and research agenda. As part of this research, the postdoctoral fellow will play a central coordinating role, together with the Principal Investigator and other members of the team, in developing OCCPOL's comparative data infrastructure. This will be a shared empirical resource, developed collaboratively and used by members of the project in their individual and joint research. The postdoctoral fellow will participate actively in the substantive and methodological choices involved in its development, including the collection, processing, harmonization, validation, and documentation of occupational data across the four project countries. This work may include developing coding protocols and reproducible data workflows; identifying and evaluating relevant historical and contemporary data sources; developing and applying appropriate computational methods for classifying occupational information; assessing changes in occupational classifications over time; and developing approaches to quality assurance. The specific methods and approaches used will be shaped by the research questions, the characteristics of the available data, and the postdoctoral fellow's interests and expertise. The postdoctoral fellow will be an active participant in shaping the intellectual development of OCCPOL. The project is intended to provide an environment in which independently initiated and collaborative research reinforce one another, and in which new research questions and approaches can emerge as the work develops. The postdoctoral fellow will work closely with the Principal Investigator and other members of the team while retaining substantial scope to develop their own research within the project. The precise balance between independently initiated research, collaborative research, and shared data development will be agreed jointly as the project develops, considering both the postdoctoral fellow's evolving research interests and expertise and the goals of the wider project. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. Relevant “related subjects” may include sociology, economics, economic history, social and economic geography, computational social science, or another discipline in which the applicant has acquired research expertise directly relevant to the position. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. For more information regarding eligibility, please see The Appointment Procedure at GU Assessment criteria Applicants must have a doctoral degree in political science or a related subject no later than the date on which the appointment decision is made. We seek applicants with research experience relevant to the research area described above. Applicants will be assessed primarily on their scientific qualifications and their potential to contribute to and develop the research conducted within OCCPOL. Particular importance will be attached to: the quality, originality, and relevance of the applicant’s own research; demonstrated ability to conduct independent research and produce high-quality scholarly work; experience of quantitative methods; experience of data construction, data harmonization, classification, record linkage, or the management of complex research data; proficiency in relevant programming environments, especially R or Python; the applicant’s ideas concerning how the OCCPOL data could be developed and used; and motivation to participate actively in a collaborative research project and contribute to its shared infrastructure. Experience of machine learning, automated text analysis, historical census or register data, occupational classifications, comparative research, or research on labor-market and political change will be considered meritorious. Knowledge of one or more project countries and relevant language skills will also be considered an asset. Applicants are not expected to have prior experience with every country, period, data source, or method used in OCCPOL. The assessment will consider the overall strength and complementarity of the applicant’s qualifications, including their capacity to identify suitable methods for new research problems and acquire additional skills where necessary. Great weight will be placed on the applicant's capacity to combine intellectual independence and initiative with constructive collaboration and responsibility for shared work. Applicants must have excellent written and oral proficiency in English. To be successful in this position, applicants must demonstrate attention to detail, strong organizational and problem-solving skills, and excellent communication and collaboration skills. Great emphasis is placed on personal suitability for the position. We will make an overall assessment and the applicant who is considered best qualified to carry out and develop the tasks described above will be appointed to the position. Employment This is a fixed-term appointment for 24 months at 100% of full time, with the possibility to extend the employment by another 12 months after review, based at the Department of Political Science. The starting date is 1 September 2027 at the latest, preferably earlier. Contact information For questions about the position or the project within which it is based, please contact Johannes Lindvall, Johannes.lindvall@gu.se . If you have any questions regarding the employment procedure, please contact Helena Jensen, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please write your application in English, as it may be reviewed by English-speaking staff. The selection process is likely to include interviews, reference checks, and work tests conducted in English. The application should include the following: A letter of application (max 3 pages) outlining how the applicant’s own evolving research agenda can contribute to the project. It should explain clearly how the applicant meets the requirements specified above. A CV (including a list of publications). An article-length writing sample. A list of at least three references (with contact information) from whom letters of recommendation may be solicited. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-19 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41688": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Political Science: Transitional Justice, Victimhood, and Survey Experiments (BIG PEACE",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Political Science: Transitional Justice, Victimhood, and Survey Experiments (BIG PEACE For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Political Science: Transitional Justice, Victimhood, and Survey Experiments (BIG PEACE) Reference number PAR 2026/745 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental politics, corruption and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the best in Sweden and one of the top 10-15 in Europe in its subject area, according to the global Shanghai ranking of academic subjects for 2024. We organize a large number of research seminars and workshops each year in the various research areas of the department. We offer education at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have around 170 employees. For more information about the Department, visit our website www.pol.gu.se . Description of the project: The Department of Political Science invites applications for a post-doctoral fellow, to conduct research within “Bridging Injustice Gaps: Peace Enhancement in the Aftermath of Conflict Engagement” (BIG PEACE), funded by The Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation with additional funding from the University of Gothenburg . This is a 2-year position, 100% of full-time, with possibility for a third year extension if mutually agreed upon. The BIG PEACE research program aims to understand the extent to which transitional justice can foster reconciliation. This project examines justice from the perspectives of those most affected—victims, former combatants, and local communities. This focus also brings attention to how victimhood is recognized in conflict-affected societies, and whether the logic of victimhood recognition shifts as we move from individual perceptions to collective, politically contested claims at meta-levels. It also questions the assumption that state-led justice is always the best approach, recognizing that in many war-torn societies, religious and tribal leaders play a crucial role in resolving disputes. This study will take a data-driven approach, gathering insights through in-depth interviews, large-n surveys, and experiments in multiple conflict-affected countries. It will explore key questions: What does justice mean to different people? Who counts as a victim of civil war, and who decides? Who do conflict affected stakeholders trust to deliver justice? And how do perceptions of justice shape peace and stability? Bringing victimhood recognition into these questions extends the study of injustice gaps to whose suffering is recognized in the first place and how such recognition may differ across individuals and groups. By identifying patterns across cultures and conflicts, BIG PEACE seeks to provide practical recommendations for policymakers and peacebuilders. The goal is to move beyond one-size-fits-all solutions and develop a more inclusive, grassroots approach to justice—one that truly addresses the needs of war-torn societies and helps prevent future violence. The postdoctoral fellow will join a collaborative research environment at the Department of Political Science, University of Gothenburg. BIG PEACE provides opportunities for international publication, methodological development, comparative and cross-country fieldwork, and collaboration within and beyond the project team, including opportunities for co-authorship. At the same time, the position provides substantial scope for developing an independent research profile at the intersection of political violence, transitional justice, ethnic politics, public opinion, and peace and conflict research. A more detailed project description is available upon request. Subject area Political science Subject area description Political Science is the study of politics. Keywords: Post-Conflict reconciliation, Survey methods, Injustice, Victimhood, Experimental design and analysis, Large n-Data, transitional justice, political violence, causal inference. Duties The employment as a postdoctoral fellow aims to give the employee an early opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher after completing their doctoral degree and to create the conditions for further qualifications. The person employed as a postdoctoral fellow must mainly conduct research. Teaching can be included in the work duties, but to a maximum of one-fifth of the working time. The postdoctoral fellow will be employed at the Department of Political Science, University of Gothenburg, and is expected to engage in close collaborative research with Kristen Kao and other members of the BIG PEACE project team. Much of the work will involve designing, programming, running, and analyzing large-N surveys and survey experiments, making strong survey research skills essential to the position. The postdoctoral fellow will be expected to develop and implement original survey and experimental designs, applying causal inference in the analysis of project data. The work will also involve contributing to data collection and fieldwork across conflict-affected countries included in the project. Thus, prior fieldwork experience or knowledge of foreign languages is a merit. Duties also include helping to organize and attending one or two international workshops or conferences; traveling to present the project’s research to international scholarly, policymaker, and development practitioner audiences; as well as contributing to and leading high-quality manuscripts based on the project data for publication in international peer-reviewed journals. The position offers opportunities for co-authorship with members of the BIG PEACE research team, alongside substantial scope to initiate original studies and develop independent publications and research questions related to the broader themes of the project. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. For more information regarding eligibility please visit The Appointment Procedure GU 2024/1687 Assessment criteria The assessment will primarily be based on scientific merit and the applicant’s ability to conduct and develop research within the BIG PEACE project. Particular weight will be given to methodological competence, the quality of the applicant’s research, and their potential to contribute to and independently develop the project’s research agenda. The following qualifications are required: A doctoral degree in political science or another field relevant to the project, obtained (or be close to completing) no later than the time at which the employment decision is made. Documented experience in running, programming, and analyzing surveys and survey experiments . Strong knowledge of causal inference and experimental methods , including the design and analysis of experiments. Strong quantitative research skills and proficiency in relevant statistical software, such as R or Stata. Demonstrated ability to conduct independent, high-quality research and produce scientific work suitable for publication in international peer-reviewed journals. Excellent written and oral proficiency in English. The following qualifications are considered meritorious: Experience conducting fieldwork and original data collection in post-conflict or conflict-affected settings. Research experience related to civil war, political violence, transitional justice, victimhood, reconciliation, peacebuilding, or closely related fields. Experience with comparative or cross-national research. Knowledge of Swedish language and/or languages relevant to the project’s fieldwork contexts, such as Somali, Ukrainian, or relevant languages spoken in other post-conflict settings that might make for a good case study for the project. Experience with complementary methods relevant to the project, such as in-depth qualitative interviewing or large text analysis. A good record of scientific publications, high-quality working papers, and/or collaborative research. To be successful in this position, you should be able to work both independently and collaboratively, take responsibility for research tasks, and contribute constructively to an international research environment. You should have strong analytical and problem-solving skills, an eye for detail, good organizational skills, and excellent communication and collaboration skills. Great emphasis is placed on personal suitability for the position. We will make an overall assessment and the applicant who is considered to be best qualified to carry out and develop the tasks described above will be appointed to the position. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 24 months, 100% of full-time at the Department of Political Science. Starting date; 08/2027 or as agreed. Contact information If you have questions regarding the employment, or details about the projects in which this position is organized, please contact Kristen Kao, Kristen.kao@gu.se If you have any questions regarding the employment procedure, please contact Helena Jensen, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. We kindly ask you to write your application in English as the application may be reviewed by English-speaking staff. In the selection process, it should be expected that interviews, reference taking, and work tests will occur in English. The application should include the following: A letter of application (max 3 pages), outlining how your research agenda can contribute to this project. It should clearly show the ways in which the applicant meets the requirements specified above with particular attention to experience running, programming, and analyzing surveys and survey experiments, and to knowledge and application of causal inference and experimental methods. A CV (including list of publications) and a link/links to evidence of survey or experimental work, such as a pre-analysis plan or replication code. One writing sample A list of at least three references (with contact information) from whom letters of recommendation can be solicited. Letters will be requested only from shortlisted candidates. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-19 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41703": {
      "title": "Associate Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Associate Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Associate Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/793 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences is one of six Institutes within Sahlgrenska Academy at the University of Gothenburg. We conduct research and education that puts people's health and quality of life in focus. We represent 16 different research areas and are divided into four sections. Through a fruitful collaboration with the healthcare and Sahlgrenska University Hospital, we have developed joint projects and meeting places that benefit both health care and research The Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of Urology at the Sahlgrenska Academy, is seeking an associate researcher with a focus on prostate cancer. Research at the Department of Urology focuses on urological cancers, with particular emphasis on prostate cancer, urolithiasis, and reconstructive surgery. The research group has conducted world class research on prostate cancer screening for 30 years, primarily based on two large randomised studies, one of which is ongoing (G2). For several years, the group has also carried out register based research on prostate cancer, currently focusing mainly on the regionally organised prostate cancer testing programme (OPT), which has now offered testing to 350,000 Swedish men. Duties The duties consist mainly of independent research related to prostate cancer and participation in seminars for researchers and doctoral students. The work includes independent processing, editing, and analysis of large datasets, including registry data, as well as interpretation of research findings. The duties also include writing scientific articles and collaborating with other researchers both within and outside the research group. Qualifications For the position, it is required that the applicant has competence in epidemiology and documented experience of epidemiological research. The position further requires good knowledge of prostate cancer, documented practical experience of independently editing and analysing large datasets, as well as strong ability to communicate in spoken and written English. In the assessment of applicants, particular emphasis will be placed on scientific independence and analytical ability, experience of research in the field of prostate cancer, experience of register-based research and work with large epidemiological datasets, as well as the ability to collaborate within the research group and with external partners. Employment The position is a fixed-term, part-time employment (20%) for a period of five months, and is placed at the Institute of Clinical Sciences the department of urology. Starting date: As soon as possible Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professor Ola Bratt; 46 702 763233 or ola.bratt@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. The application should contain: A personal letter outlining your motivation for applying this position. Copies of relevant documents demonstrating both formal and informal qualifications. At least two referees It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-29 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41680": {
      "title": "Research Associate, digital fieldwork, THINGSTIGATE",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Research Associate, digital fieldwork, THINGSTIGATE For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Research Associate, digital fieldwork, THINGSTIGATE Reference number PAR 2026/769 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design, at the University of Gothenburg, conducts education and research in Design, Film, Photography, Fine Art, Crafts and Literary Composition – as well as teacher education in Visual Arts and Sloyd. We provide highly specialised degree programmes in an outgoing, international environment – where our unique and broad range of subjects also enables our students and staff to foster a broader artistic perspective, as well as to explore new working models and research fields. Together with our sister department – the Academy of Music and Drama – we form the most broad-ranging faculty of arts in Scandinavia. We are seeking a Research Associate to join the team of the European Research Council-funded project THINGSTIGATE – Things for Politics’ Sake: Aesthetic Objects and Social Change (ERC grant 101041284), led by Dr Tintin Wulia (Principal Investigator/PI). Read more about HDK-Valand at: https://www.gu.se/hdk-valand The faculty´s vision With the challenges facing society today, education and research in the artistic field is more important than ever. One of the strengths of art is that it provides perspective and critical tools, as well as meaning and hope. The Artistic Faculty is a driving force in art, culture and learning, as well as in creativity and innovation in a broader sense. We are a locally based and globally oriented place for art, knowledge, criticality, vision and transformation. Art and culture are for everyone, and we are a hub that brings together cultural practitioners, cultural institutions, creative industries and wider civil society. Our goal is to be a house with many entrances and even more exits. Subject area Crafts & Fine Art Duties As a Research Associate you will be responsible for a pilot of public interventions as a field method in the project’s digital and Indonesian contexts, in collaboration with the partner collective 1965 Setiap Hari – whose work relates to the 1965-66 Indonesian mass killings – as part of the project’s WP3.2. You will lead the delivery of the WP3.2 pilot, supported by the Research Operations Coordinator, the Local Production Assistant, and Research Assistants, and report to the PI. Eligibility Doctoral studies in anthropology, sociology, art, design, craft, or cognate disciplines nearing completion, or completed no earlier than one year before the application deadline. Assessment criteria Essentials: Demonstrable knowledge of the project’s conceptual and theoretical framework Experience of object-centred research using digital methods, action research, public interventions, ethnographic fieldwork, and/or participatory methods Experience of ethical fieldwork in politically sensitive contexts Substantial professional experience in the NGO, civil society, or cultural sector, including responsibility for partnerships or risk management in sensitive settings Excellent written and spoken English, with a record of academic publication or equivalent published practice-based output Good competency in Bahasa Indonesia as the language of the fieldwork Desirable: Experience in working with social media data and archival material Sustained ethnographic research experience within sociology or anthropology Existing links with Indonesian civil society organisations relevant to the pilot’s sensitive research context Employment The employment is full-time (100%) 11 month position and starts no later than the Spring semester 2027. The position is based in Gothenburg. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact THINGSTIGATE PI, Tintin Wulia, phone number +46 766 186853 or email at tintin.wulia@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR function at HDK-Valand, hr@hdk-valand.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application Application Your application may be reviewed by international experts, and it is proposed that the application and its appendices be written in English. If the application is written in Swedish, it may be translated. In the event of any discrepancy between the translated document and the Swedish original, the department accepts no responsibility for the translation or for any direct, indirect or other errors that may arise in the translation. You apply for the position via the University of Gothenburg's recruitment portal by clicking on ‘Apply’. As an applicant, you are responsible for ensuring that your application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is received by the university no later than the closing date for applications. Publications that cannot be attached to the application digitally should be sent to: HDK-Valand HR Ref. PAR 2026/769 Box 131, S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden and must be received by the closing date for applications. The application should contain: A motivation letter describing why you are interested in contributing to THINGSTIGATE and your relevant experiences, addressing the assessment criteria and how your work aligns with THINGSTIGATE’s framework, maximum 2 pages. A CV including a complete list of publications, a list of fieldwork (stating sites, duration, and methods), and a statement of proficiency in and experience using Bahasa Indonesia. A portfolio of up to 3 relevant projects you have produced or co-produced, each with up to 2 images and a 100-word project description. Applicants without a project-based portfolio may submit one writing sample, such as an excerpt from a dissertation chapter or article, maximum 3,000 words. Proof of doctoral status: either a formal statement from the institution or its representative on the status of thesis submission, or a copy of the degree certificate. Contact details of two referees Applications must be received by: 2026-10-23 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41662": {
      "title": "Research assistent",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Research assistent For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Research assistent Reference number PAR 2026/774 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. At the Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology, we are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic associate researcher with a background in biotechnology, molecular biology, or a related field. The successful candidate will contribute to a research project aimed at characterizing molecular signatures underlying clonal hematopoiesis and investigating their roles in the development of leukemia. Duties The researcher will be responsible for handling biological samples, performing molecular analysis using state-of-the-art molecular biology techniques, and analyzing the resulting data. The researcher will also contribute to ongoing next-generation sequencing (NGS) and single-cell omics projects in the lab. Qualifications The applicant must hold a Master's degree in a relevant field, such as molecular biology, systems biology, biotechnology or similar subject. Documented laboratory experience in handling biological samples and using molecular biology techniques is required. Previous research experience in stem cell biology, hematology, and sequencing-based methods is considered a strong merit. Fluency in both spoken and written English is required. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability for the position. Employment The position is a full-time, fixed-term appointment for 11 months, based at the Institute of Biomedicine. Start date as per agreeement. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Abhishek Niroula, Associate Senior Lecturer, +46 (0)72 442 0580, abhishek.niroula@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please apply online. The application should contain: A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying A CV including a list of publications Proof of completed MSc Degree Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026-10-26 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41669": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/777 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for a researcher position at the Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. Duties The position is linked to research projects in obesity, metabolism and physiological regulation of body weight at the Department of Physiology. The successful candidate will primarily work with the analysis and scientific processing of previously collected research data from clinical randomized controlled trials (RCTs) investigating weight-bearing load, body weight regulation, obesity and cardiometabolic health. Responsibilities include statistical analyses, interpretation of results and integration of data from multiple assessment modalities, including body composition measurements, medical imaging, heart rate variability (HRV), circulating biomarkers and clinical outcome measures. The position also includes preparation of scientific manuscripts for publication in international peer-reviewed journals, literature reviews, scientific reporting and presentation of research findings at national and international scientific meetings. The researcher is expected to contribute actively to the dissemination and publication of results generated within the research projects. Additional duties may include participation in the planning of future research projects, preparation of research grant applications and support of ongoing research activities within the group. The work will be conducted in close collaboration with researchers at the University of Gothenburg, Sahlgrenska University Hospital and national and international collaborators in physiology, obesity and metabolism research. Qualifications A PhD degree in physiology, medicine, or a closely related subject area is required. Applicants must have documented experience of independent research within physiology, metabolism, obesity research, or a related field. Experience in statistical analysis and scientific processing of human physiological data is required. Applicants must have documented experience in conducting, coordinating and analysing clinical randomized controlled trials (RCTs), as well as a demonstrated publication record in international peer-reviewed scientific journals. Proven ability to independently prepare scientific manuscripts in English is required. The following qualifications are considered meritorious: experience in obesity, body composition, or energy metabolism research experience with heart rate variability (HRV) analyses and autonomic nervous system research experience with medical image analysis and body composition assessments experience with biomarker and laboratory data from clinical studies experience in preparing research grant applications and securing external research funding a medical degree and clinical experience in internal medicine or a related discipline. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. The successful candidate must be able to work independently, collaborate effectively within multidisciplinary research teams, and demonstrate the ability to take research projects from data analysis to scientific publication. Excellent written and spoken English communication skills are required. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 7 months, 25% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have questions regarding the employment please contact manager in charge, Professor/Assistant Head of Department Suzanne L Dickson, phone: +46 31 786 35 68, e-mail: suzanne.dickson@neuro.gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact persons. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please enclose PhD certificate with the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-26 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41612": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science - CAR T cell therapy for B cell malignancies",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science - CAR T cell therapy for B cell malignancies For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science - CAR T cell therapy for B cell malignancies Reference number PAR 2026/729 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical Science Project title: Monitoring CAR T-cell therapy in patients with B-cell malignancies The Department of microbiology and immunology is located at the Institute of Biomedicine  at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Cellular immunotherapies such as CD19-directed CAR T-cell therapy have improved treatment options for patients with relapsed or refractory B-cell malignancies. Although some patients achieve durable remissions, treatment responses vary and serious adverse events, including cytokine release syndrome (CRS) and immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity syndrome (ICANS), remain common. As the use of Advanced Therapy Medicinal Products (ATMPs) expands, clinically applicable monitoring strategies are needed to support treatment decisions and improve patient safety. This doctoral project aims to characterize CD19 CAR T-cells longitudinally in patients with B-cell malignancies and establish monitoring strategies for future ATMP workflows, including point-of-care CD19 CAR T-cell manufacturing. The project is based on an ongoing prospective cohort of patients treated with CD19 CAR T-cells. Blood samples collected at multiple time points after infusion will be used to investigate CAR T-cell expansion, persistence, phenotype and function. The role of neutrophils and their potential influence on CAR T-cell expansion and effector function will also be studied. Laboratory findings will be integrated with clinical data to investigate associations with treatment response, durable remission, relapse and treatment-related toxicities. The doctoral student will use multiparameter and spectral flow cytometry, functional T-cell assays, cytokine analyses and cell culture. The project also includes development and validation of clinically relevant assays for future CAR T-cell manufacturing and quality-control workflows. The research is conducted in a translational environment, in close collaboration with the Department of Hematology at Sahlgrenska University Hospital and research groups at the Department of Microbiology and Immunology and Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research, University of Gothenburg. Experience or interest in immunology, flow cytometry, cell culture, advanced laboratory diagnostics or cell therapies is meritorious. The doctoral student will be part of BioLabX, Sweden's national research school in biomedical laboratory scien ce. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Biomedicine, Department of microbiology and immunology. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Biomedicine require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Sukanya Raghavan, Institute of Biomedicine, e-mail: sukanya.raghavan@microbio.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-10-26 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41649": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/767 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences, is searching for a researcher to work within projects in cancer epidemiology, radiobiological modelling and advanced survival analyses. The research group has large experience of epidemiological and clinical research in which molecular markers and dose data are used to study effects on tumours but also to study side effects of treatment, such as elevated risk of cancer occurrence after treatment. The new knowledge will help to better balance risks and benefits of different new cancer treatments. Duties The researcher will work within projects in cancer epidemiology, radiobiological modelling and advanced survival analyses. The task is to perform independent work in the group with dose-response analyses using the software EPICURE, perform linkage of cohorts to several population-bases registers, perform methods for calculations of relative survival and competing risk analyses. Qualifications For the position as researcher, a PhD in medical sciences is required. Documented competence in cancer epidemiology, radiophysics and statistics within clinical sciences is a strongly meritorious. Experience of epidemiological cancer research and experience of radiobiological research is needed. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 12 months, 60 % of full-time and is placed at the Institute of Clinical Sciences. Starting date; 2027-01-01. Contact information for the post If you have questions regarding the position please contact Professor Per Karlsson, phone: + 46 31 342 1000, e- mail: per.karlsson@oncology.gu.se. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-26 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41598": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science - reproductive genetics and bioinformatics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science - reproductive genetics and bioinformatics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science - reproductive genetics and bioinformatics Reference number PAR 2026/687 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical Science Project title: The role of aneuploidies and fetal and maternal genetics in pregnancy loss The Department of Molecular and Clinical Medicine is located at the Institute of Medicine at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Pregnancy loss is the most common pregnancy complication, affecting approximately 1 in 7 clinically recognized pregnancies. Around half of pregnancy losses involve fetal chromosomal abnormalities, particularly aneuploidies, while the causes of the other half are usualy unknown. This PhD project aims to improve our understanding of the genetic mechanisms underlying pregnancy loss by developing computational approaches that may contribute to improved molecular diagnostics. The doctoral student will investigate the contribution of rare and common fetal genetic variants to pregnancy loss and how their effects differ between pregnancies with and without aneuploidies. The project will also develop scalable computational methods for detecting whole-chromosome and segmental aneuploidies using genotype-array and whole-genome sequencing data. The project uses large-scale genetic datasets from pregnancy losses and population-based cohorts and will be conducted in a collaborative, international research environment at the Wallenberg Laboratory, Sahlgrenska Academy. Meritorious experience includes: – working in R/Python and UNIX environments – statistical modelling in genetics or epidemiology; – analysis of copy-number variants or aneuploidies; – population genetics, GWAS or gene–environment interaction studies; – large-scale data analysis or bioinformatics; – pregnancy biology or statistics; and – reproducible research using tools such as Git, Snakemake and conda. Scientific writing experience, particularly demonstrated through authorship of scientific manuscripts, is also highly valued. The project requires close collaboration within and across research groups while providing substantial opportunities to develop and pursue independent research ideas. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Medicine, Department of Molecular and Clinical Medicine Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Medicine require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Pol Solé-Navais, Institute of Medicine, e-mail: pol.sole.navais@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-10-23 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41603": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Human Geography",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Human Geography For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Human Geography Reference number PAR 2026/715 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Economy and Society at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg is announcing 1 doctoral student position in Human Geography at the Unit for Human Geography. Human Geography concerns how people, companies and regions develop in relation to the outside world. We study the geographical conditions, relationships and processes of change, and all the factors that influence the development of the society in different places around the world. We take a holistic approach to the development of the society - locally, regionally and globally. Our courses look at how people, companies and regions develop in relation to their surroundings. We are interested in geographical conditions, relations and change processes. We study the diversity of factors which affect the development of society, the constructed environment and human culture in various locations around the world. The unit offers a bachelor's programme in social analysis as well as courses at undergraduate and graduate level. For more information: Unit for Human Geography | University of Gothenburg . General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages: https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies . Duties We are looking for a doctoral student for the research project CLOSE – Consequences of Local Service Expansions. The work in the project will begin immediately after the employment begins. For more information about the duties, see \"About the research project\". Doctoral studies comprise four years of full-time study and lead to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20% of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, which results in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels but may also include research and administration. More information about the doctoral education at the department can be found at: Doctoral Studies | University of Gothenburg . About the research project CLOSE – Consequences of Local Service Expansions examines the long-term social and economic consequences of introducing new neighbourhood services in Swedish cities and urban neighbourhoods. The project takes as its point of departure the growing interest in proximity-based urban planning, for example ideas associated with the “15-minute city”, where everyday services such as grocery stores, schools, healthcare centres, culture, leisure activities and other services are located close to people’s homes. Good geographical proximity to everyday services can contribute to more vibrant neighbourhoods, reduce car dependency and create better conditions for walking and cycling. At the same time, new service establishments may also increase the attractiveness of an area, affect housing prices and rents, change residential mobility patterns and contribute to the displacement of certain groups or businesses. The project therefore examines both the opportunities and risks associated with improved proximity to services, with a particular focus on neighbourhood change, segregation, gentrification and local business dynamics. The project analyses developments in Swedish cities over the period 1995–2025 using Swedish longitudinal register data. These data contain annual information on individuals and workplaces with precise geographical coordinates, making it possible to follow how people move, how neighbourhoods change, and how services and firms are established, develop and disappear over time. Methodologically, the project builds on quantitative and spatial analyses. The project centres on two overarching research questions. The first concerns how new service establishments affect residential mobility and the social composition of neighbourhoods: do they attract new groups, contribute to social mixing, or reinforce segregation and displacement? The second concerns how new service establishments affect local service and business environments: do certain services function as anchors that stimulate further establishments, or do they contribute to commercial gentrification in which small and locally rooted actors are replaced by other types of businesses? The doctoral student’s work will be an integrated part of the project. The doctoral student is expected to work with quantitative analyses of register data, spatial data and longitudinal processes of change. Within the framework of the project, there is scope to place greater emphasis on, for example, residential mobility and population composition, socio-economic neighbourhood change, service provision and local business dynamics. The doctoral project can thus be adapted to the doctoral student’s research interests, while contributing to the overall aims of the project. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totaling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. To meet the specific entry requirements for doctoral studies in Human Geography, applicants must have completed 90 credits in Human Geography/Geography, of which at least 15 credits, consist of a degree project at advanced level, in the subject or in a related subject. To be qualified, the applicant must demonstrate a high level of proficiency in both spoken and written English corresponding to English 6/English B at Swedish upper secondary school level, or an equivalent level demonstrated through an internationally recognised test. For more information about general entrance requirements, see www.universityadmissions.se (English) and www.antagning.se . Applicants who do not speak Swedish already will be expected to learn Swedish during the doctoral education. Applicants can apply before fulfilling all these requirements but the decision for admission can only be made after all requirements are fulfilled. Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate doctoral studies. During the assessment, we will specifically evaluate and place a high value upon documented experience of: Education of relevance to the research project, in addition to that which provides general and specific entry requirements. Experience of qualified research and investigative work of relevance to the research project. Experience with quantitative methods relevant to the project, such as analysis of large datasets, statistical analysis, longitudinal analyses, spatial analyses or other quantitative approaches. Experience of empirical studies focused on urban development, urban proximity, mobility, service provision, neighbourhood change, segregation, gentrification, local economy, community planning or related areas. Ability to independently acquire and use relevant quantitative analysis tools, software and data environments. Publications and theses at bachelor’s, one-year master’s and two-year master’s level. Research plan: relevance to the project, ability to formulate a research problem, processing of previous research, methodological and theoretical awareness, independent ideas and academic writing. Great importance will be placed on personal suitability. You should have a very good ability to work independently, but also very good collaboration skills as you will work closely with the supervisor, the research environment and the project's external reference group. Proficiency in Swedish is considered a merit, corresponding to the Swedish upper secondary school courses Swedish 3 (Svenska 3) or Swedish as a Second Language 3 (Svenska som andraspråk 3). Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. The position is located at the Unit for Human Geography, Department of Economy and Society. The position is funded by Formas together with the University of Gothenburg Starting date is 2027-01-04 or as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at Department of Economy and Society require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, Department of Economy and Society has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. For applicants with a foreign degree If your degree is from a university/HEI outside of Sweden or has not been examined by a Swedish authority, it is important that you include diploma and transcript of records to facilitate evaluation. Please also provide contact details (telephone number or email address) for the awarding institution so that the degree can be verified. For information on how to meet the specific English language requirement, see: https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ . For information on how to document the Swedish language proficiency considered a merit, see: Tisus – Test in Swedish for university studies . Selection process The selection process may include interviews and checks of applicants’ references. Contact information If you have questions about the employment, you are welcome to contact: Erik Elldér, Associate professor, Project manager, erik.ellder@geography.gu.se Jerry Olsson, Associate professor, Responsible for Research Education, jerry.olsson@geography.gu.se Jonas Lindberg, Senior Lecturer, Head of Unit, jonas.lindberg@geography.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please attach the following in the application: Cover letter (max 1 page) with a short motivation why you are interested in doctoral education. CV (max 2 pages). Research plan (max 2,000 words, including references) based on the description of the research project CLOSE – Konsekvenser av lokala serviceetableringar . The plan will be assessed based on the applicant's ability to formulate a research problem and the applicant's familiarity with and handling of methodological and theoretical issues. The research plan must contain a proposal for a research problem and the following: Purpose, goals and research questions. Preliminary ideas about theory, research design, methods and data. Indicative timetable. Reference List; use APA. Certified copies of degree certificates and proof of specific entry requirements. Copies of cited essays, degree projects and other publications. Academic references, such as thesis supervisors. Applicants who complete their thesis after the application deadline can still apply. The selection committee may request additional information (for example, master's theses completed after the application deadline). Applications must be received by: 2026-10-21 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41639": {
      "title": "Associate Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Associate Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Associate Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/760 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. Are you interested in translational research and keen to learn advanced methods in mucosal biology? We offer an exciting opportunity to join the Mucin Biology Groups at the Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology, Institute of Biomedicine, Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. Our research groups form a strong, internationally recognized collaborative network focused on understanding the roles of mucins and mucus in protecting the epithelial surfaces of the body, and how alterations in these systems are linked to disease. The position is part of a collaborative project between the research groups of Malin Johansson and Thaher Pelaseyed. Read more about our research here: http://www.medkem.gu.se/mucinbiology/ Duties The intestine is constantly exposed to numerous harmful agents through food intake, from microorganisms, and from endogenous substances such as hydrochloric acid, digestive enzymes, and bile. Several defense mechanisms protect the epithelium, including a secreted mucus layer and an epithelial surface glycocalyx. Defects in these protective systems have been linked to infections, chronic inflammation, and cancer. In this Gates Foundation–funded project, we will investigate how mucin barriers are altered in Environmental Enteric Dysfunction (EED), a common condition associated with malnutrition in low- and middle-income countries and characterized by low-grade inflammation in the small intestine, reduced nutrient absorption, and a weakened epithelial barrier. The overall goal is to identify defects in mucin barriers that drive disease and, ultimately, to find biomarkers and strategies to prevent or treat disease. You will analyze small intestinal biopsies and mucosal brushings from patients with EED (Lusaka cohort, Zambia) and compare them with control material. Your duties will include: - Histochemical and immunohistochemical staining of proteins, glycans (lectins and glycan-specific antibodies) and RNA (RNAscope) on fixed, paraffin-embedded tissue sections - Efficient workflows using semi-automated microscopy, as well as advanced image analysis and quantification in, e.g., QuPath, ImageJ, and Imaris - In-depth analyses using high-resolution confocal and Airyscan microscopy, as well as cell and molecular biology methods - Quantitative morphometry of crypt–villus units and analysis of goblet cells, enterocytes and their interactions with immune cells - Preparation of brushing samples for verification by mass spectrometry-based proteomics, and complementary biochemical analyses - Documentation, data management, and presentation of results within the research group The work is performed with a high degree of independence. The research environment offers excellent opportunities for collaboration and knowledge exchange with experts across a broad spectrum of experimental techniques. Qualifications The candidate must hold a Master's degree in Pharmacy. We are seeking a candidate with documented experience in the methods described above. Experience with various histological techniques and with applying advanced microscopy to tissue samples is required. Particular weight will be placed on documented prior research experience in mucin biology and related protective mucosal barriers. You are engaged, meticulous, and responsible, with the ambition to independently develop and apply new methods. Candidate selection will be based on scientific and methodological qualifications as well as personal attributes. Employment The position is a full-time, fixed-term appointment for 6 months, based at the Institute of Biomedicine, Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology. Start date: November 1, 2026 or as per agreeement. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Associate Professor Thaher Pelaseyed. Phone: 031-786 3154. E-mail:thaher.pelaseyed@medkem.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-21 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41634": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in bacterial pathogenesis",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in bacterial pathogenesis For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in bacterial pathogenesis Reference number PAR 2026/757 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: * The Department of Infectious Diseases * The Department of Microbiology and Immunology * The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology * The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. We are currently seeking a candidate for a postdoctoral position in bacterial intrauterine infections in pregnancy. The project will explore the role of specific bacterial factors in breaching the protective cervical mucus plug (CMP) that forms during pregnancy. The candidate will join a newly established Wallenberg Centre for Molecular and Translational Medicine (WCMTM) at the University of Gothenburg ( www.wcmtm.gu.se ). As part of this venture, Dr. George Birchenough has established his research group ( www.birchenoughlab.org ) at the Department of Medical Biochemistry & Cell Biology at the University of Gothenburg. The group works towards a better understanding of how mucosal defence systems control our interactions with the environment, how those systems fail in disease and how they might be prophylactically or therapeutically targeted. The group is part of a large network of researchers at Gothenburg University, most importantly the Mucin Biology Groups cluster ( www.medkem.gu.se/mucinbiology ) that currently includes over 30 researchers working at the forefront of mucosal biology. The candidate will have the opportunity to collaborate with experts in multiple experimental methods that work within the cluster, as well as more widely with other local and national groups within the WCMTM network. Subject area Bacterial pathogenesis Subject area description Our mucosal surfaces are the interface between the environment and our tissues. Their physiological functions are critical, but necessitate constant exposure of living cells to a vast collection of microbial lifeforms that includes many opportunistic and obligate pathogens. As a result, our mucosa have evolved multiple defensive systems that allow them to endure in this environment, and failure of these systems is often linked to development of acute and chronic disease. Consequently, understanding the development and regulation of these systems is key to understanding the pathoaetiology of mucosal disease and how they can be prevented or treated. Approximately 15 million children are born preterm every year, resulting directly in up to 1 million annual deaths and long-term health consequences for survivors. Over half of preterm births are associated with ascension of vaginal microbes into the normally sterile intrauterine environment, and it is thought that inflammatory responses to these microbes are an important driver of early delivery. The CMP forms in the cervical canal early in pregnancy, and is thought to act as a barrier to microbial ascension. Some specific bacterial species are able to transverse this barrier and colonise the intrauterine environment, and we hypothesise that this drives the maternal inflammatory responses that can initiate preterm birth. It is currently not known which specific factors allow these bacteria to breach the CMP, but these unknown factors may represent promising anti-infective targets reducing intrauterine infection and preterm birth. The project will therefore employ high throughput mutagenic screening with a bacterial intrauterine infection model and different mouse strains to identify specific bacterial factors that allow breach of the CMP. Duties The successful candidate will be expected to work extensively with bacteria, genetic manipulation and pre-clinical mouse models. The candidate will be encouraged and expected to conduct their experimental research in an independent manner and to make significant contributions to project planning. The candidate will also actively contribute to the local research environment at the Mucin Biology Groups and engage in interactions with collaborators and other groups belonging to the WCMTM network. If necessary, the candidate may also be asked to assist in the supervision of Masters or PhD students. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Applications will be judged primarily on evidence of relevant scientific skills. The applicant must provide evidence of a PhD in a relevant area of research and have demonstrable skills in both written and spoken English. Previous experience working with animal bacterial infection models is essential. Any previous experience in microbial genetics, microscopy, mass spectrometry, organoid culture, flow cytometry, bioinformatics or in vivo disease modelling will be considered as merits in support of an application. Eligible applicants will be asked to partake in an interview and present their previous research. Great emphasis will be placed on the candidate’s personal suitability for the position and clear interest in the subject area. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Selection process How to apply for a position | University of Gothenburg Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact George Birchenough, senior lecturer, email: george.birchenough@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: * A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying * A CV including a list of publications * Proof of completed PhD Applications must be received by: 2026-10-21 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41629": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in genomics and bioinformatics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in genomics and bioinformatics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in genomics and bioinformatics Reference number PAR 2026/756 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. At the Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology, we are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral fellow with a background in bioinformatics and human genomics. Subject area Genomics and bioinformatics Subject area description Clonal hematopoiesis, characterized by acquired genetic variants in blood cells of otherwise healthy individuals, is associated with increased risk of blood cancer and several age-related diseases. Recurrent mutations in clonal hematopoiesis demonstrate sex-specific prevalence; however, the underlying mechanisms and their implications for disease outcomes remain poorly understood. This project aims to identify and characterize the sex-specific determinants and consequences of clonal hematopoiesis. Duties The candidate will analyze large-scale genomic, sequencing, and epidemiological datasets to investigate sex-specific genetic and non-genetic determinants of clonal hematopoiesis. The position offers the opportunity to independently lead the project, collaborate with team members, and external collaborators, and shape its scientific direction. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria The candidate must hold a PhD in genetics, genomics, bioinformatics, computational biology or a related subject area. Experience in human genomics and a good understanding of cancer genomics are required. The candidate should have proficiency in R, python, or comparable programming languages. Experience of working with long-read sequencing data is a plus. The candidate should have the ability to work independently as well as work effectively within collaborative teams. Fluency in both spoken and written English is essential. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Selection process How to apply for a position | University of Gothenburg Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Abhishek Niroula, associate senior lecturer, email: abhishek.niroula@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying A CV including a list of publications Proof of completed PhD Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026-10-21 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41626": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science - Multimodal profiling of cellular function",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science - Multimodal profiling of cellular function For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science - Multimodal profiling of cellular function Reference number PAR 2026/741 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical Science Project title: Multimodal profiling of cellular function and molecular states in engineered cellular systems and human tissues The Department of medical chemistry and cell biology is located at the Institute of Biomedicine  at the Faculty of  Biomedicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Recent advances in stem-cell engineering, functional imaging and spatial molecular profiling provide new opportunities to study human tissues at cellular resolution. However, integrating these complementary technologies remains technically challenging because functional measurements, spatial molecular profiling and downstream molecular analyses are typically performed on separate samples. The aim of this PhD project is to develop and apply new methods for integrated multimodal analysis of cellular function and molecular states, with a focus on pancreatic islets and stem-cell-derived islets. The doctoral student will combine electrophysiological measurements using high-density microelectrode arrays (HD-MEAs), fluorescence imaging and biosensors with spatial transcriptomics, metabolomics and other molecular profiling approaches. The project will also have a significant computational component and will include advanced deep learning methods for image analysis and genomic data integration to identify molecular programs associated with different functional cell states. Experience in experimental cell biology, programming, biophysics, image analysis, genomics or data analysis is considered meritorious. The project will be conducted in a multidisciplinary research environment with collaborations in physics, pancreatic biology, spatial omics and advanced imaging. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Biomedicine, Department of medical chemistry and cell biology. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Biomedicine require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Joan Camunas-Soler , Institute of Biomedicine, e-mail: joan.camunas@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-10-21 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41515": {
      "title": "Associate Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Associate Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Associate Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/714 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences is one of six Institutes within Sahlgrenska Academy at the University of Gothenburg. We conduct research and education that puts people's health and quality of life in focus. We represent 16 different research areas and are divided into four sections.  Through a fruitful collaboration with the healthcare and Sahlgrenska University Hospital, we have developed joint projects and meeting places that benefits both health care and research. The Division of Upper Abdominal Surgery conducts research in translational and experimental medicine, with a particular focus on the mechanisms underlying cancer and the development of relevant model systems to understand disease processes at the cellular and molecular levels—specifically regarding pancreatic cancer. The division’s activities are characterized by interdisciplinary collaboration between clinical and preclinical research. The pancreatic cancer research group works with human-derived cancer cells to model the disease; this includes the use of cell lines as well as patient-derived organoids from pancreatic cancer and neoplastic lesions in vitro. The aim is to develop and apply advanced cell models to study disease mechanisms—with a particular focus on the tumor microenvironment and the immune system—and to establish a foundation for future drug testing. Duties The duties involve laboratory work within a research project focused on patient-derived organoids and cell-based modeling of pancreatic cancer. Responsibilities include the culture, handling, and experimental processing of human-derived cells from pancreatic tumors, as well as the application of molecular and cell biology methods relevant to the project. Additionally, the role entails documenting experiments, ensuring quality control of laboratory processes, compiling results, and actively participating in method development. The work is conducted in close collaboration with the research group and requires the ability to work both independently and systematically in a laboratory setting. Qualifications The position requires a degree in molecular biology, cell biology, biomedicine, or another field deemed equivalent by the employer. The applicant must have documented experience in laboratory work and strong proficiency in spoken and written English. Previous experience working with cell models and cell cultures—specifically involving pancreatic neoplasms—is considered an asset, as is experience with bioinformatics and relevant molecular biology methods applied to pancreatic cancer. Experience working in a laboratory setting at Sahlgrenska University Hospital or a similar academic research environment is also meritorious. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. The applicant should be meticulous and responsible, possess good collaborative skills, and demonstrate a documented interest in translational, laboratory-based research. Employment The position is a full-time, fixed-term appointment for 6 (six) months, based at the Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of Surgery. Start date: January 1, 2027. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professor and Senior Consultant Kjetil Soreide, kjetil.soreide@gu.se , phone +46 (0)766 18 25 08 or lab-manager Johan Bourghart Fagman,  johan.bourghardt@gu.se, phone +46 (0)706238876. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application must include: CV and, if applicable, a list of publications Cover letter (statement of motivation, max. one A4 page) Degree certificates and relevant credentials Contact details for at least two references In the application, the applicant should specifically describe previous experience with cell models, molecular biology methods, cell culture, and other laboratory work relevant to the position. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-20 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41619": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Neurochemistry",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Neurochemistry For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Neurochemistry Reference number PAR 2026/743 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for a postdoctoral researcher employment at the Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. Within the department, the Clinical Neurochemistry group conducts extensive research on biomarkers for brain diseases, particularly Alzheimer's disease. A large amount of data on fluid biomarkers has been generated in multiple international collaborative projects, and we are now advertising a postdoctoral position for a researcher with expertise in statistics and bioinformatics to analyze these data. Subject area Neurochemistry Subject area description Neurochemistry is a branch of chemistry that studies the chemical structure, molecular mechanisms, and function of the nervous system, the brain, and its constituents. Within clinical neurochemistry, biomarker research for neurodegenerative diseases has become an important and expansive field of great clinical relevance. Job assignments Process existing biomarker data in international collaborative projects, compile graphs and presentation materials, participate in group meetings to guide team members in their projects regarding statistical aspects, write scientific reports and articles, train team members in statistics and bioinformatics, and apply for grants. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. To qualify as a postdoctoral researcher for this employment, the applicant should hold a PhD in biomarker research, statistics or a related topic. Assessment Extensive knowledge of statistics and bioinformatics, as well as biomarkers in neurodegenerative diseases, documented in scientific publications in reputable peer-reviewed journals. Great importance is also attached to personal qualities, collaborative skills, and an interest in education and supervision. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 2 years, 100% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information If you have questions regarding the employment please contact Manager in charge Professor/Head of Department Henrik Zetterberg, phone: +46 768 672 647, e-mail: henrik.zetterberg@clinchem.gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact person. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please enclose PhD certificate with the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-10-19 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41577": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science - PhD position in neuro biomarker research",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science -  PhD position in neuro biomarker research For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science -  PhD position in neuro biomarker research Reference number PAR 2026/730 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical science Project title: Validation of a realistic screening approach for early Alzheimer's disease The Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry is located at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Alzheimer's disease (AD) places an enormous burden on patients, families and society. Newly approved anti-amyloid antibodies are most effective when given early, ideally before symptoms appear. Yet most patients are diagnosed in primary care, which is not equipped for early detection. Remote digital cognitive tests and blood-based biomarkers (BBMs) offer scalable, cost-effective solutions but have not been validated in the general population within existing healthcare. REAL AD is a prospective study run together with the entire public primary care of Västra Götaland Region (VGR; Närhälsan, 111 health centres), Sahlgrenska University Hospital's memory clinic and the region's innovation platform. It validates a screening method for early AD combining remote cognitive testing and BBMs. Dementia-free adults aged 50–80 complete app- or web-based tests (neotiv, Cognitron) at baseline, 18 and 36 months and give blood at their local health centre. A sub-cohort (N=425) is examined twice with CSF, MRI, optional tau-PET and clinical and neuropsychological assessments. The PhD project will, in collaboration with other lab members and research groups, (I) explore the cross-sectional and longitudinal associations of BBMs for AD pathology, including multi-protein panels, with digital and remotely administered cognitive assessments and register-based data on sociodemographic and lifestyle factors; (II) establish and validate meaningful threshold for cognitive tests and BBMs against reference methods such as CSF proteomics; (III) support the data-driven implementation of relevant biomarker modalities in clinical routine. Recruitment closed in February 2025 with nearly 5,000 participants, 3,095 of whom gave blood. Higher plasma p-tau217 was associated with poorer cognitive performance, supporting the feasibility of combined screening. The 18-month follow-up shows over 90% retention, and the validation phase began in February 2026. REAL AD provides implementation-ready knowledge on how precision medicine can enter routine care to improve the prognosis of AD. The study is supported by leading industry partners and has been highlighted as European best practice. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology  Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Neuroscience and Physiology require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Michael Schöll , Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, e-mail: michael.scholl@neuro.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-10-16 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41574": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science -  Bacteriophages and cardiovascular disease",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science -  Bacteriophages and cardiovascular disease For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science -  Bacteriophages and cardiovascular disease Reference number PAR 2026/681 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical Science Project title: The role of gut mucosal phages in cardiometabolic disease The Department of Molecular and Clinical Medicine is located at the Institute of Medicine  at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Phages - viruses that infect bacteria - are the most common biological entity on our planet, as well as in the human body. Still, the potential role of phages in health and disease is insufficiently understood. The gut is the largest reservoir of both bacteria and phages. However, while bacterial density is highest in the luminal contents, phages are instead enriched in the mucus that covers and protects the epithelial cells. This is because the phages bind to glycans (sugar molecules) on mucin glycoproteins. When fiber intake is low, bacteria in the large intestine may turn to these glycans as an alternate source of nutrition, liberating the attached phages. Due to their small size, these phages could easily cross epithelial and endothelial barriers into the intestinal tissue and circulation. Certain phages have been reported to trigger inappropriate antiviral immune responses that might contribute to chronic inflammation and cardiometabolic diseases. In this project, the doctoral student will investigate if disruption of the triangular relationship of phages, bacteria and gut mucosal cells partly explains the association of low fiber intake and cardiometabolic diseases. First, we will study how the composition of phages, their translocation across the gut barrier and antiviral immune responses differ in colon mucosa with normal vs. impaired mucus protection. Second, we will determine the effects of a fiber-based, dietary intervention designed to prevent excessive mucus degradation. Finally, we will develop a model to predict disruption of the gut mucosal barrier. This model will be evaluated in non-invasive samples from individuals with cardiometabolic diseases, such as fatty liver disease and atherosclerosis, and healthy controls persons. The project will combine clinical cohorts, molecular biology techniques and advanced data analysis, providing a solid foundation in translational medical science. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Medicine, Department of Molecular and Clinical Medicine. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Medicine require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Karolina Sjöberg Jabbar , Institute of Medicine, e-mail: karolina.sjoberg@wlab.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-10-16 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41590": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Arabic, fixed-term position",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Senior Lecturer in Arabic, fixed-term position For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Senior Lecturer in Arabic, fixed-term position Reference number PAR 2026/670 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Languages and Literatures (Institutionen för språk och litteraturer) is the largest department within the Faculty of Humanities, with 16 different languages, approximately 90 employees, approximately 1,090 full-time equivalent students and an annual turnover of more than SEK 110 million. The Department’s research addresses the languages and literatures of the world through broad contextualisation and on the basis of a wide range of theoretical approaches and specific applications. The Department is part of the Faculty of Humanities and is located in modern premises in central Gothenburg, close to Götaplatsen. For more information about the Department, please visit Department of Languages and Literatures, University of Gothenburg Position Senior Lecturer Subject Arabic Subject description Courses in Arabic are offered as standalone courses at all levels: first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral level. The first-cycle courses comprise four semesters, of which semester 1 is intended for beginners. During semesters 1 and 2, courses in Modern Standard Arabic and spoken Arabic dialect are taught in parallel, with the former focusing on reading and writing skills and the latter on speaking and listening comprehension. These skills are further developed during semester 3. During semester 4, students complete an independent project for a Bachelor’s degree and deepen their theoretical knowledge on a research-based foundation. The subject offers both campus-based and online courses. It also offers courses for mother-tongue teachers of Arabic, focusing on teaching and learning, multilingualism and culture. Research in Arabic is conducted in linguistics, literary studies and cultural history. Research in Arabic also forms part of the Department’s interdisciplinary research areas. Duties The duties include teaching Arabic language, literature and language proficiency at first-cycle level, beginning with the introductory course for beginners. The duties also include designing, assessing and marking examinations. The introductory course focuses on, but is not limited to, communicative skills. Teaching is primarily conducted during daytime on campus, but to some extent also online and in the evenings. Teaching on courses for mother-tongue teachers of Arabic, as well as on interdisciplinary courses and programmes, may also be required. Teaching is conducted in English or Swedish, as well as in Arabic. Most of the work is to be carried out on site at the Department, and the successful candidate is expected to participate actively and constructively in the work of the teaching team and in the Department’s development and work environment activities, including staff meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. The position may also include other duties, such as administrative tasks and development work. Work within the subject is carried out by a teaching team, and the successful candidate is expected to contribute constructively and in a collegial spirit to both the subject and the Department as a whole. Qualifications Qualifications for appointment as teaching staff are regulated by Chapter 4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance (Högskoleförordningen) and the University of Gothenburg’s Appointments Procedure. To be qualified for appointment as Senior Lecturer in Arabic, the applicant must have demonstrated teaching expertise and have been awarded a doctoral degree in Arabic or another subject considered relevant by the recruiting Department, or possess equivalent research expertise. Good proficiency in English and Arabic and the ability to teach in both languages are required from the start of the appointment. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as teaching staff are regulated by Chapter 4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance (Högskoleförordningen) and the University of Gothenburg’s Appointments Procedure. In the selection process, primary consideration will be given to documented qualifications in the following areas: Teaching expertise in campus-based and online teaching of Arabic Experience of supervision and examination in Arabic A good ability to teach Arabic in English and Arabic Research qualifications in Arabic, which will be assessed with particular regard to their degree of originality and international relevance Secondary consideration will be given to the following qualifications: Management and administration of teaching, e.g. responsibility for a course Training in teaching and learning in higher education Established links and active participation in national and international networks Collaboration with wider society Good proficiency in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language) and the ability to teach in that language are considered a strong merit. Additional merits include experience of teaching an Arabic dialect and native or near-native proficiency in Arabic. The ability to teach in Arabic and English will be assessed through an interview that includes a brief pedagogical reflection. References may also form part of the assessment. During the interview, particular consideration will be given to how well the applicant presents their reflection in English and participates in an interview conducted in English or Swedish (or another Scandinavian language), with elements in Arabic. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and is recognised as having the best qualifications for carrying out the duties included in the position. Suitability for the position will therefore also be taken into account in the overall assessment, particularly professional maturity, ability to collaborate and communicate, sense of responsibility, and the ability to contribute constructively to the organisation and to a positive work environment. Employment Number of positions: One or more Type of employment: Fixed-term employment until 30 June 2027 Extent: 100% Location: Department of Languages and Literatures Start date: 11 January 2027, or as agreed Additional information about the position Applications are submitted through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. For instructions on applications for teaching positions, applicants are referred to the Faculty of Humanities’ guidelines: Application for position | University of Gothenburg The following documents must be included with the application: Cover letter List of qualifications and employment history (CV) Description of research and teaching activities, including reflections on research and teaching Qualifications Portfolio I: Research qualifications Qualifications Portfolio II: Teaching qualifications Qualifications Portfolio III: Qualifications relating to collaboration with wider society Qualifications Portfolio IV: Management and administrative qualifications Publications submitted for consideration (maximum 5), together with a separate list of these publications Doctoral degree certificate Doctoral thesis References: names and telephone numbers of two referees Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete by the application deadline. An incomplete application will be assessed on the basis of the documents submitted. Contact information For further information about the position, please contact Director of Studies Magnus P. Ängsal at magnus.pettersson.angsal@sprak.gu.se . Questions concerning the appointment procedure, or concerning research publications, written works or similar materials that cannot be attached to the application electronically, may be sent to personalhandlaggare@sprak.gu.se . Trade unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here: How to apply for a position | University of Gothenburg Application Applications are submitted through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and received by the University no later than the application deadline. Application deadline: 22 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41562": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/732 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences is one of six Institutes within Sahlgrenska Academy at the University of Gothenburg. We conduct research and education that puts people's health and quality of life in focus. We represent 16 different research areas and are divided into four sections.  Through a fruitful collaboration with the healthcare and Sahlgrenska University Hospital, we have developed joint projects and meeting places that benefits both health care and research. The Department of Plastic Surgery conducts clinical research in the clinical areas that the plastic surgery specialty handles. Typical research areas are craniofacial surgery, surgery for cleft lip and palate, breast reconstruction, microsurgery, gender confirmation surgery and surgery for excess skin. A smaller part includes experimental research where the goal is to produce tissues such as fat, cartilage and skin for plastic surgical reconstructions. The research group that is now looking for an employee works with 3D bioprinting of adipose tissue. For a decade, they have developed techniques in several different animal models and a main focus is vascularization of 3D bioprinted implants. Duties The position includes working with 3D bioprinting of adipose tissue, skin and cartilage. The printed tissues will be implanted either in animal models (mouse, rat, pig) or in humans. Furthermore, bioprinting in the operating room will be done under sterile conditions. The work includes planning and carrying out experiments and method development independently and in collaboration with research leaders. Qualifications For the position, the applicant must have a doctorate and a master's degree in biotechnology. Preference will be given to those who have knowledge and skills that are directly applicable to the project. One must be able to perform preparation of bioink, preparation of adipose tissue from the operating room, bioprinting of fat, cartilage and skin, cell culture, nanoindentation, immunohistochemistry. Knowledge in animal experiments (mouse, rat, pig), FACS, TOF-SIMS is an advantage. Employment The position is a full-time, fixed-term appointment for 10 (ten) months, based at the Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of Plastic Surgery. Start date: 2026-12-01 Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professor Lars Kölby; e-mail: lars.kolby@surgery.gu.se or phone: +46 70 645 11 55. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. A doctoral degree (PhD) in Medicine is required. The degree must have been awarded before the application deadline, and a copy of the diploma must be attached to the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-11 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41566": {
      "title": "Researcher in Climate-Related Migration",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher in Climate-Related Migration – CLIMIG For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher in Climate-Related Migration – CLIMIG Reference number PAR 2026/725 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the School of Global Studies, we conduct research and provide education that equip students with the ability to understand and analyze social phenomena in a globalized world. Our programs are characterized by their timeliness and societal relevance, and our research aims to make the world more comprehensible through our unique interdisciplinary research environment. Our research areas include environmental social science, peace and development studies, human rights, and social anthropology. We offer programs at the undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral levels, as well as standalone courses. The School of Global Studies is seeking a full-time researcher for a 12-month position in the CLIMIG research program—A New Interdisciplinary Framework for Studying the Relationship Between Climate Change and Migration. About the Project CLIMIG is an interdisciplinary research program that examines how climate change interacts with social, cultural, economic, political, and ecological factors that influence migration. The program focuses on three mountainous regions that are particularly vulnerable to climate change: the Ethiopian Highlands, the Andes in Peru, and the Himalayas in Nepal. By combining qualitative and quantitative methods and integrating climate, migration, and socioeconomic data, the project aims to develop a new analytical framework for the study of climate-related migration. More information about the project is available on the CLIMIG website: https://www.gu.se/forskning/climig-ett-nytt-tvarvetenskapligt-ramverk-for-att-studera-relationen-mellan-klimatforandringar-och-migration Job Responsibilities As a researcher at CLIMIG, you will primarily work with the analysis of quantitative data on migration, climate change, and related socioeconomic conditions, as well as qualitative data, from the project’s research sites in Peru, Ethiopia, and Nepal. The work involves analyzing and integrating survey data, spatial data (including remote sensing data), and interview data, as well as publishing the project’s findings in scientific journals. The duties may also include combining quantitative and qualitative research findings, as well as organizing and participating in workshops where research findings are shared with relevant stakeholders. The work is carried out in close collaboration with other researchers and international partners within CLIMIG. Qualifications This position requires a Ph.D. in human geography, development studies, migration studies, social anthropology, or another field that the employer deems relevant to the project. You must have documented experience in research on climate-related migration or related issues, as well as strong expertise in quantitative social science methods and statistical analysis, and in qualitative data collection and analysis. Experience conducting research in the Andes, Ethiopia, and/or Nepal is a plus. The following are particularly advantageous: experience leading research and fieldwork teams, particularly in the Global South experience with geographic information systems (GIS), such as QGIS or ArcGIS, for the analysis and presentation of spatial data experience combining survey data with remote sensing data or other types of spatial data expertise and experience in combining quantitative and qualitative methods in data collection and analysis, particularly in the Global South experience organizing or facilitating workshops to disseminate research findings to relevant stakeholders. Experience leading and managing international research networks Great emphasis will be placed on the ability to work independently while also collaborating effectively within an interdisciplinary and international research team. You must have strong organizational skills as well as excellent communication and academic writing skills in English. Employment This is a special fixed-term position for 12 months, full-time (100 percent), based at the School of Global Studies, University of Gothenburg. Start date: January 1, 2027. Contact Information Anders Burman, Project Manager 031-786 59 19, anders.burman@globalstudies.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR Elvedina Sikiric Lidström at elvedina.sikiric.lidstrom@gu.se or by phone at 031-786 40 53. Unions You can find labor union representatives at the University of Gothenburg here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you must create an account in our recruitment system. You apply for the position via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. As an applicant, you are responsible for ensuring that your application is complete in accordance with the job posting and that it is received by the university no later than the application deadline. Your application must include: a cover letter describing your interest in and suitability for the position a resume/CV including your education, relevant research experience, and scientific publications degree certificates the names and contact information of two references. Application deadline: October 23, 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41403": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology Reference number PAR 2026/591 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology at the University of Gothenburg conducts high-quality research and education in chemical sciences and molecular life sciences. Our research and education focus on a fundamental understanding of chemical and biological processes, ranging from the molecular and cellular level to human health and the environment. The Department provides an international and interdisciplinary research environment, bringing together expertise in chemistry, molecular biology and related areas. Research spans a broad range of fields , including organic and medicinal chemistry, analytical and physical chemistry, biochemistry, molecular and cell biology, structural biology and biomolecular interactions. The Department is also responsible for education at Bachelor's, Master's and doctoral levels and contributes to several educational programmes within the Faculty of Science and Technology. The Department has approximately 200 employees and is located in Natrium at Campus Medicinareberget, providing a modern research and teaching environment with access to advanced research infrastructure and close proximity to other research environments within the Faculty of Science and Technology, and the Sahlgrenska Academy. We are now looking for an Assistant Professor with tenure track in Chemistry, specializing in Chemical Glycobiology. Following a positive evaluation, the department is responsible for offering long-term employment in a faculty position. The recruitment will strengthen the interface between chemistry and molecular life sciences at the department and contribute to the further development of research and education in molecular and chemical approaches to biological systems. The position comes with a generous package with full financial support for the Assistant Professor’s own salary, plus two to three additional recruitments (PhD student or postdoc) and running costs. Subject area Chemistry with a specializing in chemical glycobiology Subject area description Chemical glycobiology concerns the development and application of chemical approaches to understand the structures, dynamics and biological functions of glycans and glycoconjugates. The field integrates synthetic and analytical chemistry with chemical biology and molecular and cell biology to investigate how glycosylation influences molecular recognition, signalling, membrane organisation and biological function in health and disease. Relevant approaches include carbohydrate and bioconjugation chemistry, metabolic and bioorthogonal labelling, chemical probe design, glycan editing, and mass-spectrometry-based glycomics and glycoproteomics. By enabling the identification, characterisation and manipulation of glycans and glycoconjugates in complex biological systems, chemical glycobiology provides a molecular link between chemistry, glycobiology and membrane biology. At the Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology, the subject will complement and connect existing strengths in medicinal and analytical chemistry, biochemistry, glycobiology, proteomics and membrane biology. Available department infrastructure includes advanced microscopes, facilities for culturing mammalian cells and microbes, high throughput/high content phenotypic cell-based screening, equipment for purification of small molecules and macromolecules, NMR, lasers, electron microscopy, and imaging mass spectrometry. An important opportunity is to integrate experimental glycoscience with computational and AI-based approaches for predicting and interpreting molecular structure, interactions and function, in interaction with existing research groups in the department. The position is associated with the government-funded strategic research area CEMERAI (Centre for Modelling Life at the Membrane with AI), coordinated by Stockholm University, bringing together world-leading expertise in experimental and computational approaches to understand cellular membranes and their molecular components. Within this environment, chemical glycobiology provides experimental approaches and quantitative molecular information needed to understand the role of glycans and glycosylation in membrane organisation and function. A central aim is to develop and implement chemical tools to enable mechanistic studies of glycans and glycosylation in biological systems. Duties The main purpose of the position is to enable the successful candidate to establish an independent and internationally competitive research profile in chemical glycobiology and to develop the scientific and pedagogical qualifications required for promotion to Senior Lecturer within five years. The position primarily involves research, including: Establishing and developing an independent research profile in chemical glycobiology that complements and strengthens existing research at the department. Developing chemical and analytical approaches for studying glycans and glycoconjugates in biological systems. Scientific publication and active participation in national and international research collaborations. Applying for and obtaining external research funding. Supervision of students at undergraduate, master's and doctoral levels. Participation in interdisciplinary collaborations within CEMERAI and other relevant research environments at the University of Gothenburg and beyond. The position also includes teaching, course development and examination within the Department's educational programmes comprising chemistry, molecular biology and pharmaceutical programmes. The successful candidate is expected to contribute to undergraduate and advanced-level teaching in chemistry according to the needs of the department. This may include teaching in biochemistry, organic, and medicinal chemistry and related areas, as well as supervision of bachelor's and master's degree projects. Contributions to development of master’s level courses in chemical biology and related areas will be welcomed by the department. The successful candidate is also expected to participate in and contribute to the development of doctoral-level education. Teaching will initially comprise approximately 20 % of full-time employment. The distribution between research, teaching and other duties may develop during the period of employment in accordance with the needs of the department and the career development of the successful candidate. The position further includes research administration and project management, departmental service, societal collaboration and interaction, and participation in relevant research networks and research infrastructures. Competence development is included in the position, particularly training in teaching and learning in higher education and supervision corresponding to 20 higher education credits (PIL101, PIL102, PIL103 and PIL201, or equivalent). For candidates who do not speak Swedish, competence development in Swedish is also included. The University of Gothenburg will provide Swedish language courses, which are mandatory within the framework of the employment. The holder of the position is expected to develop independence as a researcher and to gain merits, both scientifically and pedagogically, to meet the eligibility requirements for a position as senior lecturer, prior to which the application for a docentship (‘docentur’ in Swedish) is a milestone. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. More specifically, the position requires: A doctoral degree in chemistry or a closely related subject, or equivalent scientific competence, relevant to the subject area of chemical glycobiology. Documented scientific competence relevant to chemical glycobiology or a closely related research area. Postdoctoral experience, preferably at least two years, at a university, college, research institute or equivalent. Very good ability to communicate in English, both orally and in writing. The applicant must demonstrate the personal qualities required to carry out the duties of the position, collaborate with colleagues and contribute positively to the research, educational and working environment of the Department. For candidates who do not speak Swedish, development of written and oral proficiency in Swedish (proficiency level B2 according to CEFR, Common European Framework of Reference for Languages) is required at least six months before the time of promotion to Senior lecturer in order to enable participation in teaching and other departmental activities conducted in Swedish. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are looking for candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The faculty for natural science and technology applies the below criteria in assessment of scientific and pedagogical skills, and general suitability. Scientific and pedagogical competence shall be sufficient relative to subject content and work tasks. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Chemistry and Molecular biology Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. T he assessment criteria for promotion as a teacher are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers. (GU 2025/1411) . The requirements for promotion to senior lecturer can be found in the procedures of the Faculty of Science and Technology: https://s3.gu.se/earkiv-publikt/GU1816-1319333663-232803.pdf Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/employment-benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. For candidates who do not speak Swedish, a development of proficiency in oral and written Swedish to level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) is expected, at least six months before promotion to Senior Lecturer, in order to enable participation in education and other departmental tasks that are performed in Swedish. The University of Gothenburg offers courses in Swedish that are compulsory within the framework of the employment. Personnel recruited internationally will be offered a relocation package and an advisory meeting about taxes / Global Mobility. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews will take place Tuesday , December 15, 2026 . Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact assistant Head of Department, professor Per Sunnerhagen, phone: +46705294180 e-mail: per.sunnerhagen@cmb.gu.se. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact hr@cmb.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The selection of candidates is made based on the qualifications registered in the application. Together with your application, the following should be attached: Cover letter CV, including a complete list of publications and a record of research grants received (with amounts). Description of scientific expertise and a list of ten selected scientific publications and why you chose to refer to each of them in your application. A research plan with a description of current and planned research as well as other planned activities relevant to this position (maximum five pages). Description of educational expertise, including information about the extent and level of your teaching experience and evaluations of the quality of your teaching. The description should also include a reflection on your educational approach and teaching philosophy. Description of experience of administrative duties such as collaborative projects and/or leadership or management positions. Copies of your most relevant publications in full format (maximum ten publications). Doctoral certificate and other certificates and documents to be considered. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be clearly marked with the position’s reference number PAR 2026/591 and sent in copies to: University of Gothenburg Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology, HR Box 462 405 30 Göteborg Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity br"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41384": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in basic medical research",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in basic medical research For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in basic medical research Reference number PAR 2026/675 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. Sahlgrenska Academy is the medical faculty at the University of Gothenburg and a leading centre for research and education in medicine, odontology and health sciences. Research, healthcare and innovation come together in a thriving life science ecosystem, supported by close collaboration with Sahlgrenska University Hospital, advanced research infrastructure, and a strong life science and biotechnology sector. Learn more about Sahlgrenska Academy . As part of the University's initiative, Sahlgrenska Academy is recruiting an Assistant Professor within the framework of the Academy's long-term efforts to strengthen life science research and research environments, spanning basic science, translational research and clinical application – as well as education. The position will be based at whichever of the Academy's six departments offers the best environment for the successful candidate's research to develop. Call for 2 assistant professor positions Subject area Basic medical research Subject area description The subject area encompasses basic medical research into the molecular, cellular and physiological mechanisms underlying health and disease. It may also include, to a greater or lesser extent, a translational component bridging basic medical research and clinical application, with the aim of developing new approaches to diagnosis, prevention and treatment. Duties The position as Assistant Professor in basic medical research primarily involves conducting independent, internationally competitive research within the subject area of the position. The research should contribute to an increased understanding of the molecular, cellular and physiological mechanisms underlying health and disease. It may encompass both basic and translational research, with the aim of creating a foundation for the development of new approaches to diagnosis, prevention and treatment. The successful candidate is expected to actively develop an independent line of research, seek external research funding and contribute to the development of a strong research environment. Duties also include collaboration with researchers within the department, Sahlgrenska Academy, the healthcare sector, and national and international networks. The position primarily involves research, but also includes teaching at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. Duties may include lectures, seminars, and the supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. The successful candidate is expected to develop their teaching expertise and gradually take on greater responsibility for the planning, delivery and development of teaching within the subject area. A successful candidate who does not have formal training in academic teaching must undertake such training within one year of taking up the position. For promotion, the candidate must have attained proficiency in spoken and written Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) no later than six months before the end of the appointment as Assistant Professor. The University of Gothenburg offers Swedish-language courses, which the successful candidate is required to attend  as part of the appointment. All other requirements for promotion set by Sahlgrenska Academy must also be met. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in the subject area of the position or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Pedagogical competence must be well documented in a manner that allows the quality of the educational activities to be assessed. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Good collaborative skills, both within and beyond the candidate's own research environment, are also highly valued. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer stated by Sahlgrenska Academy. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Jenny Nyström, Professor and Dean at Sahlgrenska Academy jenny.nystrom@neuro.gu.se Johan Wessberg, Professor and Chairman of the Academic Appointments Board at Sahlgrenska Academy johan.wessberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applications must follow the layout of the recommendations for the Guidelines for application to teaching position . Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Please note in particular that the following documents (in swedish or english) must accompany the application: Doctoral degree (If the degree certificate has been translated into English, the certificate in the original language must also be enclosed.) Research plan/Research programme A maximum of 10 selected scientific publications that the applicant want to refer to (in full text) Other certificates /proof /rating proving applicant's qualifications Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg The Sahlgrenska Academy Box 400 405 30 Göteborg SWEDEN The reference number of the application, PAR 2026/675, should be clearly stated when mailing complementary documents. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 In the event of any disagreement between the English and the Swedish versions of any announcement, the Swedish version takes precedence. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41341": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Education",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Education For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Education Reference number PAR 2026/649 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in education, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas—subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in education will be based at the Department of Education, Communication and Learning. Subject area Education Subject area description Education is a subject focusing on education, teaching, upbringing, socialisation, formation and learning. The subject in Education has a special interest in the relationship between the individual and the society, and the conditions under which institutional conditions act and have acted over time. Educational processes and outcomes are studied within contexts of structure and agency, and their interrelations, within as well as outside formal education. Duties At the Department of Education, Communication and Learning we offer prominent education programs and outstanding research in our areas of knowledge - Education, Communication and Learning. Various societal issues that follow from globalization, sustainability and digitalisation are our focus. Internationally, our research contributes to issues concerning the conditions of vulnerable children and young people, the preschool's mission and quality, migration and other societal changes, violence and racism in school and during leisure-time and the consequences of digitalisation for education and working life. A position as assistant professor is intended to enable the successful candidate to develop their teaching and research expertise for further academic career progression. The position therefore comprises 50% research and 50% teaching. The research is carried out in one of the department’s research environments and in collaboration with senior researchers at the Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools and will form part of the research programme to be carried out within the strategic research area Excellence in School . The majority of the teaching responsibilities are within the Preschool Teacher Education Programme, in the field of science, technology, mathematics, and sustainable development. Teaching and supervision in other programmes, courses, or continuing professional development may also be required. The role also includes course leadership, course development, and collaboration. Teaching at the department is conducted primarily on campus and in Swedish, although some teaching in English also takes place. Good oral and written proficiency in English and in Swedish or another Scandinavian language is therefore required. Staff are also expected to be present on campus to a significant extent, thereby contributing to a stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in education, or another relevant field of doctoral studies, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networks For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities (and suitability) required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Education, Communication and Learning Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Head of Department, Markus Nivala, markus.nivala@gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 4574 If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Administrative Manager, Kristin Martinsson, kristin.martinsson@gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 2344 Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other written works that cannot be submitted digitally with the application must be sent in duplicate to: University of Gothenburg Department of Education, Communication and Learning Kristin Martinsson Box 300 40530 Gothenburg Sweden Please mark the envelope with the reference number for the position: PAR 2026/649. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitm"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41414": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation Reference number PAR 2026/679 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and  Academy of Music and Drama. As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. The Academy of Music and Drama offers education in music and the performing arts and conducts research in artistic and educational practices. The Academy provides an environment where artistic practice, education and research intersect, and where collaboration takes place across genres, disciplines and professional fields. We are now seeking an Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation, specialising in improvisation and/or composition. You will join the Music Unit, where improvisational, compositional and technology-based practices come together in education and artistic practice. The Academy has a well-established environment for research and doctoral education in Music Performance and Creation, with projects in improvisation, composition, interpretation, sound and media, as well as collaborations across artistic fields and academic disciplines. The Academy is also home to the Lindblad Studio, an experimental laboratory for sound, media, music technology and computer-assisted composition. More about the Academy of Music and Drama: https://www.gu.se/en/music-drama Subject area Musical Performance and Interpretation Subject area description Musical Performance and Interpretation encompasses artistic research in which musical practice is an integral and indispensable part of knowledge creation. Research in the subject area explores questions relating to the creation of music and sound, musical practice, artistic processes, performance, listening, materiality, technology, notation, embodiment, interpretation, musical interaction, and the relationship between music and its social and cultural contexts. For this position, the focus is on improvisational and/or compositional practices, understood in a broad sense. This may include improvisation rooted in, for example, Nordic jazz traditions, free improvisation, electronic music, or other experimental or tradition-based forms of musical expression. It may also include composition with an emphasis on areas such as electroacoustic music, sound art, music and sound production, live electronics, generative or interactive systems, digital instruments, and other artistic methods for creating music and sound. We welcome applicants with a range of profiles and areas of emphasis within the field. Applicants may be primarily rooted in improvisation, composition, or at the intersection of these areas. Duties The Assistant Professor will conduct and further develop independent artistic research in Musical Performance and Interpretation, primarily rooted in improvisation, composition, or the intersection of these areas. The duties include developing and carrying out artistic research projects; documenting, critically reflecting on, and communicating research processes and outcomes in relevant artistic and academic formats; and actively seeking external research funding. The candidate is expected to initiate and participate in national and international collaborations and to contribute actively to the Academy’s research environment. The duties also include teaching and supervision at first-cycle and second-cycle levels and, where applicable, at doctoral level. Teaching will be within the subject area of Musical Performance and Interpretation, with the main emphasis on the candidate’s area of expertise. The candidate is expected to contribute to the development of programme content and teaching formats, and to foster a close and critically reflective relationship between artistic practice, research, and teaching. The duties also include collaboration with the professional music and cultural sectors, participation in the Academy’s collegial work, and administrative duties. At least 50% of the position is allocated to research. Responsibilities and tasks may change over time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Musical Performance and Interpretation or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The applicant must also demonstrate documented experience of artistic practice at a high level in improvisation and/or composition, evidenced by publicly presented works, productions, performances, or comparable artistic commissions. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality artistic research within the subject area of the position. Both artistic research expertise and teaching expertise will be assessed. In the overall ranking of candidates, the greatest weight will be given to artistic research expertise, followed by teaching expertise. The ability to collaborate, contribute to collegial work, and carry out administrative duties will also be taken into account. Artistic research expertise Expertise in artistic research is assessed according to the standards and publication practices that apply within the field of artistic research. Public performances, artistic presentations, participation in internationally curated projects, and dissemination through internationally recognised artistic platforms may therefore count as forms of publication and knowledge dissemination comparable to peer-reviewed publication in other disciplines. Artistic research expertise is demonstrated through publications and ongoing participation in national and international research collaborations. In assessing artistic research expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on: high-quality publications grounded in the applicant’s own strong artistic practice; the ability to formulate competitive research projects and secure external research funding on a competitive basis; documented artistic practice at a high level in improvisation and/or composition, with particular consideration given to its scope, quality, independence, and relevance to the subject area of the position. Applicants are not required to have comparable qualifications in both improvisation and composition. The assessment will be based on the quality of the applicant’s individual profile and its relevance to the field and to the Academy’s activities. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of planning, delivering and developing teaching, as well as supervising students, in Musical Performance and Interpretation or other areas relevant to the position. In assessing teaching expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on: the ability to integrate and communicate artistic practice, research and critical reflection in teaching; the ability to support students’ independent artistic development; the ability to work in both individual and group-based teaching formats. Emphasis will also be placed on documented contributions to course and programme development, evidence of pedagogical reflection, and the ability to create inclusive and intellectually challenging learning environments for students from diverse artistic backgrounds. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and deemed suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject are, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in Swedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor (docent) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885): Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here: common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during end of week 49. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Erik Jonasson, Head of the music unit, +46766184167, erik.jonasson@hsm.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at tha Academy of music and drama, hr@hsm.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Audio, video, and web-based media materials that cannot be uploaded to the application platform could be submitted via stable direct links in a Word or PDF document under the relevant eligibility criterion. The document must clearly state what the material contains and explain its relevance to the application. The links must remain accessible throughout the entire recruitment process, and the material must be available for download in order to be considered as supporting documentation submitted with the application. Publications that cannot be attached to the application digitally should be sent to: Academy of Music and Drama HR Box 210 SE-405 30 Göteborg Sweden and must be received no later than the application deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41206": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Public Administration",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Public Administration For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Public Administration Reference number PAR 2026/577 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position intended to give the appointee the opportunity to develop their teaching and research expertise. At the end of the appointment, an Assistant Professor may apply for promotion to Senior Lecturer. If the Assistant Professor is deemed suitable and fulfils the requirements for promotion, they shall be promoted and given a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. More information about the department is available on our website gu.se/en/public-administration . Subject area Public Administration Subject area description The School of Public Administration conducts education, research and collaboration within the subject of public administration. Public administration is a multidisciplinary field of research that studies the organisation, governance and functioning of the public sector and its relationship to society, integrating theories and methods from several social science disciplines. Research and education in public administration at the department are conducted mainly within three areas: politics, democracy and public administration; public sector accounting and financial management; and organisation, management and society. The School of Public Administration currently hosts research projects on, among other things, administration and bureaucracy, professions, the location of public services, artificial intelligence (AI), cybersecurity, energy policy, public procurement, local democracy, financial management and control, migration and work integration, and legal perspectives on public administration. More information about our researchers and ongoing research can be found on our website: Our Research The School of Public Administration has a long tradition of research on local politics and administration and of collaboration with the surrounding community. This environment is strengthened by the long-standing Partnership for Collaboration, Education and Research (PACER), which brings together the School of Public Administration, municipalities and Region Västra Götaland in research, education and collaboration. The School of Public Administration’s programmes in public administration comprise bachelor’s and master’s programmes, doctoral education and commissioned education. The School of Public Administration is also responsible for courses within the Pre-school Teacher Programme and the National School Leadership Training Programme. The School of Public Administration welcomes both applicants who wish to join some of our existing research areas and applicants who wish to develop other areas within the subject of public administration. Duties The duties of an Assistant Professor in public administration consist of research to one half and teaching to one half. The teaching component may also include administrative duties. The duties comprise: Research within the subject of public administration. The Assistant Professor is free to choose research questions, develop theoretical ideas, collect data and select research methods. The duties include actively applying for external international and national research funding and actively participating in the department’s research environments and seminar activities. The Assistant Professor is also expected to take part in the international scholarly conversation through participation in conferences and through international publication with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. Teaching, course coordination, supervision and examination in courses at first-cycle and second-cycle level in public administration. Teaching within third-cycle (doctoral) education may also become relevant. Teaching in most of the department’s programmes and courses may be considered. The duties also include course and programme development and participation in the department’s educational development work. Collaboration with the wider community. Active participation in the continued development of the department and its academic environment in Gothenburg. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as a teacher is set out in Chapter 4, Sections 3–4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg (GU 2024/1687) . Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in public administration or in another subject area relevant to the position in which the public administration perspective is central, such as sociology, social work, political science or business administration, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders. Participation in commissioned education, the performance of qualified research-related review assignments within the public sector, networking activities directed at public administrations, and a documented ability to communicate research findings to the public are all considered meritorious. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Considerable weight will be given to the applicant’s intention and ability to participate actively in the academic environment at the School of Public Administration in Gothenburg. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: School of Public Administration, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in public administration, upon application submitted no later than six months before the fixed-term employment expires, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the assessment criteria for appointment as Senior Lecturer that apply at the University of Gothenburg in accordance with the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teachers at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). The following requirements also apply in such an assessment: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of research expertise through: Scholarly publication of good quality in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, scholarly publication of good quality in peer-reviewed books or book chapters is also meritorious. Documented and recurring applications for external research funding in international and/or national competition. Obtaining such funding is highly meritorious. Taking responsibility for the research community, for example by having undertaken review assignments, acted as faculty opponent or served as a member of an examining committee. Progression in relation to the applicant’s research qualifications at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example by attaining the qualifications required for the title of docent (associate professor). The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of teaching expertise through: Teaching amounting to at least 1,600 hours. Experience of course development and of different forms of teaching. Completed and approved training in teaching and learning in higher education (courses PIL101–PIL103, or a decision by the Dean on validation) and supervisor training (PIL201, or a decision by the Dean on validation). Progression in relation to the applicant’s teaching expertise at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example through experience of teaching at different levels, in different subject areas, or through deepened expertise within previously covered areas. The applicant must have demonstrated collaborative and leadership ability through: Having organised and participated in outward-facing academic activities, for example workshops, panels or conferences. Having organised and participated in outward-facing activities in collaboration with the wider community, for example conferences with decision-makers, service users or organisations, or other activities directed at the general public. Having demonstrated the ability to lead and organise academic projects, education or other activities. Having taken collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in assessment groups and working groups. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: Good judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties included in the position, and the ability to cooperate and contribute to the development of activities. The applicant must fulfil the following language requirement: Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated by a passed language test, the requirements of which are described here (common-european-framework-reference-language-skills), or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is manifestly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: employment benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during December 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Vicki Johansson, telephone +46 31 786 15 96 or email Vicki.Johansson@spa.gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Maria Utterhall, HR Officer, maria.utterhall@spa.gu.se , +46 31 786 29 66. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The following documents must be attached to the application: 1. A cover letter stating why you are applying for the position. In the letter, you should describe how your research and teaching fit into the subject of public administration in general, as well as how you intend to contribute to the School of Public Administration’s existing research areas and study programmes and/or to contribute new perspectives that would enrich the environment. Please also provide a detailed account of how you intend to contribute actively to the School of Public Administration’s academic environment through by being actively present at the department in Gothenburg. 2. A curriculum vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding obtained (with amounts). 3. A research plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and must be capable of being developed into at least one competitive research grant application. 4. A description of your research qualifications, containing: a) A list of your five most important scholarly publications. State your role and the extent of your contribution in the case of publications that are co-authored with others. Co-authorship certificates must be attached; these must contain information about each author’s contribution (quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, a certificate confirming that the publication has been accepted for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research qualifications, your research profile and your research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) 5. Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most important scholarly publications. 6. A description of your teaching qualifications, containing: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught on (including courses and programmes at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle level). b) A brief account of your teaching qualifications, with information on both extent and quality, including a pedagogical self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages.) c) A certificate of completed training in teaching and learning in higher education. d) A certificate of your teaching experience from the head of department, director of studies or equivalent at at least one university/department. 7. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions to students (maximum 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your teaching qualifications (6b). 8. Certificates of employment. 9. Doctoral degree certificate. 10. Any other documents that you as an applicant wish to invoke. Publications that cannot be attached to the application electronically should be sent in two copies to: University of Gothenburg, School of Public Administration, att: Maria Utterhall, Box 712, SE-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden, stating PAR 2026/577. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archi"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41331": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance Reference number PAR 2026/658 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professors with an international background at the end of 2026. The University’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. With approximately 3,700 full-time students, 470 employees, 160 international partner universities and four departments, the School of Business, Economics and Law offers exceptional breadth and is Triple Crown accredited through EQUIS, AACSB and AMBA. The Department of Business Administration is the School’s largest department and conducts extensive research and education at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. The position is based in the Industrial and Financial Management & Logistics (IFEL) section, which conducts research and education in areas including corporate finance and collaborates closely with research environments such as the Centre for Finance (CFF), Northern Lead and Lighthouse. Subject area Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance. Subject area description Corporate Finance examines firms’ financial decision-making, with a focus on investment decisions, financing strategies, and mechanisms for ensuring a market-based return for both shareholders and lenders. Central to the field is the development of theoretically and empirically grounded frameworks that facilitate profitable investments and efficient financing while taking risk and the cost of capital into account. Particular emphasis is placed on analyzing the factors that influence firms’ choices of capital structure and investment strategies, especially in environments characterized by asymmetric information and transaction costs. A clear grounding in financial theory is essential for understanding, explaining, and analyzing these decisions and their consequences. The field has a strong methodological orientation, employing quantitative tools such as discounted cash flow models, real and financial option models, as well as statistical and econometric methods, to analyze how external market conditions affect firms’ financial decisions. Duties The position comprises a maximum of 50% teaching and a minimum of 50% research. Research is expected to focus on Corporate Finance, align with the subject area description above, and be of a quality suitable for publication in high-quality international scientific journals. Research is also expected to be actively disseminated through research seminars and academic networks. The Assistant Professor will teach at first-cycle and second-cycle (Master’s) levels and is also expected to contribute to doctoral education and contract education, as well as course development and administrative duties. Teaching will primarily concern courses in Corporate Finance, including Corporate Valuation, Advanced Corporate Finance, Financial Management, or similar courses, as well as supervision of Bachelor’s and Master’s theses in finance and within Accounting and Financial Management. The position also requires active efforts to apply for external research funding during the employment period and to contribute to the development of the department’s educational and research environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg GU 2024/3252. To be eligible for appointment, the candidate must have been awarded a doctoral degree in Business Administration specialising in Corporate Finance, or have corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to candidates who were awarded their doctoral degree or achieved equivalent competence no more than five years before the application deadline. Candidates who achieved such competence earlier may also be considered if there are special grounds, such as sick leave, parental leave, or similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg GU 2024/3252. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research in Corporate Finance. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. Particular emphasis will be placed on high-quality theoretical and/or empirical research in Corporate Finance and publication in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability and potential to conduct further high-quality research during the appointment, and the ability to obtain external research funding in competition, are meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of planning and delivering teaching in various forms, including lectures, seminars, and supervision, at first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area. Particular emphasis is placed on the quality of teaching, potential for continued pedagogical development, and documented contributions to the development of courses, study programmes, or teaching methods. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community may be demonstrated through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, and through communicating research findings to the public. Administrative competence includes experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, and contributions to the development of the department or faculty. Personal qualities, including the ability to collaborate effectively within the department and with external partners, will also be considered when assessing suitability. Overall Assessment An Assistant Professorship is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of expertise and development potential, is considered suitable and to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties of the position, collaborating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of the department. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Business Administration, School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg Starting date: By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop independence as a researcher and strengthen scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established promotion criteria (GU 2025/3441). An application for promotion must be submitted to the department no later than six months before the end of the appointment period. At the time of application for promotion, the applicant must demonstrate research expertise and clear progression in research qualifications and achievements during the appointment. The assessment considers familiarity with the subject area and prior research, creativity and originality, analytical depth and breadth, scientific quality, independence, and forward-looking development. Active participation in the scientific community, academic service, constructive research collaboration, and societal impact of research may also be taken into account. For teaching qualifications, the applicant must have completed higher education teaching and learning training in accordance with University requirements, or have equivalent documented knowledge, and must demonstrate substantial high-quality teaching experience at first-cycle and/or second-cycle level during the appointment. The assessment includes documented teaching qualifications, pedagogical reflection and development, experience from different educational environments and teaching formats, and, where relevant, course and programme leadership and production of teaching materials. For internationally recruited Assistant Professors, promotion to Senior Lecturer requires successful completion of Swedish language studies at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR, in accordance with the Vice-Chancellor’s decision (Ref. GU 2022/1887). Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work-life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR, you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned after completion of the external expert assessment. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Taylan Mavruk, Professor and Head of the IFEL Section, taylan.mavruk@handels.gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 5958, or Jonas Flodén, Associate Professor and Deputy Head of the IFEL Section, jonas.floden@handels.gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 5131. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR Officer Natalie Axell at natalie.axell@gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available on the University website. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application must include: Curriculum Vitae, including a complete publication list, copies of degree certificates and relevant certificates, and an account of external research funding obtained. A letter of intent/research plan (maximum three pages) describing how you intend to carry out the duties and develop your research in Corporate Finance during the appointment. An account of research qualifications, previous research activities, research profile and contributions (maximum two pages). PhD thesis and up to four additional scholarly works. An account of teaching qualifications, including higher education teacher training, previous teaching assignments and a pedagogical reflection (maximum two pages). Relevant course evaluations or equivalent documentation may be attached. A brief account of achievements in management, collaboration, and administration. At least two references, including name, title, current higher education institution/organisation, and contact details. Other certificates and documents that you wish to invoke. All merits must be documented and certificates attested. Attachments should be named so that their content is clearly identifiable. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41012": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Fine Art",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Fine Art For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Fine Art Reference number PAR 2026/563 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The  Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and HSM – Academy of Performing Arts and Music. As a unified faculty for art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work of creating a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is now looking for an Assistant Professor in Fine Art, who will be part of the unit Craft and Fine Art and attached to the Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary (CAPIm) a joint initiative with our partner Kungl. Konsthögskolan (KKH). The Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary is committed to interdisciplinary practice and research in the meeting between contemporary art and the future of politics. Based at two institutions of higher education in art HDK-Valand and Kungl. Konsthögskolan, the Centre’s aim is to facilitate connections between research and education through an engagement with experimental approaches. It is the first Swedish Centre of Excellence in the field of Artistic Research. HDK-Valand’s research environment values a broad and inclusive approach whereby knowledge is produced within established research formats, experimental artistic practices and education. Our research contributes to discourses on environmental, societal and cultural challenges through engagement in interdisciplinary projects. Four research themes oriented around politics, the environment, economics and materiality operate as focus areas to facilitate collaboration between different subjects and actors, and to provide a broad base for both research and teaching. An overarching objective at HDK-Valand is the development of a complete academic environment grounded in these themes More about HDK-Valand here: https://www.gu.se/en/hdk-valand More about CAPIm here: https://capim.se/en Subject area Fine Art Subject area description Fine art at HDK-Valand is characterised by the independent artistic practice of artists working individually and collectively. Fine art is typically not guided by functional or commercial imperatives that are external to the practice itself. In addition to bachelor's and master's degree programmes we have a shared commitment to freestanding courses, life-long learning, continued professional development, and research informed education in this subject area. Fine art is also characterised by a lively and broad research culture at HDK-Valand that includes the clusters: (i) Economies: Organizing, Valuating and Working, (ii) Materialities and Experimental Aesthetics, (iii) Environment: Ecological and Climate Challenges, and (iv) Politics, Practices and Publics. The subject thinks broadly and innovatively about forms of making public including but not limited to, exhibition and exhibition-making, publishing and a wide range of organizational, curatorial and mediation practices – taking account of how, where, when, for whom, and by whom art is produced, disclosed and circulated. Fine art fosters moments of exchange between education, research and the wider society. At HDK-Valand, the subject of fine art encompasses an open concept of artistic practice that includes an expanding variety of formats, genres, media, techniques and traditions such as: – Drawing; graphics; painting; automated image generation; and text-based practices. – Conceptual art; found-objects; installation; sculpture; site-specific work; land art; bio-art; and eco-art. – Moving image; animation; photography; digital media; augmented and virtual reality; robotics; and work with autonomous agents. – Performance; live-art; body-art; and sound-art. – Public art; community art; collaborative, social and relational practices; and curatorial practice. Duties Research tasks (50% of the total employment) Realise own artistic research projects within the framework of the Centre’s remit aligned with one of CAPIm’s key thematics: (i) Climate (ii) Historical (iii) Democratic and (iv) Technological Imaginaries Conduct assigned research tasks under the supervision of Prof. Mick Wilson, and in concert with colleagues at the Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary contribute to specific research trajectories associated with the Centre, including but not limited to work on: (i) Mapping the Political Imaginary; (ii) The Observatory of the Artistic Doctorate; and (iii) The role of editorial practices and small-scale presses in artistic research. This work to include tasks such as: Attending regular in-person team meetings Collaborating with colleagues at CAPIm and its broader research community to support activities (e.g., seminars with invited guest researchers; research intensives; and conferences co-organized by CAPIm.) Communicating research and research results to the public in various forms of publication and dissemination (e.g., artistic production, academic journals, academic and public presentations, seminars, workshops, symposia, online media dissemination, public screenings and exhibition) Maintaining, and sharing on a regular basis, detailed documentation of all research processes and outcomes. Participating in collaborative research funding applications based on both the assigned and self-set research Contributing to, and co-editing, joint publications produced within CAPIm Participating in competence development workshops and seminars as required Education tasks (50% of the total employment) Attending regular in-person team meetings Working together with colleagues to deliver and develop existing and new courses at Bachelor, Masters and Doctoral level, including courses associated with CAPIm, as required. Contributing to course and student administration processes and report writing, including quality assurance and enhancement processes and related tasks as required. Working together with colleagues to develop innovative educational content and formats based on current developments in research. Taking on the role of course responsible as required. Participating in competence development workshops and seminars as required. Respon s ib i l i ties and tasks may change over ti me. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. R equirements are: track record in artistic practice-based research which can be demonstrated by identifying previous artistic research activities and outputs in the application and/or supporting documentation. demonstrated knowledge and understanding of the imaginary and the political imaginary as these concepts operate within the domain of artistic production and theory which can be demonstrated by including descriptions of relevant experience in this regard (educational, research or in artistic practice) in the application and/or supporting documentation. the ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise in the fine art context is interpreted according to the norms that apply within the domain of artistic research. Activities such as public exhibition, public performances, participation in international curated projects and dissemination via internationally reputable art platforms and institutions function also as an equivalent for peer review publication in other disciplines. Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (l ectures, seminars, supervision, practical workshops, and project-based teaching ) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent post as a senior lecturer in the same subject area as that of the Assistant Professor post. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject area, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in Swedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor ( docent) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885) Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during early December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Prof. Mick Wilson, mick.wilson@akademinvaland.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at HDK-Valand: hr@hdk-valand.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Publications that cannot be attached to the application online should be sent to: HDK-Valand Box 131 405 30 and must be received by the deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41380": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in subject-matter education",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in subject-matter education For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in subject-matter education Reference number PAR 2026/672 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in subject-matter education, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas—subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in subject-matter education will be based at the Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies. Subject area Subject-matter education Subject area description Subject-matter education concerns teaching and learning with a focus on specific content, often related to school subjects, academic disciplines, or professional practice. Subject-matter education research investigates the conditions for, and the relationship between, teaching and learning in one of the following subject areas: social studies, Swedish, mathematics, or science and technology. Duties A position as assistant professor in subject-matter education in one of the specialisations listed above is intended to enable the successful candidate to develop their teaching and research expertise for further academic career progression. The position therefore comprises 50% research and 50% teaching. The research will be conducted in collaboration with senior researchers at the Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools and will form part of the research programme to be carried out within the strategic research area Excellence in School . At the Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, we conduct research, provide education, and engage in collaboration concerning teaching and learning through subject-matter education, practice-oriented research, and critical research. Our research is organised into three research environments: Didactical Classroom Studies; Critical Education Research; and Phenomenography, Variation Theory, and Learning Study. As an assistant professor, you will be affiliated with one of our research environments and one of our divisions for subject-matter education. Read more about the department, and our divisions and research environments here: Department of Pedagogical, Curricular and Professional Studies, University of Gothenburg The department’s teaching mostly takes place within the primary teacher education programme and the secondary teacher education programme, but also includes teaching on other programmes and courses and in continuing professional development. Teaching duties may also include school-based components of teacher education, core courses in educational studies, supervision of students at different levels (first-, second-, and third-cycle), and assessment of degree projects. Course coordination and administration are also included in the duties. Teaching at the department is conducted primarily on campus and in Swedish, although some teaching in English also takes place. Good oral and written proficiency in English and in Swedish or another Scandinavian language is therefore required. Staff are also expected to be present on campus to a significant extent, thereby contributing to a stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject-matter education or another relevant field of doctoral studies, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networks For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise A teaching qualification or experience of working as an educator Experience of activities specific to teacher education, such as school-based placement Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities (and suitability) required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Annika Lilja, annika.lilja@ped.gu.se, +46 31 786 24 33. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer, Mikaela Hansson, mikaela.hansson@gu.se, +46 31 786 56 08. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Please indicate which specialisation in subject-matter education you are applying for: social studies, Swedish, mathematics, or science and technology. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other written works that cannot be submitted digitally with the application must be sent in duplicate to: Mikaela Hansson, Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, University of Gothenburg,  Box 300, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Pleas"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41010": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design Reference number PAR 2026/562 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The  Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and  Academy of Music and Drama.  As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is now looking for an Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design, who will conduct research and teaching at the intersection between design studies and business studies. You will be part of the Design unit , attached to the Business and Design Lab (BDL) . The Business and Design Lab (BDL) is a research centre that since 2010 builds on a collaboration between HDK-Valand and the School of Business, Economics and Law at the University of Gothenburg. Pioneering the intersection between design studies and management and organization studies, BDL offers an inter- and transdisciplinary platform for a critical and grounded investigation of the relations between design & art, technology and the economy. Located between two faculties, the centre has developed a symmetrical dialogue between the two domains of scholarship to study both the role of design and art in organizations and the ways in which design and art work are organized and valued. With a particular interest for methodological developments based on design and artistic methods, the centre has led a large range of research projects nationally and internationally and is seeking to consolidate its next phase of development. The centre involves junior and senior researchers and is led by Elena Raviola, who holds the Torsten and Wanja Söderberg Professorship in Design Management. HDK-Valand’s research environment values a broad and inclusive approach whereby knowledge is produced within established research formats, experimental artistic practices and education. Our research contributes to discourses on environmental, societal and cultural challenges through engagement in interdisciplinary projects. Four research themes oriented around politics, the environment, economics and materiality operate as focus areas to facilitate collaboration between different subjects and actors, and to provide a broad base for both research and teaching. An overarching objective at HDK-Valand is the development of a complete academic environment grounded in these themes More about HDK-Valand here: https://www.gu.se/en/hdk-valand More about BDL here: https://www.gu.se/en/bdl Subject area Design, specialised in Business and Design Subject area description The subject area of this position is Design specialised in Business and Design as the interdisciplinary intersection of design research and management and organization studies. At this intersection between designing and organizing, research investigates design as an organizational, managerial and societal practice as well as organizations, organizing processes and other forms of economic activities as objects of design themselves. A particular area of focus is how the design of material artefacts, such as physical tools, digital technologies, and spatial architecture, is shaped by and shapes organizations and markets in different ways, including issues related to professional work, judgment, learning, leadership, accounting, innovation, strategy formulation, and other organizational phenomena, and ultimately how design contributes to the creation and transformation of organizations, markets, institutions and societal futures. It is central to the subject area of Business and Design at HDK-Valand to engage with design and artistic methodologies, such as participatory design, collaborative design, speculative design, prototyping, games, and related approaches, and to contribute to the development of innovative methodological approaches for creating new forms of engagement with researchers, practitioners, and the wider public around shared societal challenges. Research within the area may include research on the cultural sector, but may also address other sectors and phenomena depending on the candidate’s research interests. Particular priority will be given to research that, at the intersection of these fields, contributes to understand how democracy and the democratic public sphere are being reshaped through design-based and artistic methods. Duties The candidate will conduct research and teaching, with approximately 50% of the position devoted to research and 50% to teaching. Research responsibilities include to: develop and carry out interdisciplinary research projects in collaboration with other researchers nationally and internationally; publish articles and other scholarly texts in international journals and with international publishers; conduct assigned research tasks under the supervision of Prof. Elena Raviola and contribute to the national and international strategic and operational development of the Business and Design Lab, including the organization of seminar series and other meetings, the development of collaborations with local actors, and participation in larger research bid; apply for own research projects to Swedish, Nordic and European funders; participate in existing research projects by conducting research work and assisting with project management and coordination; engage with societal partners and cultural institutions in research collaborations; develop and communicate research findings in public facing activities. Teaching will take place at the undergraduate, master and doctoral levels in relevant courses and programs in design and other teaching areas of the Academy and will include to: develop existing and new courses; lead course coordination and administration; supervise student projects and theses; teach in classroom and digital formats. R esponsibilities and tasks may change over time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates R equirements are: a familiarity with the research debates and communities both in design and in management and organization studies demonstrated in the application and/or supporting documentation experience with ethnographic approaches and artistic methods in research work , demonstrated in the application and/or supporting documentation . the ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Specific assessment criteria for this position are: the demonstrated level of engagement with and potential for intellectual contributions to the research communities in design and in management and organization studies; the demonstrated capacity of methodological innovation in ethnography and artistic methods; the demonstrated level of familiarity with science and technology studies, especially as informing design research and organization studies. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. As the engagement with innovative design and artistic methods might materialize in other outputs than publications, research expertise is also demonstrated through other research outputs, such as design prototypes, public exhibitions, curated projects and participation in internationally reputable design initiatives. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, practice-based workshops, studio-based learning and supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent post as a senior lecturer in the same subject area as that of the Assistant Professor post. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject area, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in S wedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor ( docent ) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885) Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg. If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during early December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Prof. Elena Raviola elena.raviola@gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at HDK-Valand: hr@hdk-valand.gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Publications that cannot be attached to the application online should be sent to: HDK-Valand Box 131 405 30 and must be received by the deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41298": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age Reference number PAR 2026/517 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The position of Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age, is based at the Department of Historical Studies, which is part of the Faculty of Humanities. The Department conducts research in the fields of History, Archaeology, and Classical Archaeology and Ancient History. Teaching in these disciplines is offered at all levels, from undergraduate courses to doctoral education. In addition, undergraduate teaching is provided within the main field of Cultural Heritage Studies. The Department’s current educational mission comprises more than 730 full-time students. Research is carried out both within the individual disciplines and through larger interdisciplinary projects, where national and international collaboration forms an important component. Today, the Department employs nearly 100 researchers, teachers, doctoral students, and support staff. The Department is centrally located in Gothenburg. Subject area Archaeology with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age. Subject area description This position specializes in the Nordic Iron Age, that is, the period from approximately 500 BCE to 1050 CE. This era constitutes a strategic link within the Department’s research profile, connecting two strong areas of expertise: on the one hand, Stone and Bronze Age archaeology, and on the other, Medieval History. More information about the Department and the subject of Archaeology is available on our website: https://www.gu.se/historiska-studier . Duties The duties include research, teaching, and external engagement, as well as related administrative tasks. The position is primarily to be carried out on site at the University of Gothenburg, and the successful candidate is therefore expected to be physically present at the Department. The primary responsibility is to conduct and develop research in Archaeology with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age. The successful candidate is expected to develop an independent research profile, actively seek external research funding, and contribute to the development of the Department’s research environment through national and international collaborations. In addition, the successful candidate is expected to engage in course development within the subject area. The duties also include teaching and supervision in Archaeology at the undergraduate and master's levels and, depending on qualifications and the needs of the Department, at the doctoral level. Teaching may encompass both chronological and thematic courses in Archaeology and may be conducted in Swedish and English. In addition, the position includes participation in the Department’s shared responsibilities and engagement with the wider community, for example through collaboration with the cultural heritage sector, museums, and other relevant societal stakeholders. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an associate senior lecturer. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates Ability to teach in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language) and English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Great importance will also be placed on personal suitability and strong collaborative skills. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Chronologically, the primary focus should be on the first millennium CE. Applicants are expected to demonstrate a critical and independent engagement with the current state of research. We also value expertise that facilitates collaboration with our strong research environments in Bronze Age and Medieval studies, particularly the latter. Extensive experience from academic institutions outside Sweden will be regarded as a strong asset. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Experience in leading and organizing archaeological field courses is also considered a merit, as is teaching experience relevant to the main field of Cultural Heritage Studies. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Historical Studies, Gothenburg, Sweden. Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. For more information, please visit the Faculty of Humanities' webpage: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Head of Department Henrik Janson at henrik.janson@history.gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR officer Johanna Hagbranth at johanna.hagbranth@gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The application must include: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding obtained (including amounts awarded). Research plan. The plan should clearly relate to the research area of the position and should be developed to a level that would support at least one competitive application for external research funding. A description of your research qualifications, including: a) A list of your five most significant scholarly publications. For co-authored publications, please specify your role and the extent of your contribution. For all co-authored publications, co-author statements must be provided. These statements should describe each author's contribution, both quantitatively and substantively, and must be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation verifying its acceptance must be enclosed. b) A concise account of your research achievements, research profile, and research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 3 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most significant scholarly publications. A description of your teaching qualifications, including: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught, including courses and programmes at undergraduate, master's, and doctoral levels. The documentation should indicate the scope of your contributions. b) A concise account of your teaching merits and qualifications, addressing both their extent and quality, including a reflection on your teaching practice. (Maximum 3 pages.) c) Certificates documenting completed training in higher education pedagogy. d) Certificates verifying your teaching experience, issued by a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent representative at at least one university or department. e) Any pedagogical writings and publications (maximum 3 items). Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum 3 items). Please refer to these documents in your account of teaching merits and qualifications. Doctoral degree certificate (PhD diploma). Any additional certificates or documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Scholarly publications, written works, CVs, and similar materials that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: Department of Historical Studies Attn: HR Box 200 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41058": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies Reference number PAR 2026/579 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Languages and Literatures is the largest department within the Faculty of Humanities, comprising 16 different languages, approximately 90 employees, around 1,090 full-time equivalent students, and an annual turnover of just over SEK 110 million. The department operates in modern facilities centrally located near Götaplatsen Subject area English with a specialisation in Literary Studies Subject area description English encompasses both language studies and literary studies and has degree-awarding powers at all levels, including doctoral education. In addition to stand-alone courses, English is included in several degree programmes at the department, such as the Bachelor’s Programme in English with a focus on working life, the Master’s Programme in Languages and Intercultural Communication, the Teacher Education Programme, and the Nordic Master’s Programme for Language Teachers. The subject offers both campus-based and online courses. Research spans both language studies and literary studies and includes applied research projects focusing, for example, on language learning and digital humanities. In addition, research in the discipline is conducted through interdisciplinary research environments. Duties An Assistant Professor will be given the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and teacher and to strengthen both their research and teaching qualifications in order to meet the eligibility requirements for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The duties include research amounting to at least 50% in accordance with an established research plan in English Literary Studies, and teaching and administration amounting to no more than 50%. The successful candidate is expected to follow developments within their own subject area, actively apply for external research funding, and contribute to the development of the department’s teaching and research environment. Applicants should therefore describe in their application how their own research can promote and contribute to the department’s research areas. The position also includes teaching, supervision and examination in English with a specialisation in Literary Studies at first- and second-cycle levels, both on campus and online, and both on stand-alone courses (for example, literary theory and method, literary and cultural history, and academic communication) and within the Teacher Education Programme (for example, school-based education/VFU and literature teaching methodology). Course development and administrative duties are also included, such as course coordination and work with the University’s learning platform. Evening teaching may occur. Teaching on interdisciplinary courses and programmes at the department may also be included, and the successful candidate is expected to be prepared to contribute to teaching in Swedish within two years. The main part of the work is to be carried out at the department at the University of Gothenburg, and the person appointed is expected to be present and to participate actively in the department’s development and working environment, including staff meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. Work in close dialogue with colleagues is an integral part of the role, and the person appointed is expected to contribute constructively and collegially both to the discipline and to the department as a whole. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in the subject area of the position or has corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved equivalent competence at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds include sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision and examination) at first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, particular emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its teaching, and consequently improved quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of expertise and development potential, is deemed suitable and to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. A research background in postcolonial and/or decolonial studies is also highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: the Department of Languages and Literatures, the Faculty of Humanities, Gothenburg Starting date: By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their research and teaching qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. An Assistant Professor employed under Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance shall, upon application, be promoted to Senior Lecturer if they are eligible for such an appointment and, following assessment, are deemed suitable for such an appointment in accordance with the assessment criteria established by the University in connection with the recruitment. Promotion also requires completion of supervisor training and higher education teaching and learning training as determined by the Vice-Chancellor, as well as fulfilment of the Swedish-language requirement determined by the Vice-Chancellor. The assessment criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer must be stated in the vacancy announcement for the Assistant Professorship. An application for promotion entails permanent employment as Senior Lecturer. For more information, see “Guidelines for applications for appointment as, or promotion to, Senior Lecturer at the Faculty of Humanities, 2026”, available at: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position . Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work-life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. In addition to external expert review, interviews, a trial lecture and reference checks will form part of the assessment. The trial lecture will be delivered in English and the subsequent interview will be conducted in English. Particular emphasis will be placed on the applicant’s ability to deliver a trial lecture in English within the advertised subject area and to participate in an interview in English, including an oral discussion of their research and teaching experience. Interviews and trial lectures will be conducted via Zoom and are planned for week 50. Trial lecture topic: The Societal Relevance of Literary Studies: How Can Your Research Profile Advance Knowledge in English Literary Studies and Contribute to Our Understanding of Pressing Societal Issues? Based on a field related to your own research, discuss and exemplify how English literary studies can contribute to the understanding of current societal challenges. The lecture should demonstrate how theoretical, methodological and/or historical perspectives within the subject can generate new knowledge, while simultaneously relating your own research focus to broader research questions within English literary studies. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that the application is complete when the application period closes. An incomplete application will be assessed on the basis of the documents submitted. The following documents must be attached to the application: Cover letter Research plan Curriculum vitae (CV), including a record of degrees and previous employment Description of research and teaching activities, including research and teaching reflections Qualifications portfolio I: Research expertise Qualifications portfolio II: Teaching expertise Qualifications portfolio III: Collaboration with the wider community Qualifications portfolio IV: Leadership and administrative expertise Publications submitted for consideration (maximum five), together with a separate list of the publications Doctoral degree certificate Doctoral thesis References: names and telephone numbers of at least two referees For further information about the application, see “Guidelines for applications for appointment as Assistant Professor at the Faculty of Humanities, 2026” (PDF), available at: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Head of Department Petra Platen (prefektSPL@sprak.gu.se) or Director of Studies Anna-Lena Fredriksson ( anna- lena.fredriksson@sprak.gu.se). If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer at personalhandlaggare@sprak.gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41342": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Economics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Economics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Economics Reference number PAR 2026/642 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. With more than 3 700 full-time students, 470 employees, 160 international partner universities and four departments, the School of Business, Economics and Law has unique breadth. The School of Business, Economics and Law is “triple-crown” accredited - EQUIS, AACSB and AMBA. The Department of Economics is part of the School of Business, Economics and Law at the University of Gothenburg and brings together around 100 researchers and teachers from several countries. The department offers teaching in economics, finance, and statistics to around 800 students across courses and programmes. Our research spans a broad range of fields, including labour, health, applied microeconomics, environmental, development, behavioural, and financial economics. More information about the department is available at www.economics.gu.se . Department of Economics is seeking to appoint one or two Assistant Professors in Economics. Subject area Economics Subject area description Economics is a social science that studies how individuals, firms, and societies make decisions, how markets and institutions function, and how resources and opportunities are allocated. It combines theoretical reasoning and empirical methods to understand economic behaviour and outcomes, and to evaluate the effects of policies and institutions. The position is open to candidates working in any field of economics, with opportunities to contribute to both the department’s research and teaching. Duties The position comprises a maximum of 50 percent teaching and a minimum of 50 percent research. The successful candidate is expected to pursue high-quality research with the potential to make a significant contribution to international research and to publish in leading international academic journals. Active participation in research seminars and academic networks is expected. Teaching constitutes a central part of the position. The successful candidate will primarily teach core undergraduate courses in economics, such as macroeconomics, statistics, international trade, and financial economics. Teaching may also include courses at advanced and doctoral levels and in other areas of economics than those listed above. Swedish language courses are offered by the university for applicants who do not speak Swedish. Duties also include course development, course coordination, and administrative tasks related to teaching. The successful candidate is expected to actively contribute to the development and quality assurance of the department’s education, as well as to pedagogical development within the subject area. The successful candidate is also expected to contribute to the development of the department’s teaching and research environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within five years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg and the guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). Qualifications The following qualifications are required for this position: The ability to teach undergraduate courses in fields like macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics. The ability to independently initiate, conduct, and publish high-quality research. The ability to contribute positively on-site to the Department’s research, teaching, and working environment, as well as overall suitability for the position (see below). In addition to these requirements, the following qualifications are considered particularly meritorious: Previous experience of successfully teaching undergraduate courses in macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics. Previous experience of successfully teaching courses in Swedish. Previous international academic experience and publications in well-regarded peer-reviewed journals in economics. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise. Significant importance will also be attached to teaching expertise and to the ability and willingness to take an active role in the department’s teaching at both undergraduate and advanced levels. Collaborative and administrative competence will also be taken into account. The assessment will consider the qualifications of the candidates as a whole and in relation to the requirements and needs of the position. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and active participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on the quality, originality, and international relevance of the applicant’s research and publication record. The assessment takes into account demonstrated familiarity with the subject area and previous research, research independence, creativity and originality in the chosen research direction, as well as the depth of analysis and scope of scientific output, taking into consideration the length of time since the doctoral degree was awarded and the research trajectory during this period. In addition to the scholarly works cited in the application, the following factors, among others, shall be given weight in the overall assessment of the applicant’s academic merits and research expertise: Documented active participation in the scholarly community, for example through the presentation of research findings at national and international conferences, seminars, and similar forums. Demonstrated ability to interact and collaborate constructively with other researchers. Demonstrated ability to develop and sustain an independent research agenda. Evidence of research potential and a strong research trajectory, including the ability to publish in leading international and field journals and other high-quality scholarly outlets. For further details, see the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. Previous experience of teaching core undergraduate or advanced level courses such as macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics is considered meritorious. Also experience of teaching in Swedish is a merit. In the assessment of pedagogical competence, importance will also be attached to documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. For further details, see the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441) Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, active participation in seminars, mentoring, engagement in the development of teaching, and contributions to the overall development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment The position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position aimed at researchers and teachers who have completed their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of their qualifications and development potential, is assessed to have the strongest prospects for carrying out the duties of the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of the department’s activities. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:School of Business, Economics and Law at Department of Economics Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer Promotion to Senior Lecturer is regulated by the Higher Education Ordinance, the University of Gothenburg’s Appointment Regulations (GU 2024/3252), and the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). For an Assistant Professor (biträdande universitetslektor) to be promoted to Senior Lecturer (universitetslektor), an application for promotion must be submitted to the department no later than six months before the end of the five-year appointment period. In accordance with the guidelines “Appointment as Assistant Professor and Promotion from Assistant Professor to Senior Lecturer” (GU 2025/3441), at the Department of Economics, promotion to and permanent employment as a Senior Lecturer is based on demonstrated excellence in both research and teaching. During the appointment period, the candidate is expected to demonstrate clear and sustained progression in these two areas. External reviewers assess whether these criteria are met. Research progression is assessed based on a research record developed during the appointment period, which is expected to correspond to at least two publications in top field journals or high-quality general-interest journals in economics or finance. In addition, the assessment considers the candidate’s research independence, contribution to co-authored work, and overall research trajectory.  Active engagement in the research community, both within the department and externally, is expected, including seminar participation, mentoring, and related academic activities. Progression in teaching is for instance demonstrated through teaching excellence, course development, active on-site participation in teaching committees, and strong course evaluations. The overall assessment will be based on the candidate’s research and teaching merits, as well as their potential to contribute to the long-term development of the department. Promotion also requires completion of a university teaching and learning qualification programme approved by the Vice-Chancellor, as well as fulfillment of the Swedish language requirement established by the Vice-Chancellor ( Appointment Regulations for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg , GU 2024/3252) Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact:Head of Department Ola Olsson. ola.olsson@economics.gu.se , Tel: +0046 (0) 766 181341 If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact: HR Officer Neha Rohtagi, neha.rohtagi@economics@gu.se or (from 1/10) Axel Bondemark, axel.bondemark@economics.gu.se ,Tel: +0046 (0) 0766-18 27 01 Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. he following documents must be attached to the application: Curriculum Vitae (CV) , including a complete list of publications and an account of any external research funding received (including amounts). Research Plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and should be capable of forming the basis for at least one competitive research grant application. A description of your research qualifications , including: a) A list of your five most important scientific publications. For publications co-authored with others, specify your role and the extent of your contribution. Statements from co-authors must be attached; these should include information on each author's contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming acceptance for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research achievements, research profile, and research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most important scientific publications. A description of your teaching qualifications , including: a) A list of the courses and degree programmes that you have developed and taught on (including courses and programmes at the undergraduate, master's, and doctoral levels). b) A brief account of your teaching achievements and qualifications, including information on both the scope and quality of your teaching, as well as teaching self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages.) c) Documentation of completed higher education pedagogy training. d) Certification of your teaching experience from a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent in at least one university or department. Electronic copies of teaching materials , such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum three supporting documents). Refer to these documents in your statement of teaching achievements and qualifications (Section 5b) Doctoral degree certificate (PhD diploma). Reference letters from 2–3 people from your previous workplaces should be sent by email to: Neha Rohtagi: neha.rohtagi@economics.gu.se and, from October 1, to: Axel Bondemark: axel.bondemark@economics.gu.se Any other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Scientific publications that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Economics and Statistics Attn: Neha Rohtagi/Axel Bondemark Box 640 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please indicate reference number PAR 2026/642 on all submitted materials. The publications must be received no later than 2026-10-30 Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=40412": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Geology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Geology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Geology Reference number PAR 2026/390 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Earth Sciences has approximately 75 employees working across the fields of Geology, Physical Geography and Ecosystem Sciences. Our three priority research themes are \"The Geosphere\", \"Earth Surface Processes\" and \"Climate and Climate Change\". Within Geology belonging to “The Geosphere” research theme, we have established strengths in hydrogeology, geochemistry, geophysics and structural geology. Cooperation with strong specializations in the other fields, such as climatology, alpine and polar ecology as well as GIS and remote sensing, is encouraged. Our mission is to develop and deliver research, education, and services to society that enhance our understanding of the Earth System processes and the impacts of human activity. We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive workplace and a family-friendly environment. Sweden also offers parental leave and childcare policies. Further information is available on our website: Department of Earth Sciences , University of Gothenburg Employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg We welcome applications from innovative and forward-looking geoscientists across all areas of geology. Subject area Geology Subject area description Geology at the Department of Earth Sciences encompasses studies on understanding today’s geodynamic processes of the solid Earth, as well as the evolution of Earth and other terrestrial planets over the entire history of the solar system. Our research is rooted in the drive to advance basic science, but at the same time meet societal needs. We benefit from being able to use the diverse geology of Western Sweden as a natural laboratory for our studies, as one important component of an otherwise global network of field studies. Furthermore, we are a significant collaborator for a wide range of thriving geotechnical companies in the wider Gothenburg area due to expanding activities in tunnel and building construction, increasing need for water and energy resources. The geology group at the Department of Earth Sciences offers excellent research infrastructure including state-of-the-art analytical facilities, such as a micro-geochemistry laboratory, a stable isotope laboratory, water analysis facilities, a micro-Raman laboratory as well as an ice- and microplastic laboratory. These are complemented by advanced remote sensing and visualization facilities. Current research activities include mineral resources for a green economy, ice dynamics, development of new geochemical and petrophysical tools, groundwater resources, soil and groundwater contamination, subduction zone processes and links to natural hazards, climate change in the geologic past, and geothermal energy. Duties The tenure-track Assistant Professor position is primarily research-focused, with research comprising up to 80% of the workload. The position includes a fully funded PhD position attached to it, financed by the department. The appointee is expected to establish and lead an independent research programme within their chosen field in collaboration with colleagues both at the University of Gothenburg and with national and international partners. They will pursue research at the highest international level guided by their scientific expertise and priorities and are expected to build a competitive research group supported by external funding. Key responsibilities include publishing in high-quality peer-reviewed journals and actively securing external research funding. Teaching will comprise up to 20% of the workload.  The Assistant Professor will contribute to the development of new curricula and teach both classroom-based and field-based courses. Teaching will be in the subject area of geology within the Earth Science program. They will supervise students at all academic levels including Bachelor’s and Master’s student projects and may also supervise doctoral candidates and postdoctoral researchers. The position also includes participation in administrative duties at the Department and Faculty levels, as well as shared collegial responsibility for relevant analytical infrastructure. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Geology or a closely related field, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The Faculty of Science and Technology uses these criteria for the assessment of scientific and pedagogical competence and general suitability. Scientific and pedagogical qualifications must be adequate in relation to the subject content and job description of the advertised position. Research expertise Research expertise should be demonstrated through internationally recognized research, primarily evidenced by a strong record of peer reviewed publications. The ability to obtain external research funding constitutes an integral part of the assessment. The applicant must have a research profile within the field of geology. Particular emphasis will be placed on demonstrated connections to one or more of the Department’s core research areas: micro geochemistry (with a focus on critical raw materials), hydrogeology (including contaminant behaviour in soils and groundwater), and structural geology (with a focus on rock and ice microstructures). Teaching expertise In addition to the research expertise, documented pedagogical qualifications are considered meritorious for the employment as Assistant Professor. Relevant qualifications include teaching experience at undergraduate and postgraduate level within the subject area of geology, the ability to develop pedagogical activities, and experience of supervision. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence For the appointment as Assistant Professor, experience in outreach and engagement with society beyond academia is considered meritorious. Such experience may be demonstrated through experience in dissemination of information experience in community outreach experience in innovation and entrepreneurship For the appointment as Assistant Professor, leadership and administrative experience are considered advantageous. Relevant qualifications may include experience in academic leadership or management at unit, department, faculty or university level participation in boards, committees or other roles relevant to the functioning of the University experience of leadership in relevant networks/associations/organizations Overall Assessment The assessment of general suitability includes an evaluation of whether the applicant, with regard to this job description, has the personal qualities required to fulfil the duties of an Assistant Professor well, to interact with other staff and to contribute to the development of the Department’s activities. To this end, a brief written statement of how the applicant’s planned research can benefit and be integrated with the Department’s research must be included in the application. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Earth Sciences, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. The assessment criteria for promotion as a teacher are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers (GU 2025/1411). Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. The assessment of qualifications and experience will take into account documented parental leave, sick leave or other similar circumstances affecting research output and academic seniority. The reasons and extent of absence should be specified in the application. The interviews are scheduled to take place on 16 November 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Hans Linderholm, Head of Department of Earth Sciences at University of Gothenburg, Professor in Physical Geography, at +46 70-858-9504 or HansL@gvc.gu.se, or Mark Peternell, Associate Professor in Structural Geology, at +46 76-618-4553 or mark.peternell@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Therese Vestlund, Human Resources Officer, at +46 76-618-6678 or therese.vestlund@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The merit portfolio in your application consists of five parts: Personal data, degrees and evaluations Scientific portfolio Pedagogical portfolio Collaboration portfolio Leadership and administrative portfolio Together with your application which includes the merit portfolio, the following should be attached: Cover letter CV, including a complete list of publications, and a record of awarded research grants specifying funding amounts . A list of maximum ten selected scientific publications and a motivation for choosing each one. Describe your own role and input in each of the selected publications. Copies of the most relevant publications in full format (maximum ten publications). Description of how the applicant’s current and future research can be integrated with ongoing research at the department. Description of pedagogical experience, including evaluations of teaching quality. The description should also include pedagogical reflection and teaching philosophy (maximum two pages). Copy of PhD degree certificate, and other certificates/documents/transcripts that support the applicant’s qualifications. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be mailed in two copies to: Department of Earth Sciences Attn: Eugenia Andersson University of Gothenburg P.O. Box 460 S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden Application deadline: 30 October 2026 Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=40963": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention Reference number PAR 2026/549 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professor with an international background at the end of 2026. The university’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. The Department of Social Work is part of the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg. It is one of the largest and most established academic environments for research and education in social work in the Nordic region. The Department has more than 130 employees and provides education at first-, second- and third-cycle levels. Its educational offerings include the Social Work Programme, master’s programmes in Social Work and human rights, and other further education. Research is conducted across several thematic areas in an active and expanding academic environment characterised by a strong commitment to society. The department also has extensive international partnerships and collaborates with research networks and universities worldwide. More information is available on the Department of Social Work website: gu.se/en/socwork We are currently seeking to appoint an Assistant Professor in Social Work who will contribute to strengthening our research and education environment. Subject area Social Work Subject area description Social work examines the living conditions and experiences of people in situations of social vulnerability. The subject also explores the causes and consequences of social problems and how these can be understood at individual, group and societal levels. Written communication and the use of language in different forms and contexts are central to professional social work practice. Social work is a main field of study at first- and second-cycle levels and a third-cycle subject. Duties The duties of an Assistant Professor include conducting research equivalent to at least 50 per cent of full-time. This includes identifying research questions, developing theoretical ideas, data collecting and choosing research methods. The position also includes actively seeking external funding for their own research and actively participating in the department's research environments and seminar activities. The duties also consist of education in the form of preparation for, implementation of, and follow-up work on various forms of teaching at undergraduate level, advanced level and possibly in doctoral education. Course and programme development, course responsibility, course administration and other pedagogical work are also included in the position. As an Assistant Professor, you will be part of the strategic research programme Building Resilience in Crime Prevention (BRiC): Advancing Professional Knowledge of Child Vulnerability, Drug Markets and Emerging Technologies. BRiC was launched in spring 2026 and is currently in its start-up phase. The programme is funded by the Swedish Research Council and is a collaboration between Lund University, the University of Gothenburg, Malmö University and Linnaeus University. BRiC brings together more than 60 researchers and aims to strengthen scientific and professional knowledge in crime prevention through research conducted in close collaboration with practitioners in social services and the criminal justice system. As an Assistant Professor, you will have the opportunity to help shape this emerging research environment and further develop your research and collaboration networks in the field of crime prevention. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Social Work or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Proficiency in Swedish is not required for this position. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg . We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The position requires: Documented teaching expertise in higher education, Documented, current and relevant experience of practice-based and/or professionally oriented research in social work, In the research plan, applicants are expected to formulate an innovative research project of high scientific quality within crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within Social Work. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and administrative competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Particular emphasis will be placed on documented research merits with clear relevance to social work. Particular emphasis will also be placed on the quality of the submitted research plan. The research plan must clearly demonstrate how the project is connected to and contributes to social work, with a focus on practice-based and/or professionally oriented crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Social Work, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden. Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. To assess whether an applicant is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an evaluation is carried out in accordance with the assessment criteria for employment as Associate Professor (Senior Lecturer) applied at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103) . In such an evaluation, the following additional requirements for promotion apply: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of scientific competence through: High-quality scientific publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, high-quality scientific publications in peer-reviewed books or book chapters are also considered meritorious. Success in obtaining external research funding in national and/or international competition Contributions to the scientific community, for example by undertaking peer-review assignments, serving as an external examiner, faculty opponent, or as a member of an examination committee. Demonstrated progression in relation to the applicant’s scientific merits at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example by achieving Associate Professor qualifications. The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of pedagogical competence through: Teaching experience to at least 1,600 hours. Experience in course development and in various teaching methods. Completion of approved training in higher education pedagogy (courses PIL101–PIL103 or validation approved by the Dean), as well as supervisor training (PIL201 or validation approved by the Dean). Demonstrated progression in relation to the applicant’s pedagogical competence at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example through teaching at different levels, within different subject areas, or through further specialization in previously covered areas. The applicant must have demonstrated collaboration and leadership skills through: Organising and participating in outreach-oriented scientific activities, such as workshops, panels, or conferences. Organising and participating in outreach activities in collaboration with wider society, for example conferences involving policymakers, service users, or organisations, or other forms of public engagement. Demonstrated ability to lead and organise research projects, educational activities, or other initiatives. Taking collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in review panels and working groups. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: Sound professional judgement, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate and contribute to the development of the organisation. Language requirements: Proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR). This must be demonstrated by a passing result on an approved language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that such a test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. The position is based in Gothenburg and requires the successful applicant to reside in and be registered in Sweden. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews and trial lectures will be held from mid-December to the beginning of January. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Therese Wissö, therese.wisso@socwork.gu.se and Tobias Davidsson, tobias.davidsson@socwork.gu.se. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Ebba Ståhl, HR Officer, ebba.stahl@socwork.gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. All stated merits must be supported by certificates. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The following documents should be attached to the application: Curriculum vitae/CV , including a full list of publications and a statement of external funding received (including amounts). Research plan , not exceeding 3 pages. The plan must clearly relate to crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context, and be capable of being developed into at least one competitive research funding application. A description of your scientific qualifications. a) A list of your five most significant scientific publications. For co-authored publications, the applicant’s contribution must be clearly specified. Please indicate your role and the extent of your contribution. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but not yet published, a certificate of accepted publication must be attached. b) A brief statement of your scientific merits and research profile (maximum 2 pages). Full electronic copies of up to five scientific publications. A statement of your teaching qualifications containing: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught at undergraduate, advanced (master's), and doctoral levels. b) A description of your pedagogical competences and qualifications, including information on their scope and quality. c) A description of your teaching material production (textbooks and other teaching material). d) A pedagogical self-reflection on your teaching philosophy and teaching practice (maximum 3 pages). e) Certificates documenting your training in higher education pedagogy, if applicable. f) Certificates of your teaching experience from a Head of Department/Director of Studies or equivalent at at least one university or department. Complete copies of a maximum of three works from your teaching materials production , if applicable. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and instructions for students (maximum 3 items). Please refer to these documents in your statement of teaching merits and qualifications under section 5.b. Doctoral degree certificate . Other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application . Publications that cannot be submitted digitally must be sent in two identical copies to: University of Gothenburg, Department of Social Work, Att: Ebba Ståhl, Box 720, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Please state the reference number: PAR 2026/549. Application deadline : 30 October 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41377": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in educational work",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in educational work For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in educational work Reference number PAR 2026/669 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in educational work, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas; subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in educational work will be based at the Department of Education and Special Education. Subject area Educational work Subject area description Educational work is a subject that examines various perspectives and aspects of work within educational institutions in society. Research in educational work addresses issues relevant to educational stakeholders, professional groups and organizations. This includes the conditions under which teachers and students engage in teaching and learning, both in practice and in relation to policy and educational reforms. Duties A position as assistant professor in educational work aims to develop the employee’s pedagogical and academic competence for further career development; therefore, the role is evenly divided between research 50 % and teaching 50 %. The research will be conducted in collaboration with senior researchers at the Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools and forms part of the research programme to be conducted within the strategic research area ‘Excellence in Schools’. The Assistant Senior Lecturer is expected to be active in one or more of the department’s research environments and to contribute to the department’s academic development. The research role involves independently conducting and developing research in the field of pedagogical practice, publishing academic articles and actively seeking external research funding in line with the department’s strategic research programme, e.g. EU funding. The majority of the department’s teaching responsibilities are within the teacher training programmes, but teaching also takes place within standalone programmes and courses, as well as in commissioned education. The teaching remit may include teaching, supervision and assessment at undergraduate and advanced levels and, where relevant, at postgraduate level. Course coordination, course development, pedagogical development work and collaboration may also be included. Teaching at the department is mainly conducted on campus and in Swedish, although some teaching in English does take place. Proficiency in spoken and written English and Swedish, or another Scandinavian language, is therefore required. Staff are also expected to be regularly present on campus and to contribute actively to a stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networkst For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise A teaching qualification or experience of working as an educator Experience of activities specific to teacher education, such as school-based placement Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities [and suitability] required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Education and Special Education, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Alli Klapp, alli.klapp@ped.gu.se , tel. 031-786 2770 If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer, Ivana Nikolic, ivana.nikolic@gu.se , tel. 031-786 93 24. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other written works that cannot be submitted digitally with the application must be sent in duplicate to: University of Gothenburg Department of Education and Special Education Att: Ivana Nikolic BOX 300, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Please mark the envelope with the reference number for the position: PAR 2026/669. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with ve"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41287": {
      "title": "Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods Reference number PAR 2026/645 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) teaching and research activities stretch from alpine ecosystems, through forests, cultivated land and streams, and into the marine environment. In these environments, we study different levels of biological organization, from genes, individuals, and populations to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics, and conservation, and integrate across these fields to understand past and present ongoing evolutionary processes and the impact of anthropogenic changes on the environment. The strategy of the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences is to foster strong internationally networked research and to promote excellence in teaching. This position is further co-funded by the Strategic Research Area: Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services in a Changing Climate (BECC; www.becc.lu.se/ ), which is a cooperation between Lund University and the University of Gothenburg. BECC brings together scientists from natural and social sciences to perform research essential in understanding the impacts of climate change on biodiversity and ecosystem services, as well as measures to mitigate and adapt to these changes. This position is particularly linked to the BECC grand challenges “Biodiversity under Global Change” and “Ecosystem Services under Global Change” ( https://www.becc.lu.se/research-imperative-questions-our-time/becc-grand-challenges ). BioEnv also hosts internationally prominent research in biodiversity and hosts the Gothenburg Global Biodiversity Centre (GGBC; https://www.gu.se/en/ggbc-global-biodiversity ), which promotes research and outreach. BioEnv and GGBC also facilitate access to several large biological collections spanning wide areas of the world and the tree of life. Subject area Ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods Subject area description The subject area encompasses ecology, evolution, diversity, and conservation of terrestrial arthropods. This includes studies of taxonomy, systematics, phylogenetics, species interactions, and evolutionary processes, as well as the ecological roles of arthropods in natural and managed ecosystems. Research and supervision tasks may be field- and/or collections-based and contribute to understanding the distribution, diversity, conservation and the role of arthropods in the functioning of terrestrial ecosystems and ecosystem services in a changing climate. Duties The main purpose of the position is to enable the successful candidate to establish an independent and internationally competitive research profile in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods and to develop the scientific and pedagogical qualifications required for promotion to Senior Lecturer within 5 years. The research focus can encompass ecology, evolution, systematics or other aspects concerning terrestrial arthropods. Research can be field- and/or collection-based. Research tasks include developing an independent research program in the ecology and evolution of invertebrates. You are expected to apply for external, national and international grants and build and lead a research group, as well as supervise PhD students. Teaching tasks are conducted at undergraduate and graduate levels within the biological and environmental science programs and are conducted in both Swedish and English. You are expected to teach about ecology and evolution, with a focus on Scandinavian terrestrial arthropods. Teaching might also include ecology, evolution and conservation as well as other aspects of biology and environmental sciences. Teaching forms include traditional, in-the-field, collection-based and other forms of lectures and exercises, as well as grading reports, theses , and examinations. Teaching occurs both at the department and in collaboration with several other departments and faculties (also see the ClimBEco Graduate Research School ). In addition to lectures and exercises, course organization is also part of the job. Other tasks include supervision of first- and second-cycle degree projects in biological and environmental science. As a part of a larger team, you are expected to take an active role in the development and renewal of teaching at the department in both form and content. Contributions to the development of master’s level courses in ecology and related areas will be welcomed by the department. The successful candidate is also expected to participate in and contribute to the development of doctoral-level education. Teaching will initially comprise approximately 20% of full-time employment. The distribution between research, teaching and other duties may develop during the period of employment in accordance with the needs of the Department and the career development of the successful candidate. The position further includes research administration and project management, departmental service, societal collaboration and interaction, and participation in relevant research networks and research infrastructures. Competence development is included in the position, particularly training in teaching and learning in higher education and supervision corresponding to 20 higher education credits (PIL101, PIL102, PIL103 and PIL201, or equivalent, Courses offered in English | The PIL Unit ). For candidates who do not speak Swedish, competence development in Swedish is also included. The holder of the position is expected to develop independence as a researcher and to gain merits, both scientifically and pedagogically, to meet the eligibility requirements for a position as senior lecturer, prior to which the application for a docentship (Sve. ‘docentur’) is a milestone. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or related fileds or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Broad knowledge in ecology, evolution, taxonomy, and phylogeny, of  terrestrial arthropods is required. The following are meritorious: Knowledge of European and/or Scandinavian arthropod taxa Faunistic knowledge of multiple arthropod orders. Knowledge of species interactions and conservation biology Experience in applying an ecosystem services perspective regarding terrestrial arthropods Documented education in higher education didactics Documented ability to cooperate internally and externally Documented capacity for academic collegial cooperation and external collaborations with the society at large. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations and should be tightly linked to the subject area. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (e.g. lectures, seminars, field- and collection- based teaching, supervision, etc) at undergraduate, graduate and doctoral levels (meritorious but not required) within the subject area of the position and with emphasis on the ability to teach about Scandinavian terrestrial arthropods. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of courses and/or study programmes and instruction, consequently improving the teaching quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently (up to seven years). The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. During the employment period, no later than 6 months before it ends, the Assistant professor is expected to apply to be promoted to a permanent position as senior lecturer. It is to be noted that the application for promotion must be made within the same subject in which the applicant is employed. The requirements for promotion to senior lecturer can be found in the procedures of the Faculty of Science and Technology: https://s3.gu.se/earkiv-publikt/GU1816-1319333663-232803.pdf Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place December 14. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Åsa Arrhenius, head of department +46 31 786 2625 asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se Søren Faurby, senior lecturer +46 736 422 482 soren.faurby@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should include: Curriculum Vitae/CV, including a complete list of publications and record of received research grants (with amounts) and a link to profile in Inaturalist or similar platform. A research plan. The plan must clearly link to the research area for the position and must be able to be formulated into at least one competitive application for research funding. A description of your scientific qualifications including : a) A list of your top five scientific publications. Indicate your role and the extent of your work in relation to publications co-authored with others. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, a certificate must be attached confirming that the publication has been accepted for publication. b) A brief statement of your scientific qualifications, your research profile and your research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your top scientific publications. A description of your teaching qualifications including : a) a list of the courses and educational programs you have developed and taught (including courses and programs at undergraduate level, advanced level and postgraduate level. b ) A brief statement of your teaching credentials and qualifications with information on both scope and quality, including a pedagogical self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages) c ) certificate of completed university pedagogical education. d ) Certificate of your teaching experience from head of department, director of studies or equivalent at at least one university/one department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, literature lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions to students (max. 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your statement of your teaching credentials and qualifications (4b). Diploma Doctoral Degree. Other certificates and documents that you wish to refer to. Scientific publications, writings, CVs and similar that are not available in electronic form should be sent to:  Maria Thell, Box 463, SE40530 Gothenburg, Sweden. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=40911": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more Reference number PAR 2026/538 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental policy, corruption and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the leading department in Sweden and among the top 10–15 in Europe in its subject area, according to the 2024 Shanghai Ranking of Academic Subjects. We organise a large number of research seminars and workshops each year within the department’s various research areas. We offer courses at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have approximately 160 staff members. For more information about the Department, visit our website The Department of Political Science, University of Gothenburg Subject area Political Science Subject area description Political Science is the study of politics. Duties The role involves research amounting to at least 50 per cent of a full-time position. The Assistant Professor is free to choose research topics, develop theoretical ideas, collect data and select research methods. The role includes actively seeking external funding for their own research and actively participating in the department’s research environments and seminar activities. Active participation in the international research community is also expected, through attendance at conferences and international publication. The duties also include teaching, course coordination, supervision and assessment in undergraduate and postgraduate courses, and possibly also in postgraduate education, corresponding to at least 30 per cent of a full-time position. Teaching on most programmes and courses at the department may be required. The duties also include course and programme development, as well as participation in the department’s educational development work. The Department of Political Science is actively involved in public debate, and its responsibilities include initiating and actively engaging in collaboration with the wider community and promoting the practical application of research findings. Management duties, teaching duties and other duties within the departmental, faculty and university collegial forums may, in the long term, form part of the role. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in the subject area of the position or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Political Science, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. In order to assess whether an applicant is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an assessment is carried out in accordance with the criteria for appointment as a senior lecturer applicable at the University of Gothenburg, in accordance with the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103) . In such an assessment, the following requirements for promotion also apply: Scientific competence must be demonstrated through high-quality scientific publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. Publication in peer-reviewed books or book chapters is considered an asset. The applicant must also have secured external research grants through national or international competitive processes, taken on responsibilities within the academic community through, for example, review assignments, acting as a faculty examiner or serving on an examination board, and demonstrated progression in relation to their qualifications at the start of their appointment, for example by attaining the qualification of docent. Pedagogical competence must be demonstrated through at least 1,600 hours of teaching, experience of course development and various teaching methods, as well as a passed university pedagogy course equivalent to PIL101–PIL103, and supervisor training equivalent to PIL201, or a decision by the Dean regarding validation. The applicant must also have demonstrated pedagogical progression, for example through teaching at different levels, within different subject areas, or through specialisation in previous areas. Achievements in research and teaching carry the most weight in promotion decisions and are assessed equally. The applicant must also have demonstrated a capacity for collaboration and leadership by organising and participating in outreach scientific activities and activities in collaboration with the wider community, shown an ability to lead and organise scientific projects, teaching or other activities, and taken collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in assessment or working groups. With regard to the assessment criterion of suitability, consideration is given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to carry out the duties included in the post, and the ability to collaborate and contribute to the development of the organisation. Applicants must also meet the Swedish language requirement of at least level B2 according to the CEFR, as demonstrated by a passed language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. The interviews are scheduled to take place between 17 December 2026 and 8 January 2027. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Lena Wängnerud, Head of the Department, lena.wangnerud@pol.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Helena Jensen, HR Officer, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here . Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. We ask that you submit your application in English, as it will be assessed by external experts whose working language is English. Please attach the following to your application: Résumé/CV, including complete list of publications and a list of external research grants obtained (with amounts). A description of your research qualifications containing: a) A list of your top five scholarly publications. Please indicate your role and the scope of your contribution for publications co-authored with others. Co-author certificates must be attached; they must contain information about each author's contributions (quantitatively and in substance) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but not yet published, a certificate must be attached proving that the article or book has been accepted for publication. b) A brief account of your research qualifications, your research profile, and your research plans for the coming years. (Max. 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of your top five scholarly publications. A description of your teaching qualifications containing : a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught (including courses and programmes at first-, second- and third-cycle levels b) A brief account of your teaching qualifications with information on scope and quality, including a self-reflection on teaching and learning in higher education. (Max. 2 pages.) c) Evidence (certificates of completion) of completed courses in teaching and learning in higher education. d) Certification of your teaching experience from the head of department, director of studies or equivalent at a minimum of one university/department where you have taught. Electronic copies of teaching materials such as course syllabuses, reading lists, examination tasks, lecture PowerPoint slides, and instructions for students (maximum 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your educational achievements and teaching qualifications (4b). PhD Diploma Other certificates and documents that you wish to cite. Scholarly publications, articles, CVs, and the like that are not available in electronic form should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Political Science Attention: Helena Jensen Box 711 SE-405 30 Göteborg Sweden Applications must be received by: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41227": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Mathematics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Mathematics Reference number PAR 2026/400 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology has about 200 employees and is one of the largest departments of mathematical sciences in the Nordic countries. The department consists of three divisions: Algebra and Geometry, Analysis and Probability, Applied Mathematics and Statistics. We contribute in a significant way to society by our extensive research and education. The working environment at the department is supportive, stimulating, and international, with a continuous flow of international guests. Equality and diversity are substantial foundations in all our activities. More information about us is available on the website: http://www.chalmers.se/en/departments/math/ This is a \"tenure-track\" position which comes with a start-up package that includes funding for a doctoral student, as well as funding for travel and inviting guest researchers. Subject area Mathematics Subject area description We are looking for an Assistant Professor in mathematics to contribute to the department's research activities at the division of Algebra and Geometry. Division of Algebra and Geometry | Chalmers The new employee is expected to independently develop and conduct research at a high international level. The position also includes teaching at 20%, this corresponds to one course including examination, a total of about 10 weeks, per year. We expect the new employee to contribute to strengthening and complementing the research in mathematics conducted at the division of Algebra and Geometry. The department has dedicated teachers with extensive and broad experience in all aspects of undergraduate and postgraduate education in mathematics. We are appreciated by students and continuously seek opportunities for pedagogical development. Our ambition is that our teaching in mathematics will be conducted at the highest international level. Duties The duties include developing and conducting research in mathematics, supervising students (at bachelor's, master's and doctoral levels), actively seeking external grants, participating in the department's activities through administrative assignments, teaching mathematics at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Communication and collaboration skills are important as many of your tasks will be carried out in collaboration with others. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. A PhD or equivalent foreign degree in a subject area relevant to the position is a requirement. Primary consideration should be given to applicants who have been awarded a PhD or achieved equivalent expertise no more than seven years before the application deadline for the position as Assistant Professor. Applicants who were awarded a PhD or achieved equivalent expertise earlier may also be considered if there are special grounds. Such grounds include leave due to illness, parental leave, or other similar circumstances. The reasons for the absence, its dates, and its extent must be clearly specified in the application and supported by appropriate documentation. The official language at the University of Gothenburg is Swedish and teaching is normally in Swedish at bachelor's level and in English at higher levels. Therefore, good language skills and the ability to teach in English are required. Assessment criteria Primary consideration will be given to applicants’ scientific qualifications, although pedagogical skills will also be taken into account. In the assessment of scientific proficiency, particular attention will be paid to research documented through high-quality publications. Quality is considered more important than quantity. Successful research after the PhD and international scientific collaboration are considered merits. In the assessment of pedagogical merits, particular emphasis will be placed on the ability to plan and teach courses in mathematics. The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. International experience, as well as experience from academic environments other than the one in which the doctoral degree was obtained, is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Mathematical Sciences, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The position comes with a start-up package which includes funding for a PhD student as well as for own travel and to invite guests, thereby allowing the candidate to start building their own research group. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. The assessment criteria for promotion as a teacher are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers. (GU 2025/1411) Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place on December 17. Contact information Bernt Wennberg, Head of Department, tel +46 (0)31-772 5326, email wennberg@chalmers.se Daniel Persson, Head of Division, tel +46 (0)31-772 3174, email danper@chalmers.se Lars Martin Sektnan, Head of Unit, tel +46 (0)31-772 3719, email sektnan@chalmers.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the ‘Apply’ button. In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. Together with your application, the following should be attached: Personal letter. CV, including list of publications. Certificate of doctoral degree and other documents that you wish to be considered. Copies of your most relevant publications in full format (maximum six publications) together with motivation for your choice and an account of your contribution to the publications. A 4-year research plan with a description of current and planned research as well as other planned activities relevant to this position (maximum four pages). Description of pedagogical expertise, including information about the extent and level of teaching done and evaluations of its quality. It should also include a short pedagogical reflection (maximum four pages). Description of your experience of leadership, administration and cooperation with the surrounding community. Two references whom we can contact. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Mathematical Sciences Att: Pernilla Junedahl Chalmers tvärgata 3 S-412 96 Gothenburg Sweden Stating PAR 2026/400. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41350": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Health Promotion",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Health Promotion For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Health Promotion Reference number PAR 2026/526 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in Health Promotion, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas—subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in Health Promotion will be based at the Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science. Subject area Health Promotion Subject area description Health promotion is concerned with understanding how health and well-being can be promoted. Research in this field addresses a broad range of issues related to health promotion practice. It is interdisciplinary and encompasses a variety of theoretical approaches as well as both qualitative and quantitative methods. An important aim is to generate knowledge and expertise to support the development, implementation, and evaluation of health-promoting interventions. The research focuses primarily on food and physical activity. The health promotion research environment at the department also shares a strong commitment to exploring how participation and empowerment can be promoted and strengthened. Duties You will also be part of the interdisciplinary research environment in health promotion at the Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science. The work addresses societal challenges related to pupils´ dietary habits, physical activity, learning, and well-being, and aims to develop, test, and evaluate didactic models and educational programmes that can promote healthy and sustainable lifestyles. The Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science develops knowledge for a thriving world through food, nutrition, and sport. Our mission is to conduct world-class education and research in food and nutrition science and sport science. Research at the Department is characterised by a strong connection to education, interdisciplinary collaboration, and close engagement with wider society. The main focus of the Department’s research is to develop and disseminate scientific knowledge and professional expertise that can contribute to the effective promotion of sustainable habits relating to sport, physical activity, food, and meals. The Assistant Professor is expected to contribute to research and the development of teaching in close collaboration with schools and teachers, with a particular focus on integrating subjects such as Home and Consumer Studies, Physical Education and Health, and other school subjects. The aim is to develop, test, and evaluate teaching models that promote students’ learning, well-being, and sustainable lifestyles. The position offers excellent opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration within the educational science research environments at the University of Gothenburg. As an Assistant Professor, you are expected to: develop an independent research profile in health-promoting and interdisciplinary teaching in schools, with a focus on students’ dietary habits, physical activity, learning, and well-being; apply for external research funding to support research projects and doctoral students; publish scientific work in collaboration with senior researchers in food and nutrition science and/or sport science; contribute to the academic environment of the Department and the research field; plan, deliver, and evaluate teaching within relevant parts of the Department’s educational programmes. In addition to teaching within the Faculty’s teacher education programmes, you will teach in one or more of the Department’s bachelor’s programmes. Teaching in other programmes, courses, or continuing professional development activities may also be included. Teaching duties may also involve supervising students at different levels (first-cycle, second-cycle, and doctoral level) and examining degree projects. Course leadership and administrative duties are also included in the position. Teaching within the department is conducted primarily on campus and predominantly in Swedish, although teaching in English also occurs. Applicants are therefore expected to possess excellent oral and written communication skills in English and Swedish, or another Scandinavian language. Staff members are also expected to maintain a substantial on-campus presence in order to contribute to a vibrant and stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who holds a doctoral degree in Food and Nutrition, Sport Science or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking applicants with a demonstrated ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networks For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities and suitability required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer (Universitetslektor) An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Applicants’ eligibility will be assessed after the application deadline. The documents of applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will then be sent to external experts for assessment. In addition to external expert assessment and interviews, the selection process may include trial lectures and reference checks. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Professor Frode Slinde Head of Department Tel: +46 (0)31 786 4220 Email: frode.slinde@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Anneli Svensson HR Officer Tel: +46 (0)31 786 2466 Email: anneli.svensson@gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previ"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41059": {
      "title": "Promotion to Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social m For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social media Reference number PAR 2026/490 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professor with an international background at the end of 2026. The university’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. This position is based at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) within the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg. It is one of the leading research environments in the Nordic countries in the field of media and communication studies, with particular strengths in areas such as political communication, news media, and journalism. Subject area Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social media. Subject area description Media and Communication Studies is a broad and interdisciplinary field that examines the significance of media and communication in society. The field explores how communication is created, disseminated, interpreted, and influences individuals, organizations, and societies, as well as how it is shaped by social, cultural, political, economic, and technological conditions. Research within the field may encompass everything from everyday communication to crisis communication, political communication, strategic communication, and journalism, as well as media such as traditional mass media, social media, alternative media, and digital platforms. The specialization of this position is in digital and social media, with a focus on their societal role and significance. This entails the study of the internet, digital media, social media, and digital communication, as well as the application of relevant quantitative and qualitative methods—including data-driven and computational methods—to investigate the production, dissemination, and consumption of digital media and digital information. It could apply to any of the ongoing research themes at JMG, for example focusing on online misogyni, the role of digital media in breeding conspiracy thinking, or the role of digital media in processes of democratic erosion. Research Areas at JMG. Duties The duties associated with this position include research, teaching, and engagement with the wider community. The position includes an entitlement to research time corresponding to at least 60% of the appointment. The successful candidate is expected to conduct research and actively seek external funding for their own research. They are also expected to participate actively in and contribute to the department’s research environment and seminar activities, as well as to engage with the international research community through participation in conferences and publication in international scholarly outlets. Teaching responsibilities include courses at the undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral levels. Teaching and assessment are conducted in either Swedish or English, depending on the courses involved. The duties also include pedagogical development and the administrative tasks associated with an academic position. Teaching at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) is campus-based, and staff members are expected to be present on campus in order to contribute to a vibrant and stimulating academic environment. JMG collaborates with a wide range of external partners for example DEMICOM, Mid Sweden University, Hamrin-stiftelsen, Medier och Demokrati och Tore G. Wärenstams stiftelse, and the position includes participation in the department’s external engagement activities. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or has attained equivalent academic qualifications in Media and Communication Studies, or in a related field with a clear focus on Media and Communication Studies, within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an assistant professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise must be demonstrated through independent research characterized by a high degree of ability to formulate research problems, theoretical and methodological awareness, and analytical skill. Research expertise is further demonstrated through scholarly publications and ongoing participation in national and/or international research collaborations. In the assessment of scholarly merit, particular weight will be given to documented research accomplishments relating to digital and social media (see above). Documented experience in the use of mixed methods, combining quantitative and qualitative approaches, is considered meritorious. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is further placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious.  Consideration will also be given to how well the applicant’s research relates to and complements the research and research groups already existing within the department. Research at JMG. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Completion of further training in higher education pedagogy is considered meritorious. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of journalism, media and communication at the University of Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor has the right to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Associate Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that the individual meets the eligibility requirements for such a position and is also deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established assessment criteria for promotion to the position of Associate Senior Lecturer. To determine whether the applicant has achieved sufficient qualifications for promotion, an assessment will be conducted in accordance with the assessment criteria for employment as an Associate Senior Lecturer applicable at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). In addition, the following promotion requirements apply: Research expertise must be demonstrated through high-quality scholarly publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. Publications in peer-reviewed books or book chapters are considered meritorious. The applicant must also have actively applied for external research funding in national and international competitive calls. Success in obtaining such funding carries considerable weight in the assessment. Furthermore, the applicant must have contributed to the scholarly community through activities such as serving as a peer reviewer, acting as an external examiner (faculty opponent) at a doctoral thesis defence, or serving as a member of a doctoral examination committee. The applicant must also demonstrate progression in relation to the qualifications held at the time of appointment, for example by having attained the qualifications required for appointment as a docent. Teaching expertise must be demonstrated through at least active participation in teaching, experience of course development and different teaching methods, completion of qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education equivalent to PIL101–PIL103, and completion of supervisor training equivalent to PIL201, or a decision by the Dean recognising equivalent qualifications. In assessing an application for promotion, primary emphasis is placed on research qualifications and secondary emphasis on teaching qualifications. The applicant must also have demonstrated collaboration skills and leadership ability by, for example, participating in collaboration activities with the wider community, demonstrating the ability to lead and organise research projects or other activities, and assuming collegial responsibility within the department, for example through participation in assessment panels or working groups. With regard to the assessment criterion of suitability, consideration will be given to sound professional judgement, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department's activities. The applicant must also fulfil the Swedish language requirement at a minimum of CEFR level B2. Compliance with this requirement must be demonstrated either by passing an approved language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department confirming that a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/employment-benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during December 2026 and the beginning of January 2027. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Marie Grusell, Head of Department marie.grusell@jmg.gu.se or Jesper Strömbäck, Assistant Head of Department jesper.stromback@jmg.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Sofia Hallsten, HR-officer sofia.hallsten@jmg.gu.se Trade Union Representatives Information about trade union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application To apply for this position, please submit your application through the University of Gothenburg's online recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure that the application is complete, in accordance with the instructions in this advertisement, and submitted to the University no later than the closing date. As the applications will be assessed by external experts whose working language is English, applicants are requested to submit all application documents in English. The application must include: Curriculum vitae/CV , including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding received (including amounts). Research plan. The plan must be clearly linked to the research area of the position and should be suitable for development into at least one competitive research grant application. A description of your research qualifications including: a) A list of your five most significant research publications. State your role and the extent of your contribution to publications co-authored with others. Co-author statements must be attached; these must include information on each author’s contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming its acceptance for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research merits, research profile and research plans for the next few years. (Max. 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most significant research publications. A description of your teaching qualifications including: a) a list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught (including courses and programmes at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle level). b) A brief account of your teaching merits and qualifications, providing information on both scope and quality, including a reflection on your own teaching practice. (Max. 2 pages.) c) documentation of completed training in teaching and learning in higher education. d) Certification of your teaching experience from the Head of Department, Director of Studies or equivalent at at least one university/department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions for students (max. 3 supporting documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your teaching merits and qualifications. Doctoral degree certificate Other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Research publications, other written works, CVs and similar documents that are not available in electronic form should be sent to: Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) Attn: Sofia Hallsten Box 710 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Closing date for applications: 30 October 2026 The University of Gothenburg is committed to promoting equal opportunities, equality, and diversity, and values the contributions that a diverse workforce brings to the organisation. The University applies individual salary setting. In accordance with the regulations of the Swedish National Archives, the University is required to retain application documents for two years following the appointment decision. If an applicant specifically requests the return of their application documents, they will be returned after the two-year retention period has expired. Otherwise, the documents will be disposed of in accordance with the applicable regulations. To recruitment agencies and advertising sales representatives: The University of Gothenburg has contracted recruitment advertising services and therefore respectfully declines all direct contact from recruitment and staffing agencies, as well as from advertising sales representatives. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the q"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41172": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution Reference number PAR 2026/537 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Sociology and Work Science is home to one of Sweden's leading research groups in criminology. In conflict resolution, researchers and practioners work together in a well-integrated environment focused on empirical studies of crime and conflict. The Department has approximately 110 employees and conducts internationally regocnized in areas such as social inequality, work, organizations, welfare, and education in close collaboration with the wider community. For more information, please visit the department’s website . Subject area Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution Subject area description The field encompasses sociological research on crime, social control, conflict, conflict management, conflict resolution, and related processes that contribute to the understanding of contemporary societies. The position is intended to strengthen the department's research and teaching within these areas and to contribute to the development of internationally competitive research environments. Duties The Assistant Professor is expected to conduct research of the highest international standard within criminology, conflict resolution, or closely related areas of sociological research. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop an independent research profile with the potential to attain international recognition and leadership in the field. The successful candidate is expected to: develop and lead an independent research agenda publish in leading international academic journals and other scholarly outlets actively pursue external research funding establish and develop national and international research collaborations contribute to the department's research environment teach at first-cycle (undergraduate), second-cycle (Master's) and doctoral levels contribute to research-based development and academic advancement of the department's educational in conflict resolution Particular emphasis will be placed on the candidate’s ability to strengthen the scientific foundation of the department's activities in conflict resolution by integrating current research with education and knowledge development in the field. An appointment as an Assistant Professor includes research duties amounting to at least 50% of the total working time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Gothenburg, Sweden Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. To assess whether a candidate is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an evaluation will be conducted in accordance with the assessment criteria for appointment as Associate Professor (Senior Lecturer) at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). The following additional requirements shall also apply when assessing eligibility for promotion: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of research excellence through: publication of high-quality research in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, publication of high-quality research in peer-reviewed books or book chapters will be considered a merit. success in obtaining external research funding in national and/or international competition. contributions to the scholarly community, for example through service as a peer reviewer, external examiner of doctoral theses, or member of an examining committee for doctoral dissertations. demonstrated progression in research qualifications during the period of employment as Assistant Professor, for example through achieving the qualifications required for appointment as Docent (Associate Professor). The applicant must have demonstrated teaching excellence through: teaching experience equivalent to at least 1,600 hours. experience in course development and in a variety of teaching and learning formats. completion of accredited training in teaching and learning in higher education (PIL101-PIL103 or equivalent qualifications validated by decision of the Dean), as well as doctoral supervision training (PIL201 or equivalent qualifications validated by decision of the Dean). demonstrated progression in teaching competence during the period of employment as Assistant Professor, for example through experience of teaching at different educational levels, across different subject areas, or through further development and specialization within previous areas of teaching. The applicant must have demonstrated engagement, collaboration, and leadership through: organising and participating in outward-facing scholarly activities, such as workshops, panels, or conferences. organising and participating in outreach and engagement activities in collaboration with stakeholders outside academia, such as conferences involving policymakers, practitioners, or civil society organisations, or other activities directed towards the wider public. demonstrating the ability to lead and coordinate research projects, educational activities, or other forms of academic work. contributing to the collegial governance and development of the department, for example through active participation in the department's seminar series, evaluation committees, working groups, or similar bodies. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to carry out the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate effectively and contribute to the development of the department and its activities. The applicant must meet the following language requirement: proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of CEFR level B2. This must be demonstrated either through a passed language test in accordance with the requirements specified here (Common European Framework of Reference for Languages: Language Skills) or through certification from the Head of Department stating that such a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits| University of Gothenburg. If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during week 51-52 (December 16 to December 23, 2026). Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact head of department, Kristian Daneback, kristian.daneback@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact human resource officer Anna Karin Wiberg, ph +46 31 786 3156, anna-karin.wiberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg's recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring their application is complete in accordance with the advertisment and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The following documents must be attached to the application: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of externally funded research grants obtained (including amounts awarded). Research Plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and be formulated so that it could form the basis of at least one competitive external research funding application (maximum 3 pages). A statement of your scientific qualifications, including: a) A list of your five most significant scientific publications. For publications co-authored with others, specify your role and the extent of your contribution. Co-author statements must be enclosed and should describe each author's contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming acceptance for publication must be provided. b) A concise account of your scientific merits, research profile, and research plans for the coming years (maximum 2 pages). Complete electronic copies of up to five of your most significant scientific publications. A statement of your pedagogical qualifications, including: a) A list of the courses and educational programmes you have developed and taught, including courses and programmes at the undergraduate, master's/advanced, and doctoral levels. b) A concise account of your teaching merits and pedagogical qualifications, addressing both the scope and quality of your teaching experience, including a reflective statement on your teaching practice (maximum 2 pages). c) Certificates documenting completed higher education teaching and learning training. d) Documentation of your teaching experience issued by a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent representative at at least one university or academic department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum three supporting documents). These materials should be referenced in your statement of teaching merits and pedagogical qualifications (Section 5b). Doctoral degree diploma. Any additional certificates or documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Publications that cannot be submitted electronically must be sent in two identical hard copies to: University of Gothenburg Department of Sociology and Work Science Attn: Anna-Karin Wiberg Box 720 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please state reference number PAR 2026/537. The application must be received no later than 30 October 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41383": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences Reference number PAR 2026/674 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. Sahlgrenska Academy is the medical faculty at the University of Gothenburg and a leading centre for research and education in medicine, odontology and health sciences. Research, healthcare and innovation come together in a thriving life science ecosystem, supported by close collaboration with Sahlgrenska University Hospital, advanced research infrastructure, and a strong life science and biotechnology sector. Learn more about Sahlgrenska Academy . As part of the University's initiative, Sahlgrenska Academy is recruiting an Assistant Professor within the framework of the Academy's long-term efforts to strengthen life science research and research environments, spanning basic science, translational research and clinical application – as well as education. The position will be based at whichever of the Academy's six departments offers the best environment for the successful candidate's research to develop. Call for 2 assistant professor positions Subject area Clinical research in medicine and health sciences Subject area description The subject area encompasses clinical, translational and health sciences research, with a focus on prevention, diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation and follow-up across the entire continuum of care. It includes research in medicine, health and care sciences, and odontology, as well as research integrating biological, clinical, behavioural and societal perspectives to improve health and healthcare. Duties The position as Assistant Professor in clinical research in medical and health sciences primarily involves conducting independent, internationally competitive research within the subject area. The research must be clinically relevant to human health and disease and contribute to increased knowledge in prevention, diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation and care. The successful candidate is expected to actively develop an independent line of research, seek external research funding and contribute to the development of a strong scientific and interprofessional research environment. Duties also include collaboration with researchers within the department, Sahlgrenska Academy, the healthcare sector, and national and international networks. The position primarily involves research, but also includes teaching at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. Duties may include lectures, seminars, and the supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. The successful candidate is expected to develop their teaching expertise and gradually take on greater responsibility for the planning, delivery and development of teaching within the subject area. A successful candidate who does not have formal training in academic teaching must undertake such training within one year of taking up the position. For promotion, the candidate must have attained proficiency in spoken and written Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) no later than six months before the end of the appointment as Assistant Professor. The University of Gothenburg offers Swedish-language courses, which the successful candidate is required to attend as part of the appointment. All other requirements for promotion set by Sahlgrenska Academy must also be met. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in in the subject area of the position or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on clinical expertise as well as collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on documented experience of clinical research in medicine or health sciences. The candidate must demonstrate the ability to establish an independent profile in clinical research relevant to the subject area of the position. Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. Particular emphasis will be placed on high-quality publications with reputable international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. Greater weight will be given to the quality, originality, and impact of publications than to the total number of publications. Considerable weight will also be given to the ability to obtain external research funding on a competitive basis. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Pedagogical competence must be well documented in a manner that allows the quality of the educational activities to be assessed. Clinical expertise In the assessment of clinical expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on clinical research relevant to the subject area of the position. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence will be assessed based on experience of scientific, clinical and academic leadership, for example through leading, coordinating and developing research or educational activities, as well as through contributions to the development of the department or faculty. Experience of scientific and clinical leadership within national and international networks is particularly meritorious. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer stated by Sahlgrenska Academy. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Jenny Nyström, Professor and Dean at Sahlgrenska Academy jenny.nystrom@neuro.gu.se Johan Wessberg, Professor and Chairman for the Academic Appointments Board johan.wessberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applications must follow the layout of the recommendations for the Guidelines for application to teaching position . Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Please note in particular that the following documents (in swedish or english) must accompany the application: Doctoral degree (If the degree certificate has been translated into English, the certificate in the original language must also be enclosed.) Research plan/Research programme A maximum of 10 selected scientific publications that the applicant want to refer to (in full text) Other certificates /proof /rating proving applicant's qualifications Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg The Sahlgrenska Academy Box 400 405 30 Göteborg SWEDEN The reference number of the application, PAR 2026/674, should be clearly stated when mailing complementary documents. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 In the event of any disagreement between the English and the Swedish versions of any announcement, the Swedish version takes precedence. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41775": {
      "title": "Researcher Position in spintronics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher Position in spintronics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Position in spintronics Reference number PAR 2026/763 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Physics at the University of Gothenburg is located in the center of Gothenburg, with approximately 100 employees. The communication routes are good both nationally and internationally. The research focuses within the fields of Atom- and Molecular Physics, Condensed Matter Physics and Spintronics, and Complex Systems and Biophysics and is performed in an international environment with extensive national and international collaborations. The education programs at the Department include Bachelor- and Master programs in Physics, physics teacher-training programs, as well as outreach courses in Physics aimed for the general public. Gothenburg Physics Centre is a close collaboration with three other departments within the Faculty of Science and Chalmers University of Technology, which creates an innovative environment for all researchers and students at the department. For further information about the Department of Physics, please visit our website at www.physics.gu.se/english. More information about Gothenburg Physics Centre can be found at www.chalmers.se/en/centres/gpc/Pages/default.aspx. About the research group One of the department's most successful research areas is spintronics, with a focus on magneto dynamic phenomena, spin/orbital torque, nanofabrication, and the fundamental physics of spintronic oscillators. We study these devices using magnetic, electrical, and optical methods complemented by GPU-accelerated micromagnetic simulations for modeling. Johan Åkerman's research group is running a major project funded by Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation (KAW), dedicated to developing new spintronic devices and related materials. The department is therefore seeking a researcher to contribute to driving this part of the project forward. Job assignments The primary responsibility of this position is to expand the application space of spin Hall nano-oscillators (SHNOs) across a wide range of material systems. This role requires strong expertise in spintronic material development, as well as microwave measurement and characterization techniques for SHNOs. Key tasks include growth and optimization of materials for SHNOs; design and fabrication of nanostructured devices using nano-lithography techniques; microwave measurement and characterization to evaluate the dynamic properties and performance of SHNO devices; and the implementation of sub-THz measurement techniques using Brillouin Light Scattering (BLS) microscopy measurements. You will work together with PhD students and postdocs, contributing to both experimental design and analysis to drive research forward. Additional responsibilities include publishing findings in scientific articles, contributing to team publications, and presenting results at scientific conferences. Eligibility A person is eligible for employment as a researcher if they hold a Swedish doctoral degree or a foreign qualification deemed equivalent to a Swedish doctoral degree. Qualifications We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated strong scientific skills in the following areas: spintronic material growth development, growth and characterization of ferrimagnetic nitrides, more than 5 years of nanofabrication techniques, and microwave measurement and characterization of spintronic devices. Experience with spin Hall nano-oscillators, as well as electrical and focused m -BLS microscopy measurements is considered a plus. Scientific merit will primarily be assessed based on peer-reviewed publications in scientific journals and presentations at international conferences. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment 1 year, 12 months/year. Extent: 100% Location: Department of Physics, Gothenburg First day of employment: 1 January 2027. Appointment procedure Please apply online. The application shall include: - Cover letter with an explanation of why you apply for the position - CV including scientific publications - Copy of exam certificate - Two referees (name, telephone no, relation) Contact details for the position For further information regarding the position and employment procedure, please contact: Johan Åkerman, Professor, johan.akerman@physics.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg: http://www.gu.se/english/about_the_university/job-opportunities/union-representatives How to apply In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. The application deadline is: 2026-11-06 . The University of Gothenburg promotes equal opportunities, equality and diversity. Salary is determined on an individual basis. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41759": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Psychopharmacology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Psychopharmacology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Psychopharmacology Reference number PAR 2026/808 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for a postdoctoral researcher employment at the Department of Psychopharmacology. The department has a long and strong tradition of research in psychopharmacology, which today encompasses both clinical and experimental studies as well as data analysis of clinical and epidemiological data. Subject area Psychopharmacology Subject area description Psychopharmacology encompasses studies of how drugs affect mood, thoughts and behaviour, as well as how drugs can be used to treat psychiatric disorders. The field brings together knowledge from psychiatry, pharmacology, psychology and neurology to understand how drugs affect brain chemistry and neuronal communication. Job assignments The project concerns the evaluation of the effects and adverse effects of interventions for depression and ADHD through meta-analyses at the study and individual participant levels, with a particular focus on the conditions under which different analytical methods (conventional meta-analysis, network meta-analysis and individual participant data meta-analysis) may produce conflicting results. The postdoctoral researcher will primarily analyse data from clinical trials in which the effects of interventions for depression and ADHD have been evaluated. Depending on the postdoctoral researcher’s research interests, the project may also include experimental work related to the mechanisms of action of psychopharmaceuticals. The project may also include studies of the use of psychopharmaceuticals at the population level and the consequences of such use through the analysis of registry data. The postdoctoral researcher is expected to independently conduct research projects under supervision and report regularly to the rest of the research group. The postdoctoral researcher is also expected to contribute to student supervision, for example as a supervisor of degree project students and/or as a co-supervisor of doctoral students, as well as to teaching. Specific duties include data management, data analysis and data visualisation of data from clinical studies and registries using Python/R, with methods such as machine learning and network meta-analysis. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. To qualify as a postdoctoral researcher for this employment, the applicant must hold a PhD in medicine. Assessment Applicants must demonstrate good proficiency in both English and Swedish, spoken and written. Documented experience of managing and analysing large datasets in R/Python is a requirement. Experience of network meta-analyses, individual participant data meta-analyses, machine learning and statistics is particularly meritorious. Furthermore, experience in the subject areas of psychiatry and psychopharmacology is meritorious. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 2 years, 100% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information If you have questions regarding the employment please contact Associate Senior Lecturer/Researcher Fredrik Hieronymus, e-mail: fredrik.hieronymus@neuro.gu.se . Manager in charge is Professor/Head of Department Michael Winder, phone: +46 31-786 34 77, e-mail: michael.winder@pharm.gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact persons. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please enclose PhD certificate with the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-05 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41753": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Mathematics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Mathematics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Mathematics Reference number PAR 2026/748 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The department of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology is one of the largest mathematics departments in Sweden with about 200 employees. The department has three scientific divisions, Algebra and Geometry, Analysis and Probability Theory, and Applied Mathematics and Statistics. We have an international environment with frequent exchanges with other universities around the world. The department provides a creative and supportive atmosphere with a steady flow of international guests. There are many committed teachers with extensive and broad experience of all aspects of higher education. Our department continuously strives to be an attractive employer. Equality and diversity are substantial foundations in all our activities. We work actively to be a parent-friendly organization. More information about us can be found on our website: Mathematical Sciences | Chalmers At the Division of Algebra and geometry, we conduct research at a high international level in areas such algebraic geometry, complex geometry, number theory and representation theory. We are now looking for a postdoctoral researcher strengthening our research in complex geometry. Subject area Mathematics - Complex Geometry Subject area description This postdoctoral project, which, as part of the larger project Symmetrizations of Kähler manifolds, is supported by the Göran Gustafsson Foundation, aims to study degenerations of special Kähler manifolds, specifically compact Calabi-Yau manifolds, near large complex structure limit points. Recent advances in the study of such degenerations have revealed striking connections of this field with the theory of optimal transport, regularity theory of real Monge-Ampère equations, non-Archimedean geometry and, most recently, the theory of theta functions from the Gross-Siebert programme. The aim of this project is to use these new methods to answer open questions about the regularity of the limits of the associated Kähler metric degenerations. One concrete goal is to understand location and size of the locus of singularities. This is expected to play a key role in understanding metric mirror symmetry. You are expected to collaborate with project leader David Witt Nyström at the institution. Duties You will be part of the research group in complex geometry. You are expected to independently develop new ideas in this collaboration. If the candidate is interested, at most 10% of the time can be spent on teaching and supervision. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Preferred Qualifications Experience with Kähler geometry and the theory of Monge-Ampère equations, as well as a strong background in degenerations of PDEs and optimal transport theory, is highly desirable. Familiarity with the Gross-Siebert programme is also a merit. Assessment Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. The assessment of your application will be based mainly on your scientific skills, and in particular skills relevant to the project as described above. You are expected to provide evidence of excellence in research. On the personal level you should be highly motivated and independent. Your ability to collaborate and communicate will be considered in the assessment. Fluency in English is expected. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, two years Extent: 100% Location: Division of Algebra and Geometry, Department of Mathematical Sciences, University of Gothenburg First day of employment: January 2027 Contact information Dr Lars Martin Sektnan, Head of Unit, Division of Algebra and Geometry sektnan@chalmers.se Phone: +46 31 772 1399 Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Submit the following documents: Personal letter CV, including a complete list of publications and conference talks Research statement (maximum two pages), accounting for your previous experience and future plans Contact details for two reference persons Attested copies of completed education, grades and other certificates Copies of at most five publications Applications must be received by: 5 November 2026 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41736": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral in Pluripotent Stem Cell Models of Human Development",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral in Pluripotent Stem Cell Models of Human Development For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral in Pluripotent Stem Cell Models of Human Development Reference number PAR 2026/796 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: * The Department of Infectious Diseases * The Department of Microbiology and Immunology * The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology * The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. The Guibentif lab is looking to recruit a post-doctoral fellow. We are a European Research Council-funded lab, applying single-cell profiling towards establishing novel pluripotent stem cell-based platforms for the study of human embryonic development and disease. We are affiliated to the University of Gothenburg (GU), one of the largest universities in Northern Europe. Given our affiliation to the Institute of Biomedicine and to the Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research, we are part of a supportive interdisciplinary environment, comprising basic developmental biologists as well as clinical scientists. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research is a joint venture between the GU Faculty of Medicine and the Sahlgrenska University Hospital focused on cancer translational research. Subject area Pluripotent Stem Cell Models of Human Development Subject area description The overall goal of this project is to bridge current in vitro models of human embryonic development with the latest progress in developmental hematopoiesis studied in vivo. This will lead to new platforms to study molecular mechanisms of embryonic development. We harness single-cell genomics and advanced experimental models to determine to what extent in vitro stem cell systems mimic the variety of molecular programmes underlying the emergence of hematopoietic cells at multiple anatomical locations and timepoints in the developing embryo. Duties The selected candidate will be expected to work in a cross-disciplinary team, and to establish proteomics and single-cell profiling analysis workflows to dissect developmental processes modelled in stem cell differentiation cultures. Therefore, we seek a candidate capable of working as part of a diverse team, with an interest in studying molecular drivers of mammalian embryonic development as well as prior experience in aseptic cell culture techniques and molecular biology. We seek a candidate eager to work with others, and to develop leadership and teaching skills by participating in the supervision of junior colleagues. The selected candidate will also be expected to contribute to manuscript writing and will be expected to present their work regularly at group and department meetings. Prior hands-on experience in chromatin immunoprecipitation, next-generation sequencing library preparation, and/or droplet-based single-cell profiling techniques will be an advantage. Applicants having demonstrated skills in proteomics, fluorescence-activated cell sorting, flow cytometry data analysis, or computational skills in next-generation sequencing data analysis will also be considered. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria The candidate selection will be based on the cover letter, Curriculum Vitae, interview and contact with references. See the selection criteria below, in decreasing order of importance: 1 – Expertise in developmental biology, stem cells and/or single-cell profiling techniques 2 – Communication and team work skills 3 – Hands-on experience in chromatin immunoprecipitation, next-generation sequencing library preparation, and/or droplet-based single-cell profiling techniques 4 – Demonstrated skills in human pluripotent stem cell culture and differentiation, proteomics, fluorescence-activated cell sorting, flow cytometry data analysis, and/or computational analysis of next-generation sequencing data. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Selection process How to apply for a position | University of Gothenburg Contact information Please contact: Carolina Guibentif, Researcher, +46 702253076, carolina.guibentif@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: * A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying * A CV including a list of publications * Proof of completed PhD * Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026-11-11 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41728": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Single-cell Genomics of Human Development",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoc in Single-cell Genomics of Human Development For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoc in Single-cell Genomics of Human Development Reference number PAR 2026/792 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: * The Department of Infectious Diseases * The Department of Microbiology and Immunology * The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology * The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. The Guibentif lab is looking to recruit a post-doctoral fellow. We are a European Research Council-funded lab, applying single-cell profiling towards establishing novel pluripotent stem cell-based platforms for the study of human embryonic development and disease. We are affiliated to the University of Gothenburg (GU), one of the largest universities in Northern Europe. Given our affiliation to the Institute of Biomedicine and to the Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research, we are part of a supportive interdisciplinary environment, comprising basic developmental biologists as well as clinical scientists. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research is a joint venture between the GU Faculty of Medicine and the Sahlgrenska University Hospital focused on cancer translational research Subject area Single-cell Genomics of Human Development Subject area description The overall goal of this project is to bridge current in vitro models of human embryonic development with the latest progress in developmental hematopoiesis studied in vivo. This will lead to new platforms to study molecular mechanisms of embryonic development. We harness single-cell genomics and advanced experimental models to determine to what extent in vitro stem cell systems mimic the variety of molecular programmes underlying the emergence of hematopoietic cells at multiple anatomical locations and timepoints in the developing embryo Duties The selected candidate will be working in a cross-disciplinary team, and will apply computational biology approaches to analyse high-throughput next generation sequencing data for uncovering novel drivers of mammalian embryonic development. The selected candidate will be expected to contribute to co-supervision of younger colleagues, to manuscript writing and will be expected to present their work regularly at group and department meetings. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria The candidate selection will be based on the cover letter, Curriculum Vitae, interview and contact with references. Specific selection criteria: 1 – Prior hands-on experience with R and/or Python language, bash shell environment, high performance computing, next-generation sequencing data analysis, and single-cell analysis workflows 2 – Communication and team work skills 3 – Interest and theoretical background in stem cells, developmental biology and/or hematology 4 – Interest in the development and implementation of novel computational workflows to uncover novel biology. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Selection process How to apply for a position | University of Gothenburg Contact information Please contact: Carolina Guibentif, Researcher, +46 702253076, carolina.guibentif@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: * A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying * A CV including a list of publications * Proof of completed PhD * Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026- 11-11 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41745": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/800 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for a Researcher employment at the Department of of Clinical Neuroscience. Duties It is expected that the holder will actively participate in neurological research, mainly with a focus on stroke. This applies both to a more laboratory focus (biomarkers in liquor and blood) as well as to the use of registers and organizational analysis. The holder must also participate actively to coordinate stroke research at Gothenburg University linked to Sahlgrenska University Hospital. Qualifications Demonstrated scientific, pedagogical and clinical skills are mandatory requirements for the employment, as well as a medical degree, specialist competence in neurology and a doctorate in a medical subject or another subject that the employer finds equivalent. It is advantageous to have experience in supervising doctoral students to the degree. Great importance will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 9 months, 20% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have questions regarding the employment please contact Manager in charge Professor/Assistant Head of Department Asgeir Jakola, phone: +46 31 342 97 41, e-mail: jakola.asgeir@gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact person. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please enclose PhD certificate with the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-04 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41693": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Design to HDK-Valand",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Research Assistant Design to HDK-Valand For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Research Assistant Design to HDK-Valand Reference number PAR 2026/789 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and  Academy of Music and Drama. As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is currently seeking a Research Assistant to work with administration and communication within the Business Design Lab, the HEPAHESTUS research project, as well as with research project applications. The position is a fixed-term appointment for eight months. For more information about HDK-Valand, please visit www.hdk-valand.gu.se . Duties The position involves a combination of administrative tasks, communication, and research support. Administrative duties include, among other tasks, transcribing interviews and supporting the development of activities within Business and Design. Communication duties include, among other tasks, organising internal and external communications for the Business and Design Lab. Research support duties include, among other tasks, conducting literature searches and collecting and analysing material for a newly initiated research project on visual evidence, digital infrastructure, and expert work. The work will largely be carried out independently, with some support from research leaders. The position requires flexibility, as new tasks and priorities may arise. Qualifications To be eligible for this position, you must have: An MA or MSc degree in Visual Studies, Fine Art, Organisation Studies, Sociology, International Relations, or a related field Experience with visual practices Experience in communication, preferably including social media Experience in academic reading and writing Proficiency in Swedish and excellent proficiency in English, both spoken and written You are a relatively recent graduate with an interest in pursuing research within academia. You are keen to learn more about academic research and research collaboration. You are well organised, work well with others, and are comfortable working independently. You are also able to adapt easily to changing circumstances. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment (Section 5.1 of the Swedish Employment Protection Act) for a period of eight months. Extent: Full-time (100%). Location: HDK-Valand, Gothenburg. Start date: 1 November 2026, or as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact professor Elena Raviola, elena.raviola@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR function at HDK-Valand, hr@hdk-valand.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives For international applicants, please read the following information: The position requires you to work on site at the Faculty of Fine, Applied and Performing Arts in Gothenburg on several occasions each working week. Remote work from abroad is not possible, as the University of Gothenburg is a Swedish government authority. In Sweden, applicants for a work permit must meet certain maintenance requirements. To find out how these requirements may affect you as an international applicant, please read more about the maintenance requirements for work permits on the Swedish Migration Agency’s website or contact hr@hdk-valand.gu.se . Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 28 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41826": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Cancer research with a focus on pancreatic neoplasia and hepatobiliary malignancies",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Cancer research with a focus on pancreatic neoplasia and hepatobiliary malignancies For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Cancer research with a focus on pancreatic neoplasia and hepatobiliary malignancies Reference number PAR 2026/824 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences is one of six Institutes within Sahlgrenska Academy at the University of Gothenburg. We conduct research and education that puts people's health and quality of life in focus. We represent 16 different research areas and are divided into four sections.  Through a fruitful collaboration with the healthcare and Sahlgrenska University Hospital, we have developed joint projects and meeting places that benefits both health care and research. The Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of surgery, is now searching for 1-2 postdoctoral fellows to work with preclinical and clinical research, mainly concerning pancreatic cancer. The research group is led by Professor Kjetil Søreide, who conducts both clinical and translational research in pancreatic cancer and hepatobiliary malignancies. The newly founded research group will utilize carefully curated clinical datasets and biobanks available, create new translational studies and, will work closely with collaborating groups at Sahlgrenska, but also nationally and internationally. The research group is based at the Institute of Clinical Sciences in the recently opened facilities for Experimental Surgical Research, sharing lab space with other surgical research groups. The Soreide research group works in close vicinity to groups affiliated to the Wallenberg Center for Molecular and Translational Medicine and the Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research (SCCR), which provides good opportunities for career development and network building. SCCR consists of a large network of research group leaders and their team members within the University of Gothenburg and beyond. We are currently seeking 1–2 dedicated postdoctoral researchers with experience in translational/experimental cancer research focused on liquid biopsy (i.e. ctDNA), or spatial transcriptomics or, patient-derived organoids including experience in biostatistics/bioinformatics to join ongoing and upcoming projects in our group focused on improving the understanding of cancer biology, as well as clinical risk stratification, early detection, prediction and prognostication, and clinical outcomes in pancreatic pre-neoplasia, pancreatic cancer and hepatobiliary maligncies. The project is conducted in close collaboration with other clinical and translational researchers, nationally and internationally. Join us to work with unique datasets and advance research on pancreatic cancer, with the aim of improving patient care and clinical outcomes for these hard-to-treat cancers. Subject area Cancer research with a focus on pancreatic neoplasia and hepatobiliary malignancies Subject area description Translational and experimental research on pancreatic cancer and hepatobiliary malignancies, with a focus on liquid biopsy, spatial omics technology and patient-derived organoids (PDOs). Duties The work includes planning and carrying out clinical studies using translational/experimental lab techniques, but also collecting information from already completed studies. Within the project, you will also plan, design and carry out research work aimed at studying microenvironments in tumors using cellular and molecular methods. Other methods include different types of DNA, RNA and protein analyses, as well as work with 3D models and clinical samples. A close collaboration will take place with our preclinical laboratory with advanced methods for the isolation and characterization of exosomes. The work will mainly be carried out at the Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research. You are expected to lead specific research studies largely independently. This work includes all parts of the laboratory experiments and analytical research, including writing statistical analysis plan (SAP), perform statistical analyses of data and manuscript writing. You may also be involved in co-supervising PhD students, contributing to project management, and assisting other group members' research. You may contribute to funding applications and scientific meetings within the project. Eligibility To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria To qualify as postdoc the applicant should hold a PhD in clinical research, biology, biochemistry, biotechnology, molecular biology or equivalent is required. Experience in both clinical and preclinical research is advantageous. Experience with hands-on work and execution in one of either lab technique areas is mandatory for the position, and includes either; i) experience in liquid biopsy research; ii) experience in spatial omics technology; and/or, iii) experience in working with patient-derived organoids. Experience in more than one of these technologies is advantageous but not a strict requirement. The work takes high demands on self-motivation, independence and accuracy. Of great importance is personal qualities such as commitment, initiative and cooperation skills. You are expected to have excellent spoken and written English skills, as well as documented experience in writing and publishing scientific articles in high-impact journals. You have the clinical experience to formulate relevant research questions and interpret results from such studies. Knowledge of Swedish or another Nordic language is a merit. We value independence in designing studies; conducting, maintaining and quality-assure lab protocols and SOPs; conducting statistical analyses; and, leading research projects. We are looking for a driven and independent individual with good organizational skills and the ability to work efficiently. You are collaborative and a team player with strong social skills and empathy. Furthermore, you are curious, adaptable, and willing to learn from other research fields, with a structured yet flexible approach to work. Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 24 months, 100% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of Surgery. Starting date: 2027-02-01 (or, earliest start availability after agreement) Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professor and Senior Consultant Kjetil Soreide, kjetil.soreide@gu.se, phone +46 (0)766 18 25 08 or lab-manager, Associate Professor Johan Bourghart Fagman, johan.bourghardt@gu.se, phone +46 (0)706238876. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application shall include: A short cover letter with description of your experience and what makes you qualified for this particular job A detailed curriculum vitae (CV) A publication list Name and contact information of two references Applications must be received by: 13 November 2026 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41817": {
      "title": "Amanuens/teaching assistant, one or more",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Amanuens/teaching assistant, one or more For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Amanuens/teaching assistant, one or more Reference number PAR 2026/823 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The department of Computer Science and Engineering is a fully integrated department with the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology as principles. The department offer courses in a broad range of subjects. The department of Computer Science and Engineering is looking for amanuens/teaching assistant for spring semester 2027 to work with courses within the fields of Computer Network and Systems, Computing Science, Data Science and AI, Interaction Design and Software Engineering. Job assignments As an amanuens, you will work with helping and supporting teachers with practical elements in the different courses. This could for example be lab and exercise tutoring. You will have regular contact with the teachers about which elements and practical moments you can be of help within. The specific courses for spring 2027 can be found in the linked document: CSE Courses Spring 2027 In the document you will find more information about each course, for example course language and at which campus the course will be given. You will find the courses divided by division in the order; Computer Network and Systems, Computing Science, Data Science and AI, Interaction Design and Software Engineering. Qualifications To be qualified for the position you are an active student at University of Gothenburg or Chalmers University of Technology, preferably studying in one of the institution's BSc or MSc level programmes. To be qualified for the position in a course you should have passed either the same or a similar course. To be qualified for the position you should have good knowledge in the language used in the courses you apply for, that is, English and/or Swedish, both spoken and written. To be able to complete your work as amanuens/teaching assistant you will need to be in Gothenburg, Sweden and be able to perform work tasks at campus Johanneberg or campus Lindholmen. It is a merit to have completed related continuation courses. It is also a merit if you have experience of teaching and are skilled in instructing and helping people in a pedagogical way. We will make an overall assessment where great importance will be placed on personal suitability. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, spring semester 2027 Extent: 10%-20% of full-time Location: Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Gothenburg First day of employment: 2027-01-11, or upon agreement Contact information for the position If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact our Director of Studies Computer Network and Systems - Elias Hällqvist elias.hallqvist@chalmers.se Computing Science - Peter Ljunglöf peter.ljunglof@cse.gu.se Data Science and AI - Birgit Grohe birgit.grohe@cse.gu.se Interaction Design - Olof Torgersson olof.torgersson@chalmers.se Software Engineering - Daniel Strüber danstru@chalmers.se For questions regarding your application or the recruitment process, please contact Jenny Lind jenny.lind@cse.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. Fill in the section about personal information and answer the questions. You also need to upload your CV and a transcript of courses that you have passed (can be downloaded from Ladok). You will be asked to fill out which course-/s you are interested to work in. Please keep the document under Job assignments open so you can copy course code and course name directly from the document to your application. After the deadline, all applications will be processed. Your application need to be submitted by: 2026-11-11 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41811": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in biology with focus on tropical forest ecology and carbon dynamics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in biology with focus on tropical forest ecology and carbon dynamics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in biology with focus on tropical forest ecology and carbon dynamics Reference number PAR 2026/822 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way into the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organisation from genes, individuals, and populations, to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics, and combinations of these fields to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is placed at two different localities: in Natrium at Medicinaregatan 7B in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg marine research station operated by the Marine Infrastructure at the University of Gothenburg. The current employment is based at Natrium. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://medarbetarportalen.gu.se/doktorand/?languageId=100001&skipSSOCheck=true We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Project description Forests gain and lose biomass not only through tree growth and mortality, but also when living trees lose branches or parts of their crowns and subsequently recover. These non-lethal fluxes are largely invisible to conventional forest inventories and are therefore poorly represented in forest carbon budgets. This PhD position is part of TWIG, a five-year ERC Starting Grant project that will combine repeated terrestrial laser scanning (TLS) with long-term forest inventories to directly quantify damage, recovery and biomass redistribution in tropical forests. The doctoral student will analyse environmental and biological drivers of tree-level biomass fluxes and investigate the ecological aftermath of small-scale disturbances. The student will participate in field campaigns, help coordinate measurements with local teams, and analyse multi-temporal 3D data using quantitative, spatial and causal-inference approaches. More specific questions and analytical developments will be refined by the doctoral student together with the supervisor. The research will focus on two intensively monitored, mature tropical forest sites: Amacayacu in the Colombian Amazon and Alto da Figueira in the Brazilian Atlantic Forest. The student will be hosted at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences under the main supervision of Dr. Daniel Zuleta. The doctoral student will work closely with two TWIG postdoctoral researchers, MSc and BSc students, local field teams in Colombia and Brazil, and international collaborators with expertise in tropical forest monitoring, terrestrial laser scanning and vegetation modelling. The project is connected to the strategic research area BECC (Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services in a Changing Climate; http://www.becc.lu.se/ ) and may contribute to international forest research initiatives including ForestGEO, GEO-TREES, and the Alliance for Tropical Forest Science Mortality Working Group. The doctoral student will therefore work in an international research environment with opportunities for cross-training in tropical forest monitoring, 3D remote sensing, and quantitative ecology. Duties The main task is to conduct the PhD thesis work under supervision, which includes development of the PhD student's methodological experience, analytical skills, as well as theoretical depth and breadth. Techniques used within the project may include terrestrial laser scanning, point-cloud and 3D canopy analysis, forest inventory methods, environmental and tree-attribute measurements, spatial statistics, causal inference, and programming in R and/or Python. The studies will be conducted mostly as a combination of tropical field studies and computational analysis. Specific research topics associated with the research project include tropical forest dynamics, structural damage and recovery, tree biomass fluxes, canopy-gap dynamics, biodiversity effects and remote sensing. Education at third-cycle level comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Eligibility Education at third-cycle level requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for education at third-cycle level are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. To meet the specific entry requirements for third-cycle studies, applicants must: have a second-cycle (advanced-level) degree in a relevant* subject area in the natural sciences, or have completed studies for at least 60 higher education credits at a second-cycle level in relevant subject areas in the natural sciences, or have completed a corresponding programme of relevance to the planned third-cycle programme, in Sweden or in another country, or have equivalent qualifications.  *Relevant subject for the planned third-cycle education are biology or environmental sciences. Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the education at third-cycle level. It is a requirement that the applicants have: - Knowledge of ecology, with particular relevance to forest ecology, tree demography, biodiversity and/or ecosystem dynamics - Strong analytical skills in producing, handling and interpreting ecological or environmental data - Quantitative and computational skills, including experience with statistical analysis and programming in R, Python or a comparable environment - Ability to think creatively and to work independently as well as collaboratively in an international research team - Willingness and ability to conduct extended field work in tropical forests in Colombia and Brazil Excellent communication skills in English, both written and spoken, are necessary since we work in an international environment. Other desirable qualifications are: - A MSc or BSc project in a topic relevant to tropical forest ecology, forest dynamics, remote sensing or quantitative ecology - Knowledge of tropical forest ecology, forest inventories, tree demography or carbon dynamics - Experience conducting field work under remote or logistically challenging conditions - Experience with advanced quantitative methods, such as causal inference, Bayesian statistics, hierarchical models or spatial analysis - Experience working collaboratively in field or research teams - Experience with remote sensing, terrestrial laser scanning, LiDAR, 3D point clouds or related spatial data - Experience with scientific writing, a thesis of high quality and/or scientific publication(s) - Experience coordinating field measurements or working with field crews - Language skills in Spanish or Portuguese, or willingness to learn one of these languages - Willingness to teach undergraduate/master students at the host department The application should preferably be written in English and must include: A short cover letter with the applicant’s justification for the application, i.e., that describes how the applicant meets the selection criteria (no more than one page) An attested list of qualifications (CV) (no more than three pages) Examination certificates and a transcript of courses with grades A copy of the Master thesis (or equivalent) Employments certificates and other documents deemed important by the applicant The top ranked candidates will be selected for an interview, which will be held in English and could also be performed digitally. Employment Once you have been admitted for education at third-cycle level, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at third-cycle level for four years. Placed at the department of Biological and Environmental Sciences. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: February 1 st , 2027 or upon agreement. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Contact information For further information please contact: Daniel Zuleta, Associate Senior Lecturer Email: daniel.zuleta@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: https://www.gu.se/en/about/find-staff/danielzuleta Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department Tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science, hr@bioenv.gu.se Union Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande How to apply You can apply to be admitted for education at third-cycle level via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application must be received no later than November 19, 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41806": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Tropical Forest Ecology, Terrestrial Laser Scanning and Forest Biomass Dynamics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Tropical Forest Ecology, Terrestrial Laser Scanning and Forest Biomass Dynamics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Tropical Forest Ecology, Terrestrial Laser Scanning and Forest Biomass Dynamics Reference number PAR 2026/821 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way into the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organisation from genes, individuals and populations, to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics and combinations of these fields in order to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is placed at two different localities: in Natrium at Medicinaregatan 7B in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg marine research station operated by the Marine Infrastructure at the University of Gothenburg. The current position is placed at Natrium. We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Subject area Tropical Forest Ecology, Terrestrial Laser Scanning and Forest Biomass Dynamics Subject area description Forests gain and lose biomass not only through tree growth and mortality, but also when living trees lose branches or parts of their crowns and subsequently recover. These non-lethal fluxes are largely invisible to conventional forest inventories and are therefore poorly represented in forest carbon budgets. This postdoctor position is part of TWIG, a five-year ERC Starting Grant project that will combine repeated terrestrial laser scanning (TLS) with long-term forest inventories to directly quantify damage, recovery and biomass redistribution in tropical forests. The postdoctor will lead the methodological work that converts multi-temporal 3D point clouds into robust estimates of tree- and stand-level biomass change. Duties The main aim of the position is to develop, validate and apply the TLS-to-aboveground-biomass (AGB) workflow that underpins TWIG. The postdoctor will contribute to TLS data acquisition at the project sites. The postdoctor will compare TLS-derived biomass estimates with conventional allometric approaches and develop reproducible stand-level carbon-balance calculations that include branch turnover and regrowth. The position will also quantify differences between TLS-based and traditional inventory estimates of biomass stocks, gains, losses and net fluxes. Field and analytical work will focus on two intensively monitored tropical forests: Amacayacu in the Colombian Amazon and Alto da Figueira in the Brazilian Atlantic Forest. The postdoctor will work closely with the PI, a PhD student, MSc and BSc students, local field teams, and international collaborators. The work will involve large 3D datasets and reproducible computational workflows, including scripted quality control and, when appropriate, parallel processing on high-performance computing infrastructure. The project includes training and scientific exchange with leading TLS researchers and forest ecologists, as well as participation in international meetings and conferences. The successful applicant will contribute to cross-training within the TWIG team and will have opportunities to co-supervise MSc and BSc students working with forest inventory and TLS. The position is strongly collaborative and combines method development, field data acquisition, computational analysis and scientific publication. The postdoctor will be hosted at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences under the main supervision of Dr. Daniel Zuleta. The project is connected to international forest research and monitoring initiatives including ForestGEO, GEO-TREES, the Alliance for Tropical Forest Science Mortality Working Group, and the strategic research area BECC. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Required qualifications for the employment: To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in Biology, Ecology, Forest Engineering or another relevant subject Excellent communication skills, written and spoken, in English are necessary since we work in an international environment. Strong quantitative and computational skills for processing and analyzing large ecological datasets Documented experience with terrestrial laser scanning (TLS) data acquisition and processing Experience with scientific programming or scripting and reproducible data-analysis workflows Willingness and ability to conduct extended field work in tropical countries, including Colombia and Brazil Desirable qualifications for the employment: A strong track record of scientific publications related to terrestrial laser scanning, forest remote sensing, forest biomass, forest structure and/or forest ecology Experience with individual-tree segmentation, quantitative structure models (QSMs), or comparable 3D reconstruction approaches Experience with forest biomass estimation, tree allometry, uncertainty propagation and/or forest inventory data Experience with quantitative analysis and programming in R, Python or similar languages, and/or high-performance or batch-computing workflows Fieldwork experience in tropical forests and under remote or logistically challenging conditions Language skills in Spanish or Portuguese, or willingness to learn one of these languages The application should be written in English and must include: A cover letter with the applicant’s justification for the application, i.e., that describes how the applicant meets the selection criteria (no more than one page) A list of qualifications (CV) (no more than three pages) Certificate of PhD exam Complete list of publications, including submitted and accepted manuscripts The most qualified candidates, in relation to the assessment criteria in the advertisement, are invited for an interview. Employment The employment is a temporary employment for three years, 100 % of full time, placed at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science. Starting date February 1 st , 2027, or upon agreement. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact: Daniel Zuleta, Associate Senior Lecturer Email: daniel.zuleta@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: https://www.gu.se/en/about/find-staff/danielzuleta Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department Tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Publications that cannot be attached to the application in digital format should be mailed in single copy to: Department of Biological and Environmental Science Att: Daniel Zuleta University of Gothenburg Box 461 or 463 S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden Label the envelope \"Postdoctoral Position in Terrestrial Laser Scanning and Forest Biomass Dynamics, PAR 2026/821\". The publications must be received no later than the application deadline. Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ Applications must be received by: November 19, 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41783": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in obstetric, perinatal, and implementation science research",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in obstetric, perinatal, and implementation science research For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in obstetric, perinatal, and implementation science research Reference number PAR 2026/814 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Institute of Clinical Sciences, at the Sahlgrenska Academy is seeking a postdoctor in obstetric, perinatal, and implementation science research. The position is linked to a major research and development program focused on studying the development and implementation of a novel differentiated person-centred model of care for pregnancy, childbirth, and the postnatal period, as well as the evaluation of the model’s effects on clinical outcomes, experiences of care, and staff work environment. The project is conducted in close collaboration with the Departments of Obstetrics and Neonatology at Sahlgrenska University Hospital. The research group is led by Professor Verena Sengpiel and includes both clinicians and researchers with expertise in obstetrics, neonatology, caring science, epidemiology, qualitative research methodology, implementation science, work environment research, person-centred care, and reproductive health. The research environment is based at the University of Gothenburg and Sahlgrenska University Hospital in Gothenburg, Sweden. The working languages of the group are Swedish and English. Subject area Maternity care, neonatal care, implementation science, work environment research Subject area description The position is focused on research in obstetrics, perinatal health, implementation science, and work environment research. Key research areas include the needs and experiences of pregnant women, partners, co-parents, and healthcare professionals, implementation processes, and the effects of implementing a new person-centred model of care on maternal, neonatal and perinatal outcomes, experiences of care, and the work environment of healthcare professionals. Duties As a postdoctor, you will play a central role in the planning and execution of the research project. Your responsibilities will include project administration and coordination, serving as project leader for several sub-studies, organizing and leading project meetings, coordinating collaborations between researchers and clinical departments, and contributing to the overall development of the project. The position also involves preparing applications for research funding, drafting and submitting applications to the Swedish Ethical Review Authority, and ensuring that the project is conducted in accordance with relevant regulations and ethical standards. The postdoctor will also be expected to contribute to the supervision of master's students and PhD candidates. Data collection, analysis, and evaluation of implementation processes constitute central parts of the work and include the development and administration of surveys, conducting interviews, managing and analyzing quantitative and qualitative data, and working with national health and quality registers. The position further includes scientific writing, preparation of manuscripts for submission to international peer-reviewed journals, presentation of research findings at national and international conferences, and responsibility for manuscript submission and revision. The project employs both quantitative and qualitative methods and offers excellent opportunities for methodological development. Eligibility To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Applicants must hold a PhD in a relevant field, such as healthcare sciences, medicine, public health, psychology, implementation science, reproductive and perinatal health, or another closely related discipline. Applicants must have documented experience in scientific research and a demonstrated ability to independently plan, conduct and report research, as well as a documented record of scientific publications, including publications in international peer-reviewed journals. Excellent oral and written communication skills in both Swedish and English are required. As the position involves project management and coordination of multiple sub-studies, considerable emphasis will be placed on documented experience in project management, organisation of research projects and collaboration with different professional groups and organisations. Applicants are expected to be able to work independently while also demonstrating strong collaborative skills. The following qualifications are considered meritorious: Experience in implementation science, organisational change, or the development and evaluation of complex interventions within healthcare. Experience in research within obstetrics, neonatology, reproductive or perinatal health, as well as occupational health research or healthcare organization and services research. Experience with mixed-methods study designs, qualitative interview studies, survey research, and register-based research. Experience with data analysis using software such as SPSS, Stata, R, Nvivo or Atlas. Experience preparing ethics applications and research grant applications. Experience supervising master's students, doctoral students, or other research trainees. A documented ability to secure external research funding. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 24 months, 100 % of full-time and is placed at the Institute of Clinical Sciences, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Starting date: as agreed Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Verena Sengpiel, Professor/Chief Physician: verena.sengpiel@obgyn.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Your application should include: A cover letter describing your motivation for applying for the position and how your previous experience and qualifications are relevant to the research project (maximum 2 pages). Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a list of publications. A copy of your doctoral degree certificate (PhD diploma) or equivalent qualification. Contact details for two referees. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-10 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41785": {
      "title": "Lecturer in craft",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Lecturer in craft - 3D digital tools with a specialisation in Crafting Futures For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Lecturer in craft - 3D digital tools with a specialisation in Crafting Futures Reference number PAR 2026/803 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and Academy of Music and Drama. As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is now looking for a lecturer in Lecturer in craft - 3D digital tools with a specialisation in Crafting Futures at HDK-Valand, Campus Steneby. Campus Steneby is a unique environment for education and research in design and crafts within the University of Gothenburg. Through our programs, we aim to develop knowledge of materials and techniques as a foundation for pushing the boundaries of material-based craft and design. With a strong emphasis on artistic exploration and methodologies, we equip students with the tools to apply their skills in new contexts within a rapidly changing world. Read more about HDK-Valand Campus Steneby here. Subject area Lecturer in craft - 3D digital tools with a specialisation in Crafting Futures. Subject area description Crafting Futures is a specialism at Campus Steneby and an environment that fosters interdisciplinary education and research within craft. Our aim is to build a strong and relevant programme with a research environment that can reflect and inform the content of the education. Crafting Futures is a craft-led artistic BFA education where students learn to develop a craft practice that bridges a variety of materials and media. By using the term craft-led, the programme emphasises the way in which craftsmanship leads a knowledge creation that contributes to a broader discourse of contemporary artistic expressions and theories. The programme is designed for students who want to develop artistic work focused on craftsmanship, material knowledge, and method. The courses within the programme are directed towards various artistic and social contexts where the approach is investigative, creative, and reflective. Emphasis is placed on how the choice of media, materials, and methods can position craft as co-creator of resilient ecologies. With focus on the geographical position and rural identity of Campus Steneby, students develop artistic methods that seek to preserve and develop local ecologies, places, and community environments – an educational effort that integrates place as a resource in artistic practice. The students examine the foundational components of an artistic craft practice where recurring and established concepts will be challenged in relation to current norms within the field of Craft. This includes material choices, methods and tools, as well as expected artistic results. Upon completion of the programme, students have developed the ability to work with artistic craft methods that apply tools, processes, methods, and theories in an investigative manner, considering and contributing to the creation of community-oriented resilience. Students are prepared for a wide range of professional activities in the arts, including innovation, cultural heritage, conservation, community development, studio crafts, and public art. Duties We are looking for an adjunct lecturer to join our collegium at the Crafting Futures programme. We expect you to have a craft-led artistic practice with an emphasis on integrating 3D digital tools and working methods within a broad range of materials and techniques, and to be curious about developing new craft skills and knowledge. The language of instruction is English. Duties and responsibilities include: Developing and delivering practical teaching moments that encompass a wide range of craft materials and techniques with a focus on 3D digital practice Work closely with colleagues at Crafting Futures with further integration of 3D digital practice in the curricula Be responsible for examining moments Work closely with technical staff with further development of our 3D digital workshop facilities Participating in the development and evaluation of the craft subject at Campus Steneby Contributing to the research environment at Campus Steneby through artistic development, research projects, or societal collaboration Assignments within the department and faculty can be part of the employment. Responsibilities and work tasks can change over time. Distance teaching may be included in accordance with Gothenburg University’s policies. Development time is included in the employment in accordance with the University of Gothenburg's working time agreement for teachers. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a lecturer, the applicant is required to have demonstrated teaching expertise and to have completed higher education at second-cycle level at least or have the corresponding competence or some other expertise that is of value in view of the subject area content of the post and the duties it will involve. This position requires experience of teaching at university level and very good knowledge of English, both verbally and in writing. Assessment criteria Basis for evaluation of Artistic/Scientific excellence: Sound knowledge and documented experience in craft methods and techniques that span 3D digital art practice and material investigation A well-documented artistic practice involving a range of craft techniques Basis for evaluation if Pedagogical competence: Prior experience of teaching in higher education Prior experience of guiding students through technical introductions In making the appointment, emphasis will also be placed on the applicant having: Documented experience of working with national or international networks, partners, or contexts in craft or a related field Experience of working with 3D digital clay processing The following criteria are meritorious for the position: Documented experience of artistic development, research projects, or societal collaboration Foundational understanding of glass processing Applicants who have not previously completed the higher education pedagogical courses provided by the University (or equivalent) are expected to do so in the first two years of employment. We will conduct an overall assessment and employ the candidate who is considered to possess the best qualities for implementing and developing the aforementioned tasks. Great emphasis is placed on personal suitability, and a judgement will be made as to how the applicants’ skills complement existing staff competences at Campus Steneby. Employment Type of employment: Temporary employment, HF 4 kap 10 § Konstnärlig verksamhet (the Higher Education Ordinance, Chapter 4) 3 years Extent of employment: 20% of fulltime Placement: The position is located in Dals Långed and the duties require you to be present at Campus Steneby on a regular Occupancy: Start date January 18, 2027, or by The employment is limited to 3 years according to the Higher Education Ordinance Chapter 4 §10 Teachers in artistic activities. Possibility of renewal for another 7 years. This form of employment can be given for a maximum of 10 years. If you are an international applicant, read more here: Working hours are based at the Faculty of Fine Arts in Gothenburg with several sessions each working week. Remote work from abroad is not possible as the University of Gothenburg is a government agency. In Sweden, there is a dependency requirement for those applying for a work permit. To find out how this affects you as an international applicant, please read about income requirements for work permits on the Migration Agency's website or contact hr@hdk-valand.gu.se . The official language of communication at the University of Gothenburg is Swedish, but internationalization means that English is often used in parallel. Since knowledge of Swedish is often a prerequisite for collegial cooperation, teachers, researchers and doctoral students who do not have a good knowledge of Swedish should be offered the opportunity to learn the language. This is in order to be able to take on assignments and for promotion to senior lecturer and professor where Swedish is a requirement. Contact information If you have any questions about the recruitment, please contact Carl-Johan Skogh, Head of Unit at Campus Steneby, carl.johan.skogh@hdk.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment procedure, please contact Maria Krång, HR administrator, hr@hdk-valand.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Publications that cannot be attached to the application in digital format should be sent to: HDK-Valand HR Ref: PAR 2026/803 Box 131 405 30 Göteborg and should be received by the application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-11-08 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41778": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Arabic",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Senior Lecturer in Arabic For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Senior Lecturer in Arabic Reference number PAR 2026/671 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg is a public-sector authority, which means that employees enjoy generous employment benefits, including wellness activities during working hours, reimbursement of medical appointments, a minimum annual leave entitlement of 28 days and an occupational pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . As a Senior Lecturer at the University of Gothenburg, your position includes at least 15% research time and opportunities for continuing professional development, with good prospects for further academic advancement. The Department of Languages and Literatures is the largest department within the Faculty of Humanities, encompassing 16 languages, approximately 90 employees, around 1,200 full-time equivalent students and an annual turnover of just over SEK 110 million. Research at the Department addresses the languages and literatures of the world, drawing on broad contextual perspectives and a wide range of theoretical approaches and specific applications. The Department is based within the Faculty of Humanities in modern premises centrally located near Götaplatsen. For further information about the Department, please visit https://www.gu.se/en/languages . Position Senior Lecturer Subject Arabic Subject description Research in Arabic is conducted in linguistics, literary studies and cultural history. Research within the subject also forms part of the Department's interdisciplinary research areas. Arabic is offered both as standalone courses at all levels—first-cycle (undergraduate), second-cycle (Master's) and third-cycle (doctoral) level—and within the Master's Programme in Languages and Intercultural Communication. First-cycle studies comprise four semesters, the first of which is intended for beginners. During the first and second semesters, courses in Modern Standard Arabic and spoken Arabic dialect are offered in parallel. The former focuses on reading and writing proficiency, while the latter focuses on oral proficiency and listening comprehension. During the third semester, students further develop these skills and are introduced to various theoretical perspectives. During the fourth semester, students complete an independent degree project for a Bachelor's degree and deepen their theoretical knowledge on a research-based foundation. Second-cycle studies in Arabic offer both thematically and theoretically oriented courses and independent degree projects leading to a 60-credit or 120-credit Master's degree. These courses are characterised by, and require, a greater degree of independence on the part of students. Examples of second-cycle courses include The Arabic Grammatical Tradition, Classical Poetry and The Quran: Language and Structure. Arabic is also offered as a specialisation within the Master's Programme in Languages and Intercultural Communication (120 higher education credits). The subject offers both on-campus and online courses. At advanced stages of study, specialised courses in linguistics and literary studies are offered, as well as courses involving degree projects. The subject of Arabic also offers courses aimed at mother tongue teachers. Duties Teaching duties include teaching Arabic linguistics, language proficiency and literature at first- and second-cycle levels. Teaching is conducted both on campus and online, during the day and in the evenings. Teaching duties may also include professionally oriented courses in Arabic for mother tongue teachers. Teaching responsibilities also include preparing examinations, assessment and examination, supervision, seminars, course coordination, course administration and course development, among other duties.Teaching within the Department's interdisciplinary courses and programmes may also be required, as well as administrative assignments. The position also includes independent research. The successful candidate is expected to publish research findings, actively seek research funding from leading Swedish and international research funding bodies, and contribute to the research environment within the subject and the Department, for example by participating in research seminars. The successful candidate is expected to conduct research within the subject of Arabic, as must be demonstrated in the research plan. Most duties are carried out at the Department of Languages and Literatures at the University of Gothenburg. The successful candidate is expected to be present and participate in the Department's day-to-day activities on site. Teaching within the subject is organised in a teaching team, and the successful candidate is expected to contribute constructively and collegially to both the subject and the Department as a whole. The position may also involve other duties, such as administrative tasks and development activities. The successful candidate is also expected to participate actively in the Department's development activities and work related to the health and safety of the work environment, including staff meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. Eligibility Eligibility for teaching appointments is regulated by Chapter 4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance (Högskoleförordningen) and the University of Gothenburg's Appointments Procedure. To be eligible for appointment as Senior Lecturer in Arabic, applicants must have demonstrated teaching expertise and hold a doctoral degree in Arabic or possess equivalent scholarly competence. Good proficiency in, and the ability to teach in, Swedish (or another Scandinavian language), English and Arabic are required from the start of employment. As a Senior Lecturer at the University of Gothenburg, you may apply for promotion to Docent (Reader/Associate Professor) and Professor. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for teaching appointments are regulated by Chapter 4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg's Appointments Procedure. In assessing applicants' qualifications, equal attention will be given to teaching expertise and scholarly expertise. In the selection process, documented qualifications in the following areas will be considered in the first instance: Scholarly qualifications in Arabic, assessed with particular regard to originality and international recognition and engagement. Teaching expertise in on-campus and online teaching of Arabic. Experience of supervision and examination in Arabic. A good ability to teach Arabic in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language), English and Arabic. In the second instance, weight will be given to the following qualifications: Experience of managing and administering teaching activities (e.g. course coordination). Experience of applying for and obtaining external research funding. Established connections and active participation in national and international networks. Collaboration with the wider community. Training in teaching and learning in higher education. Additional qualifications considered advantageous include experience of teaching Arabic dialects, native or near-native proficiency in Arabic, and experience of teaching or conducting research in Arabic language didactics. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and development potential, is considered suitable and best qualified to perform the duties associated with the appointment. The overall assessment will therefore also take into account suitability for the position, particularly professional maturity, collaborative and communication skills, a sense of responsibility, and the ability to contribute constructively to the Department's activities and a positive work environment. Employment Number of positions: 1 Type of employment: Permanent Extent: 100% (full-time) Location: the Department of Languages and Literatures, the Faculty of Humanities, Gothenburg. Start date: August 2027 or as agreed. A probationary period may apply. Application Applications must be submitted through the University of Gothenburg's recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. For instructions on applying for teaching positions, applicants are referred to the Faculty of Humanities' guidelines: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position . The following documents must be attached to the application: Cover letter. Research plan. List of degrees and previous employment. Description of scholarly and teaching activities, including reflections on research and teaching. Qualifications Portfolio I: Scholarly qualifications. Qualifications Portfolio II: Teaching qualifications. Qualifications Portfolio III: Qualifications relating to collaboration with the wider community. Qualifications Portfolio IV: Management and administrative duties. Selected publications (maximum 10), together with a separate list of these publications. Doctoral degree certificate. Doctoral thesis. References: names and telephone numbers of at least two referees. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their applications are complete by the application deadline. Incomplete applications will be assessed on the basis of the documents submitted. In addition to external expert review, an interview and a trial lecture, reference checks may also form part of the assessment. The trial lecture and interview will be conducted in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language), with elements of Arabic and English. We welcome applications written in English, as they may be reviewed by international experts whose working language is English. Application deadline: 2026-11-16. Additional information about the position If applicants do not possess the qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education required by the University of Gothenburg (PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103) at the time of application, these qualifications must be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of commencing employment (https://www.pil.gu.se/en) Contact information For further information about the position, please contact the Head of Department at prefekt@sprak.gu.se or Director of Studies Magnus P. Ängsal at magnus.pettersson.angsal@sprak.gu.se . For questions about the recruitment procedure or scholarly publications, written works and similar materials that cannot be attached electronically to the application, please contact personalhandlaggare@sprak.gu.se . Trade union organisations Information about trade union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here . The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41769": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in French",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Senior Lecturer in French For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Senior Lecturer in French Reference number PAR 2026/702 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg is a public-sector authority, which means that employees enjoy generous employment benefits, including wellness activities during working hours, reimbursement of medical appointments, a minimum annual leave entitlement of 28 days and an occupational pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here As a Senior Lecturer at the University of Gothenburg, your position includes at least 15% research time and opportunities for continuing professional development, with good prospects for further academic advancement. The Department of Languages and Literatures is the largest department within the Faculty of Humanities, encompassing 16 languages, approximately 90 employees, around 1,200 full-time equivalent students and an annual turnover of just over SEK 110 million. Research at the Department addresses the languages and literatures of the world, drawing on broad contextual perspectives and a wide range of theoretical approaches and specific applications. The Department is based within the Faculty of Humanities in modern premises centrally located near Götaplatsen. For further information about the Department, please visit https://www.gu.se/en/languages Position Senior Lecturer Subject French Subject description The subject of French encompasses both linguistics and literary studies and is authorised to award degrees at all levels, including doctoral level. In addition to standalone courses, French is included in various study programmes offered by the Department, such as the Master's Programme in Languages and Intercultural Communication, the Teacher Education Programme for Secondary and Upper Secondary School (Ämneslärarprogrammet) and the Nordic Master's Programme for Language Teachers. The subject offers both on-campus and online courses. Research in French is primarily conducted in linguistics (including contrastive studies, corpus linguistics, discourse analysis, stylistics, sociolinguistics, language acquisition and language education) and literary studies (including early modern French literature, contemporary French literature, thematic studies and comparative literature). Researchers also participate in the Department's shared research areas. Duties The successful candidate is expected to conduct research in French linguistics, as must be demonstrated in the research plan. The successful candidate is also expected to keep abreast of developments in their field, actively apply for external research funding and contribute to the Department's educational and scholarly development. Applicants must therefore describe, as part of their application, how their research can promote and contribute to the Department's research areas, as well as to research within the subject of French. Teaching at all levels may be included in the position, both on campus and online, primarily during the day but also in the evenings. The successful candidate must be able to teach across a broad range of areas within the subject, including oral and written language proficiency, French literary studies and linguistics (for example, courses in literature, grammar and translation), and culture and society. Teaching may take place both within standalone courses and within Department-wide courses and programmes. The position also includes teaching within the Teacher Education Programme for Secondary and Upper Secondary School, including courses focusing on subject didactics, as well as visits and seminars associated with teaching practice placements (verksamhetsförlagd utbildning, VFU). As the subject offers contrastive courses, a substantial proportion of teaching is conducted in Swedish. Teaching within the Department's other interdisciplinary courses and programmes may also be required, in both Swedish and English. Teaching duties also include supervision, seminars, course coordination, course administration and course development, among other responsibilities. The position may also involve other duties, such as administrative tasks and development activities. Most duties must be performed on site at the University of Gothenburg, and the successful candidate is expected to participate actively in the Department's day-to-day activities. Teaching within the subject is organised in teaching teams, and the successful candidate is expected to contribute constructively and collegially to both the subject's activities and the Department as a whole. The position also involves participation in the Department's development activities and work related to the health and safety of the work environment, including attendance at staff meetings, internal meetings and conferences. Eligibility Provisions concerning eligibility for teaching positions are set out in Chapter 4, Sections 3–4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance (högskoleförordningen) and the University of Gothenburg's own Appointments Procedure. To be eligible for appointment as Senior Lecturer, applicants must hold a doctoral degree in French or possess equivalent scholarly competence and must be able to demonstrate teaching expertise. A good ability to teach in French as well as in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language) is required from the start of employment. As a Senior Lecturer at the University of Gothenburg, you may apply for promotion to Docent (Reader/Associate Professor) and Professor. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for teaching appointments are set out in Chapter 4, Sections 3–4 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg's Appointments Procedure. In assessing applicants' qualifications, equal attention will be given to teaching expertise and scholarly expertise. In the selection process, documented qualifications in the following areas will be considered in the first instance: Scholarly qualifications in French linguistics, assessed in terms of quality, originality and relevance to the subject. Teaching expertise in on-campus and online teaching of French. A good ability to teach French in French and in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language). Experience of supervision and assessment in French. Experience of teaching within the Teacher Education Programme for Secondary and Upper Secondary School. In the second instance, weight will be given to the following documented qualifications: Management and administration of teaching activities (e.g. course coordination). Established connections and active participation in national and international networks. Collaboration with the wider community. Training in teaching and learning in higher education. Experience of and ability to teach in English will also be considered an advantage. Weight will be given to how well applicants deliver a trial lecture in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language) on a specified topic, as well as to their performance in an interview conducted in Swedish, with elements of French and English, during which applicants will have the opportunity to discuss their research and teaching experience orally. Weight will also be given to the cover letter, in which applicants must describe their career development to date, their anticipated future career development and how this relates both to the Department's research areas and to research conducted within the subject. The position also requires a sense of responsibility, good interpersonal and collaborative skills, and flexibility, preferably supported by relevant documentation. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment, is considered the most suitable and best qualified to perform the duties associated with the appointment. Employment Number of positions: 1 Type of employment: Permanent Extent: 100% (full-time) Location: the Department of Languages and Literatures, the Faculty of Humanities, Gothenburg Start date: August 2027 or as agreed A probationary period may apply. Additional information about the position If applicants do not possess the qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education required by the University of Gothenburg (PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103) at the time of application, these qualifications must be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of commencing employment ( https://www.pil.gu.se/en ) Applications must be submitted through the University of Gothenburg's recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. For instructions on applying for teaching positions, applicants are referred to the Faculty of Humanities' guidelines: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position For further information about the position, please contact Head of Department Petra Platen at prefektspl@sprak.gu.se or Director of Studies Jacob Carlson at jacob.carlson@sprak.gu.se . For scholarly publications, written works and similar materials that cannot be attached electronically to the application, as well as questions about the recruitment procedure, please contact personalhandlaggare@sprak.gu.se . The following documents must be attached to the application: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae (CV), including a list of degrees and previous employment. Description of scholarly and teaching activities, including reflections on research and teaching. Qualifications Portfolio I: Scholarly qualifications. Qualifications Portfolio II: Teaching qualifications. Qualifications Portfolio III: Qualifications relating to collaboration with the wider community. Qualifications Portfolio IV: Management and administrative qualifications. Selected publications (maximum 10), together with a separate list of these publications. Doctoral degree certificate. Doctoral thesis. Research plan. References: names and telephone numbers of two referees. Where applicable, documents such as certificates demonstrating good collaborative skills and flexibility. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their applications are complete by the application deadline. Incomplete applications will be assessed on the basis of the documents submitted. In addition to external expert review, an interview and a trial lecture, reference checks may also form part of the assessment. We welcome applications written in English, as they may be reviewed by international experts whose working language is English. Application deadline: 2026-11-09. Trade union organisations Information about trade union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here . The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41842": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences - Person-Centred care in ICU",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences - Person-Centred care in ICU For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences - Person-Centred care in ICU Reference number PAR 2026/785 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Health Care Sciences Project title: The Impact of the ICU Care Environment on the Provision of Person-Centred Care The Department of Diagnostics, Acute and Critical Care is located at the Institute of Health and Care Sciences at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Health Care Sciences comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Intensive care units are complex, high-tech environments where critically ill patients receive care. High demands, advanced medical technology, and heavy workloads may affect both the experiences of patients and their families and healthcare professionals’ ability to provide safe and person-centered care. This project aims to investigate factors within the ICU care environment that facilitate or constrain the provision of person-centered care. The project forms part of the larger SoLiDe ICU research project ( https://www.gu.se/forskning/solide-icu) , which investigates the impact of the physical environment on staff health. The project aims to develop and validate an instrument to assess how the ICU care environment influences healthcare professionals’ ability to provide person-centered care using quantitative and qualitative research methods. As a doctoral student in this project, you will have the opportunity to work in an international research environment and contribute to the development of new knowledge to improve the ICU care environment. Experience in intensive care is considered an advantage, as is documented experience of qualitative and quantitative research methods, including interviewing and statistical analysis, and experience of working with digital research systems. The ability to independently structure and manage project tasks, as well as to handle complex data flows in clinical research, is considered an asset. The project involves interviews and data collection in Swedish in contact with staff in the clinical environment. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Health Care Sciences  Department of Diagnostics, Acute and Critical Care. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Health Care Sciences require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Sepideh Olausson, Institute of Health Care Sciences, e-mail: sepideh.olausson@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-11-18 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/job?site=7&lang=UK&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&job_id=41546": {
      "title": "Doctoral student position in Social Work",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student position in Social Work For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student position in Social Work Reference number PAR 2026/724 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Social Work is part of the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg and is one of the largest and most well-established environments for research and education in social work in the Nordic region. The Department has more than 130 employees and provides education at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle levels. Its range of courses and study programmes includes the Bachelor of Science Programme in Social Work, Master’s programmes in Social Work and Human Rights, professional development programmes and courses, and doctoral studies. As a research discipline, social work focuses on understanding people in vulnerable social situations, societal interventions, social policy measures and social processes. Research is conducted within several profile areas and is characterised by a strong commitment to society. The Department also has extensive international contacts and collaborations through research networks and partnerships with universities in different parts of the world. More information about the Department is available at: https://www.gu.se/en/socwork Duties During the programme, the doctoral student is expected to complete both a thesis component and a course component, comprising compulsory and elective doctoral courses in accordance with the general syllabus and an individual study plan. During the period of doctoral studies, the thesis work will be presented and discussed at a minimum of three seminars. The programme concludes with the doctoral student defending their doctoral thesis at a public dissertation defence. Doctoral studies comprise four years of full-time study and lead to a doctoral degree. As part of your doctoral studentship, you may undertake departmental duties corresponding to a maximum of 20% of full-time employment, distributed over the entire period of study. If so, the period of study will be extended accordingly. Departmental duties normally consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administrative duties. Eligibility To be eligible for admission to doctoral studies, applicants must meet the general entry requirements and, where applicable, the specific entry requirements set out in the general syllabus for the third-cycle subject area. An applicant meets the general entry requirements for doctoral studies if they have: been awarded a second-cycle qualification; or completed courses corresponding to at least 240 higher education credits, of which at least 60 credits are at second-cycle level; or acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. For admission to doctoral studies in social work, applicants must also meet the following requirements: a Degree of Bachelor of Science in Social Work, 210 higher education credits; and at least 60 higher education credits in social work at second-cycle level, including an independent project of at least 15 higher education credits; or equivalent knowledge acquired in some other way corresponding to both of the requirements above. Assessment criteria Selection among applicants who meet the general and specific entry requirements is based on their ability to benefit from doctoral studies. The assessment therefore aims to evaluate the applicant’s capacity to undertake doctoral studies and to develop their ability to formulate a research problem, independently carry out a research project and present research findings. As the programme is primarily conducted in Swedish, a good command of Swedish is required. A good command of English is also required in order to benefit from the programme. The applicant’s ability to benefit from doctoral studies will be assessed on the basis of: the quality of the research proposal submitted with the application; the feasibility of the proposed project; the project’s relevance to the field of social work; the quality of previous theses and other written work; and academic and research qualifications. The Department’s ability to offer academic supervision based on the applicant’s preliminary research focus, as well as to provide a good research environment for the planned doctoral thesis, is also taken into consideration. Previous professional experience in social work is considered a merit. Applications will be assessed and prioritised by an assessment group at the Department of Social Work. Interviews form part of the selection process. The final decision on admission is made by the Head of Department and cannot be appealed. Applicants who are employed by another employer and whose doctoral studies are financed by that employer may also be admitted as externally employed doctoral students. These students are known as collaborative doctoral students (SWE: Samverkansdoktorand ) and are subject to the same admissions criteria as other applicants. A separate agreement will be drawn up between the employer and the Department. Employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. The initial period of employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Please note, doctoral studies at the Department of Social Work require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the Department of Social Work has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Additional information For more information about the programme, please see the General Syllabus for Doctoral Studies in Social Work, available at: https://www.gu.se/en/socwork/doctoral-studies/about-doctoral-studies The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. The position is based in Gothenburg and requires the admitted doctoral student to reside in and be registered in Sweden. Contact information Sofia Vales, Doctoral Studies Administrator sofia.vales@socwork.gu.se Andreas Eriksson, Deputy Head of Department responsible for doctoral studies andreas.eriksson@socwork.gu.se Moa Irekvist, doctoral student moa.irekvist@socwork.gu.se Department website: https://www.gu.se/en/socwork Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position Application Applications for admission to doctoral studies must be submitted via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. As an applicant, you are responsible for ensuring that your application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it reaches the University no later than the application deadline. For an application to be considered complete, it must include: An introductory letter of no more than 500 words. The letter must include: (a) a brief presentation of yourself, (b) a brief presentation of your qualifications and experience, and (c) a brief presentation of the proposed research project. A CV, including certified documentation of qualifications and a list of publications. Theses, degree projects and other relevant written work. A research proposal of no more than 1700 words, including references. In the research proposal, the applicant must identify a research area and relevant research questions, explain their relevance, and discuss theories, methods and materials that may be appropriate. The proposal should indicate possible directions for the applicant’s doctoral thesis and will serve as a basis for assessing the applicant’s ability to identify relevant and interesting research questions and to make appropriate methodological considerations in relation to the proposed project. Certified copies of degree certificates and documentation demonstrating that the specific entry requirements have been met. A description of, and supporting documentation for, any other relevant qualifications, such as participation in research, evaluation activities or similar development work. Documents, certificates, publications and similar materials that are not available in electronic form must be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Social Work Attn: Sofia Vales Box 720 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please state registration number PAR 2026/724 . Applications must be received by 28 January 2027. Information for International Applicants - Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region.To give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    }
  },
